(window.__D=window.__D||{})["data/ch10.json"]={"n": 10, "title": "The Pontic Greek Genocide, 1916–1923", "events": [{"id": "H7-001", "date": "1914-08", "date_text": "August 1914 onwards", "precision": "month", "title": "Ottoman mobilisation: Christian men drafted into labour battalions", "summary": "With the general mobilisation of August 1914, non-Muslim men, Pontic Greeks among them, were drafted largely into unarmed labour battalions (amele taburları) that built and maintained roads, railways and tunnels and carried supplies to the fronts. Rations, clothing and shelter were poor and mortality was high: a postwar Greek report gives 23 survivors of some 3,000 labour conscripts from Ayvalık, another report an 80 percent death toll among labourers at İslahiye, and Col. Rawlinson, a British officer held hostage in the eastern vilayets, estimated that 'the life of a Greek in a labor gang is generally about two months' (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 387). By early 1916 most adult Greek men of the coast were in the battalions or in hiding, so the deportations of 1916-17 fell mainly on women, children and the old. At Samsun, a Greek report of 19 May 1916 says that deserters who surrendered were held by the hundred for six weeks without bread, fed only by the community, and then sent in handcuffs to Sivas, fifteen days' march (Brown & Ion 1918, pp. 12-13). A decision of the recruiting office of 21 October 1915 ordered the families of deserters who did not surrender sent to the interior (p. 10).", "sources_primary": ["[British officer held hostage in the eastern vilayets] Rendel, 'Memorandum by Mr. Rendel on Conversation with Col. Rawlinson on 23rd May 1922', 24 May 1922, FO 371/7878, quoted by Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 387 n. 28", "[Greek consul-general at Konya, dispatch of 7 March 1917] Greek Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Persecutions of the Greek Population in Turkey (1918), p. 11, via Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 387 n. 26", "German and Austrian consular reports; Greek Legation reports 1915–17", "British intelligence estimate that a Greek labourer's life expectancy was about two months (cited by Basso 2016)", "[Greek consul at Konya, report of 7 March 1917] Brown & Ion 1918, pp. 9-10 (Morris & Ze'evi cite the same report from the Greek Foreign Ministry's own 1918 edition, p. 11)", "[Greek consular report] Samsun (Amissos), 19 May 1916, in Brown & Ion 1918, pp. 12-13: deserters who gave themselves up held for six weeks without bread, then sent in handcuffs to Sivas"], "sources_modern": ["Zürcher, 'Ottoman labour battalions in World War I', in Kieser & Schaller (eds.), Der Völkermord an den Armeniern und die Shoah (Zurich: Chronos, 2002), pp. 187-196", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 387", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (New York and Athens: Caratzas, 2011), pp. 92-93 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 27)", "Basso, Genocide Studies and Prevention 10.1 (2016), pp. 5-29", "Minasidis, New Perspectives on Turkey 70 (2024), 89–106", "Basso 2016", "Akçam, The Young Turks' Crime against Humanity (Princeton UP, 2012), pp. 95-96 (labour battalions as one of the parallels between the Greek expulsions of 1914 and the Armenian deportations, with Morgenthau's account of Greek labour soldiers dying in thousands on the Caucasus roads) and p. 111 (men of the Samsun villages taken into labour battalions in January 1917) [Turkish-born scholar abroad]"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "That the battalions existed and were deadly is not in dispute. Basso's death rates 'sometimes over 90%' (2016, p. 22) are the highest for particular battalions, not an overall rate, and no total of labour-battalion deaths for the Pontus has been established. The age bands given for the call-up vary (18–48, 20–45).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Col. Rawlinson via Rendel (FO 371/7878) and Morris & Ze'evi; Zürcher 2002; Basso 2016", "Greek sources: Greek consular and postwar reports (via Morris & Ze'evi, Hofmann)", "Neutral sources: German and Austrian consular reports; British intelligence estimate (via Basso 2016); Akçam 2012", "Greek sources: Greek Legation reports 1915-17; Minasidis 2024"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Erzurum", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.266, "role": "Caucasus front: road and supply work (typical destination; Pontic presence not established)"}, {"name": "Sivas (Sebasteia)", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.016, "role": "Sivas-Erzurum road works (typical; not tied to Pontic units)"}, {"name": "İslahiye", "lat": 37.026, "lon": 36.631, "role": "Amanus railway works; '80 percent' death toll among conscripted labourers (Morris & Ze'evi p. 387, after Hofmann 2011, pp. 92-93)"}, {"name": "Gümüşhane (Argyroupolis)", "lat": 40.46, "lon": 39.481, "role": "road works to the front"}, {"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "call-up centre"}]}, {"id": "H7-003", "date": "1915-12", "date_text": "December 1915", "precision": "month", "title": "Greeks of the Ünye district expelled", "summary": "Morris and Ze'evi (p. 389), in a paragraph on the deportations of 1915, write that the villagers of the Ünye (Ounia) area were deported 'in December', and that according to the Greek Patriarchate Turkish troops murdered batches of Greek men and raped women. Their source is a memorandum by Jesse B. Jackson, the former US consul at Aleppo, dated 4 March 1918. The Patriarchate's compilation dates the evacuation of seven villages of the Ünye region (Tzaguere, Touz-messe, Tchoktze, Tzillar, Kiretch-tepe, Liman-dere, Tere-Bashi) to December 1915, and the deportation of the town's community to July 1917 and after, to Kastamonu, Zile and elsewhere; a letter from Ünye of 10 March 1917 describes the men taken off in small groups and killed by Kurds under a Circassian, Ismail Bey (Patriarchate 1919, p. 126).", "sources_primary": ["[US official, retrospective] Jackson (Washington DC), untitled memorandum, 4 March 1918, USNA RG 59, 867.4016, Roll 46 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 49)", "Greek Patriarchate report, via Jackson (Washington), untitled memorandum, 4 March 1918, USNA RG 59, 867.4016, Roll 46 (as cited by Morris & Ze'evi, n. 49)", "[Patriarchate compilation] Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (1919), p. 126"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 389", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 389"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Morris & Ze'evi (p. 389) place the Ünye deportation among those of 1915; their source is a Greek Patriarchate report forwarded in a US memorandum of 1918, so the killings rest on a Greek source. The date, December 1915, is given both by that memorandum and by the Patriarchate's compilation. Burnings in Ünye villages in October 1916, given in the Villages section, would be a second wave.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Jackson memorandum (via Morris & Ze'evi)", "Greek sources: the Patriarchate's account as quoted", "Greek sources: Greek Patriarchate report", "Neutral sources: Morris & Ze'evi 2019, p. 389; US memorandum of 4 March 1918 (transmitter)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Ünye (Oinoe)", "lat": 41.13, "lon": 37.288, "role": "deported from"}]}, {"id": "H7-063", "date": "1916", "date_end": "1922-05-24", "date_text": "1916-1922", "precision": "year", "title": "Greek armed bands in the Pontic mountains: what non-Turkish sources say", "summary": "Non-Turkish sources confirm that Greek armed bands existed and at times attacked Muslims, but give no casualty totals. Morris & Ze'evi write that in 1914-18 bands of draft evaders 'occasionally attacked Turkish villagers and gendarmes' (p. 383; no source cited). In early September 1921 the USS McFarland's war diary recorded a Greek band of nearly a hundred attacking a Turkish village eight miles from Samsun, killing some villagers and burning houses, and a similar raid seems to have followed in early October (Shenk 2017, p. 201; H6-079). In May 1922 Col. Rawlinson told the Foreign Office that 20,000-30,000 armed Greeks were banded together in the mountains (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 415). Greek and American sources describe the bands of Mount Nebyan as the refuge of those fleeing the burned villages (B1-007), and a Turkish deputy said in the Assembly that Nureddin's methods had driven Greeks to the mountains (H7-031). Western observers in 1921 'uniformly asserted that there was no Pontine rebellion, either underway or in preparation' (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 406). Agtzidis (1992, p. 167), a Greek historian, puts the bands at more than 18,000 armed men at the peak, about 1920 (beside Rawlinson's 20,000–30,000), and names 'Russian aid' among their sources of arms: this partly corroborates the Turkish claim of Russian arms for the bands. Agtzidis (2005, pp. 165, 184) writes of an 'uprising' after the armistice and of Muslim–Christian clashes with 'hundreds of victims', and says that Germanos and Chrysanthos 'encouraged the insurgents'.", "sources_primary": ["[US Navy] USS McFarland war diaries, 31 Aug - 8 Sept 1921, NA RG 45 box 832; USS Williamson to Bristol, 9 Oct 1921 (via Shenk 2017)", "[British] Rendel, memorandum of conversation with Col. Rawlinson, 24 May 1922, FO 371/7878 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 244)", "[US missionary] White to Robbins, 18 Nov 1921 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 170)", "[Greek Metropolitan, letter of 27 Feb 1917] Patriarchate 1919, p. 121 (about 300 deserters armed from a Russian torpedo boat)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), pp. 383, 406, 415", "Shenk, 'Ethnic Cleansing, American Women, and the Admiral', in Shirinian (ed.), Genocide in the Ottoman Empire: Armenians, Assyrians, and Greeks, 1913-1923 (Berghahn, 2017), p. 201", "See also B1-007, H6-079", "Agtzidis 1992, p. 167 (bands at first village self-defence; more than 18,000 armed at the peak, about 1920; calls for a 'general revolution' from 1916; arms from battles, Russian Greek networks and 'Russian aid') [Greek]", "Agtzidis 2005, pp. 165, 184 (an 'uprising' after the armistice; Muslim–Christian clashes with 'hundreds of victims'; Germanos and Chrysanthos 'encouraged the insurgents') [Greek]"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Rawlinson's 20,000–30,000 is one officer's estimate, and Agtzidis' 18,000 is set beside it, not averaged with it. A Greek primary source also speaks of armed deserters: the Metropolitan of Amaseia wrote on 27 February 1917 that the pretext for the persecution was 'about 300 deserters' who had gone to Trebizond for arms brought by a Russian torpedo boat, and that the government had the right to punish them, but not the women, children and villages (Patriarchate 1919, p. 121). The Turkish accounts of particular raids, and the Muslim casualty figures, which no non-Turkish source confirms, are given under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H7-004, H6-013, H7-T08, H7-T10).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: US naval diaries; British FO (Rawlinson); Morris & Ze'evi", "Greek sources: Agtzidis 1992, 2005", "Neutral sources: as before"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Nebyan Dağı", "lat": 41.35, "lon": 36.0, "role": "band refuge (approximate)"}, {"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "band raid on a village about 8 miles away, Sept 1921 (plotted at Samsun)"}]}, {"id": "H7-054", "date": "1916-01-31", "date_end": "1916-10-13", "date_text": "31 January - October 1916", "precision": "month", "title": "Talat on the Greeks, as reported by the Central Powers' diplomats (1916)", "summary": "On 31 January 1916, according to an Austrian report cited by Fotiadis, the interior minister Talat told an Austrian agent that 'he sees the increased necessity for Turkey to get rid of Greeks, as she did before with Armenians' (Kyriakidis 2026, p. 50). In October 1916 Talat confirmed to the German ambassador Radowitz that a wholesale deportation of all Greeks from the Ottoman coasts had been planned earlier in the year, but that Enver had stopped it so as not to provoke Greece, then still neutral; on 3 October Radowitz reported that the 'cleansing' had been partly accomplished by expelling 'the Greeks from certain districts of Asia Minor and Rumelia' (Hofmann 2011, p. 59).", "people": ["Mehmed Talat Pasha", "Enver Pasha", "Hans von Radowitz"], "sources_primary": ["[Austrian report, 31 Jan 1916] via Fotiadis, Η Γενοκτονία των Ελλήνων του Πόντου (2004), p. 178, as cited by Kyriakidis 2026, p. 50 n. 47", "[German diplomatic] Radowitz to Berlin, 3 Oct 1916, PA-AA, IA Türkei 183, Armenien Bd. 44, No. 7155, R 14093; Radowitz, 5 and 13 Oct 1916 (via Hofmann 2011, p. 59 nn. 66-67)"], "sources_modern": ["Kyriakidis (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias Press, 2026), p. 50", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (New York and Athens: Caratzas, 2011), p. 59"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Both are diplomats' reports of conversations, reaching us through Greek-edited compilations (Fotiadis). The Austrian agent is not named.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Austrian and German diplomatic reports", "Greek sources: Fotiadis (edition); Kyriakidis; Hofmann (historian)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "conversations reported"}]}, {"id": "H7-007", "date": "1916-06", "date_end": "1916-07-16", "date_text": "June-July 1916 (Kuckhoff's telegram of 15 or 16 July 1916)", "precision": "month", "title": "Deportation of the Greeks of İnebolu, Sinope and the Kastamonu coast; Kuckhoff's telegram", "summary": "The Greek population of İnebolu and its villages (Cide, Patheri, Atsidono, Karaca, Askordassi) was deported in June 1916 (Morris & Ze'evi, pp. 389-390, after the Patriarchate). In mid-July 1916 the German vice-consul at Samsun, Kuckhoff, telegraphed that, 'according to trustworthy sources', the whole Greek population of Sinope and of the coast of the Kastamonu province had been deported; that 'in Turkish the terms deportation and destruction have the same meaning, for in most cases those who are not killed fall victim to diseases or starvation'; and that the vali of Kastamonu was using 'the flight of Greek conscripts over the sea and the prevention of espionage as a pretext to annihilate the entire people' (Hofmann 2011, p. 57). Hofmann's German (aga-online) reads 'Ausweisung und Ausrottung sind türkisch gleiche Begriffe'. Hofmann adds that the Greeks of Sinope were given four hours' notice, and quotes a New York Times cable of 21 August 1916 on caravans sent to 'concentration camps in the interior'. The Patriarchate's compilation adds that at the end of July 1916 Sinope, Gerze and its villages as far as Alaçam, Ayancık and Ayandon were evacuated; after eight days' march the people reached Kastamonu, where the vali Atif Bey sent them on to Taşköprü, Boyabat and Safranbolu; and that a fire at Sinope destroyed 400 Greek houses and shops (Patriarchate 1919, p. 119). For Cide and İnebolu it confirms June 1916 (p. 127).", "people": ["Kuckhoff (German vice-consul, Samsun)", "M. Kuckhoff"], "sources_primary": ["[German diplomatic] Telegram No. 129 from the German consulate at Samsun, 15 (or 16) July 1916, as given by Hofmann 2011, p. 57 n. 55, after Fotiadis, Der Genozid an den Pontosgriechen, vol. 12 (2003), p. 114 (PA-AA file signature not given)", "[Press] New York Times, 21 Aug 1916, p. 2, 'Turks deporting Greeks' (via Hofmann 2011, p. 57 n. 56)", "Kuckhoff to the German embassy, Samsun, 15 or 16 July 1916 (Politisches Archiv des Auswärtigen Amts)", "[Patriarchate compilation] Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (1919), pp. 119, 127"], "sources_modern": ["Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (New York and Athens: Caratzas, 2011), p. 57 (16 July in the text; English)", "Shirinian, 'The Background to the Late Ottoman Genocides', in Shirinian (ed.), Genocide in the Ottoman Empire: Armenians, Assyrians, and Greeks, 1913-1923 (Berghahn, 2017), p. 52 and n. 166 (16 July 1916; English after Fotiadis, vol. 13 (2004), p. 19)", "Kyriakidis (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias Press, 2026), p. 62 n. 92 (16 July 1916; English after Fotiadis 2004, p. 164)", "Hofmann, Kurzer Abriss zum osmanischen Genozid an den Griechen des Pontos (aga-online.org), n. 1 (German text)", "Travis, 'On the Centenary of the Greek Genocide', AHIF Policy Journal 8 (Spring 2017), p. 2 and n. 18 ('summer 1916'; ' [e]xile and extermination in Turkish are the same', after Thea Halo, Not Even My Name (2000), p. 127)", "Kévorkian & Astourian (eds.), Collective and State Violence in Turkey (Berghahn, 2020), p. 201 (16 July)", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), pp. 389-390 (İnebolu, June; Morris & Ze'evi do not mention Kuckhoff)", "Hofmann, Kurzer Abriss zum osmanischen Genozid an den Griechen des Pontos (aga-online.org), n. 1, after Fotiadis, Der Genozid an den Pontosgriechen, vol. 12 (2003), p. 114 (15 July; German text)", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), p. 57 (English text; Kuckhoff, 16 July 1916, in the text, but n. 55 gives 'Telegramm Nr. 129, 15 July 1916 by Heinrich Bergfeld, Consulate Samsun', after Fotiadis, p. 114)", "Kyriakides (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias 2026; Greek ed. 2015), p. 62 n. 92 (Kuckhoff, 16 July 1916, after Fotiadis, Η Γενοκτονία των Ελλήνων του Πόντου, p. 164)", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 389–390 (İnebolu, Cide, Patheri, Atsidono, Karaca, Askordassi deported in June; after the Greek Patriarchate, p. 127)", "Kévorkian & Astourian (eds.) 2020, p. 201 (16 July)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The date and the sender: the published texts give 16 July and Kuckhoff (Hofmann 2011, text; Shirinian; Kyriakidis, p. 62; Kévorkian & Astourian), but Hofmann's footnote, after Fotiadis, reads 'Telegramm Nr. 129, 15 July 1916 by Heinrich Bergfeld, Consulate Samsun' (n. 55), and so does his web essay; elsewhere Hofmann calls Kuckhoff the German consul at Samsun in February 1916 (p. 97) and Bergfeld the consul at Trebizond in August 1915 (p. 56). The addressee is given as the embassy, as 'Berlin' (Shirinian, who also calls the sender 'the German ambassador at Amisos', an error), or as 'the German Ministry of the Interior' (Kyriakidis, an error). The English versions differ: 'deportation and destruction have the same meaning' (Hofmann 2011), 'Exile and annihilation have the same meaning' (Shirinian 2017) and 'Deportation and extermination mean the same' (Kyriakidis 2026); the German is given above. Travis (2017, p. 2, n. 18) gives only 'summer 1916', after Thea Halo (2000, p. 127).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: German consular telegram (via Hofmann, Shirinian, Kyriakidis); New York Times; Morris & Ze'evi; Hofmann; Travis 2017", "Greek sources: Fotiadis (edition); the Patriarchate (İnebolu)", "Armenian sources: Kévorkian & Astourian (eds.) 2020", "Neutral sources: Kuckhoff (German consular report); Hofmann; Travis 2017", "Greek sources: Fotiadis, Der Genozid an den Pontosgriechen, vol. 12 (edition)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "İnebolu", "lat": 41.975, "lon": 33.76, "role": "deported from, June 1916"}, {"name": "Cide", "lat": 41.89, "lon": 33.005, "role": "deported from"}, {"name": "Sinop (Sinope)", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.151, "role": "deported from, July 1916"}, {"name": "Kastamonu", "lat": 41.376, "lon": 33.776, "role": "inland destination area (probable)"}, {"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "seat of the German consulate reporting"}]}, {"id": "H7-055", "date": "1916-07-03", "date_text": "3 July 1916 (20 Haziran 1332)", "precision": "day", "title": "Talat orders Greeks of Görele, Vakfıkebir and Şarlı sent to Sivas", "summary": "A cipher telegram from the interior minister Talat to the vali of Sivas, Muammer Bey, dated 20 Haziran 1332 (3 July 1916), states that, since some Greeks of the Görele and Vakfıkebir kazas and the Şarlı nahiye of Trabzon vilayet had been found to engage in espionage, the army had asked for these Greeks, men and women, to be sent by way of Giresun and Samsun to Sivas vilayet, and that they were to be settled there in scattered places. The document comes from Sait Çetinoğlu's collection and is transcribed by Çilingir (2016, p. 51). A later instruction of Talat (22 May 1917) to Ankara, Sivas, Kastamonu and Samsun ordered that deportees suspected of harmful activity be split into small groups under surveillance (BOA DH.ŞFR 60/144, 73/72, 76/243; ibid.).", "people": ["Mehmed Talat Pasha", "Ahmed Muammer Bey"], "sources_primary": ["[Ottoman document] Dahiliye Nezareti, Emniyet-i Umumiye Müdüriyeti, cipher of Talat to the vali of Sivas, 20 Haziran 1332 (umumi 49011, hususi 45), from the collection of Sait Çetinoğlu, transcribed in Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], p. 51"], "sources_modern": ["Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], pp. 51-52"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The number of deportees is unclear in the transcription ('zükür ve inas üç-dört bir raddesinde'). Görele and Vakfıkebir lay close to the front of July 1916; whether these Greeks were refugees from the Russian-held zone is not stated. Akçam (2012, p. 107) quotes a cable of Talat to Sivas of 29 June 1916 (BOA DH.ŞFR 65/126) saying that Greeks reported earlier had been sent to Sivas for military reasons, without naming their places of origin; it may belong to the same affair, but the dates differ (3 July in Çilingir's document). The May 1916 rule for the coast sent those moved from the Trebizond vilayet to Kastamonu and to Sivas (H6-014).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Talat's cipher of 3 July 1916 (order to deport: admission)", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Çetinoğlu (collection) and Çilingir (transcription); flagged"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Görele (Elevi)", "lat": 41.031, "lon": 39.003, "role": "deported from"}, {"name": "Vakfıkebir", "lat": 41.046, "lon": 39.278, "role": "deported from"}, {"name": "Şarlı (Şalpazarı)", "lat": 40.94, "lon": 39.19, "role": "deported from (nahiye; approximate)"}, {"name": "Giresun (Kerasous)", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.39, "role": "route: waypoint 1"}, {"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "route: waypoint 2"}, {"name": "Sivas (Sebasteia)", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.016, "role": "destination vilayet"}]}, {"id": "H7-008", "date": "1916-11-30", "date_text": "29 or 30 November 1916 (statements of 26 and 28 November)", "precision": "day", "title": "Kwiatkowski reports Rafet Bey's words: 'do with the Greeks as we did with the Armenians'", "summary": "Ernst von Kwiatkowski, the Austro-Hungarian consul for Trebizond, who had moved to Samsun after the Russian capture of Trebizond, reported to Foreign Minister Burián at the end of November 1916 what the local commander Rafet (Refet) Bey had told him. In the English of greek-genocide.net, which cites the file (report of 30 November): on 26 November, 'We must at last do with the Greeks as we did with the Armenians…'; on 28 November, 'We must now finish with the Greeks. I sent today battalions to the outskirts to kill every Greek they pass on the road.' Kyriakidis (2026, p. 50), after Enepekidis (1996, p. 139), dates the 'top secret report' from Samsun to 29 November 1916 and renders the words: 'We must at last do away with the Greeks. Today I sent patrols of gendarmes to the outskirts ordering them to kill any Greek they would come across.'", "people": ["Ernst von Kwiatkowski", "Rafet Bey (Rafet Pasha)", "István Burián", "Rafet Bey (Refet Bele)"], "sources_primary": ["[Austrian diplomatic] Kwiatkowski to Burián, Samsun, 30 Nov 1916, HHStA PA XXXVIII, Karton 369, Konsulate 1916, Trapezunt, Zl. 44/pol. (reference as cited by greek-genocide.net, 'Rafet Pasha', n. 1)", "Kwiatkowski to Burián, Samsun, 30 Nov 1916, HHStA Vienna, PA XXXVIII, Karton 369, Konsulate 1916, Trapezunt, Zl. 44/pol. (reference as cited by greek-genocide.net, 'Rafet Pasha', n. 1)"], "sources_modern": ["Enepekidis, Γενοκτονία στον Εύξεινο Πόντο. Διπλωματικά έγγραφα από τη Βιέννη (1909-1918) (Thessaloniki, 1996), p. 139, as cited by Kyriakidis 2026, p. 50 n. 48", "Kyriakidis (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias Press, 2026), p. 50", "Bjørnlund, 'The persecution of Greeks in Anatolia' (2011)", "greek-genocide.net, 'Rafet Pasha' (archival citation)", "Kyriakides (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias 2026; Greek ed. 2015), p. 50 (Kwiatkowski, 'top secret report sent from Samsun on 29 November 1916'; English after Enepekides 1996, p. 139; n. 47: Fotiadis dates Refet's earlier remark to 26 November 1915)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "A reported conversation, not an order. The report is dated 30 November 1916 by greek-genocide.net, which gives the Vienna archive reference, and 29 November by Kyriakidis (p. 50), after Enepekidis (1996). Fotiadis, as cited by Kyriakidis (n. 47), dates a remark of Refet's to Kwiatkowski 'in the same sense' to 26 November 1915, probably a slip for 1916. Bjørnlund's chapter on the Danish sources in Hofmann et al. (2011), sometimes cited for the episode, does not give it; it notes that there were no Danish observers in Pontus in 1916 (p. 176).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Kwiatkowski (Austro-Hungarian consular report, HHStA)", "Greek sources: greek-genocide.net (transmits reference and English); Enepekidis 1996 (edition); Kyriakidis 2026", "Neutral sources: Kwiatkowski (Austro-Hungarian consular report, HHStA); Bjørnlund 2011", "Greek sources: greek-genocide.net (transmits the archival reference and the English text)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "where the statements were made and the report written"}, {"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "where the conversations took place"}]}, {"id": "H7-011", "date": "1917-01-11", "date_end": "1917-01-21", "date_text": "11 January 1917 (Talat's cable); 21 January 1917 (the circular)", "precision": "day", "title": "Talat's cable to Samsun: the coastal Greeks to be taken 30-50 km inland", "summary": "On 11 January 1917 the interior minister, Talat, sent a coded telegram to the district (sancak) of Samsun (Canik). He had spoken with Enver, who said that the Third Army had made the arrangements; the aim was to take the Greeks along the coast \"some thirty to fifty kilometers inland\", and no person or property was to be attacked during the deportations. On 21 January the Security Directorate told the provinces of Sivas, Kastamonu and Ankara and the district of Bolu that the Third Army command judged it urgent to remove the Greeks of the Samsun basin further inland, and that they were to be placed in villages in those provinces. Akçam, who quotes both cables from the Ottoman archive, adds that the order was not carried out as written: some villages had a few hours' notice, men were taken into labour battalions, women and children could take nothing with them, Muslim neighbours looted the villages, and villagers were made to sign statements that their attackers had been Armenians (Akçam 2012, pp. 111-112). Kieser writes that 'Talaat ordered the deportation of the Pontus Greeks of the Black Sea coast' (2018, pp. 257-258). The removals in the Samsun district had begun in December 1916 (H6-028).", "people": ["Mehmed Talat Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["[Ottoman document] Talat to the provincial district of Samsun, coded telegram, 11 January 1917, BOA DH.ŞFR 71/234 (Akçam 2012, p. 111, n. 52)", "[Ottoman document] Interior Ministry, General Directorate of Security, to the provinces of Sivas, Kastamonu and Ankara and the district of Bolu, 21 January 1917, BOA DH.ŞFR 72/62 (Akçam 2012, p. 111, n. 51)"], "sources_modern": ["Akçam, The Young Turks' Crime against Humanity (Princeton UP, 2012), ch. 4, 'The Transformation of Ottoman Policies toward the Ottoman Greeks during the First World War', pp. 111-112 [Turkish-born scholar abroad]", "Akçam 2012, Preface, p. xxi: the military relocation of Christians, 'particularly Greeks', from strategic coastal areas inland 'beginning in late 1916' [Turkish-born scholar abroad]", "Akçam, 'Introduction: The Anatomy of the Ottoman Genocide', in Akçam, Kyriakidis & Chatzikyriakidis (eds), The Genocide of the Christian Populations in the Ottoman Empire and Its Aftermath (1908-1923) (Routledge, 2023), p. 10 (the late-1916/1917 deportations from the Samsun and Trebizond coasts; the reason given was the Russian advance)", "Kieser, Talaat Pasha: Father of Modern Turkey, Architect of Genocide (Princeton UP, 2018), pp. 257-258"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Scope: the cable went to the Samsun district, but its words speak of 'the Greeks along the coast'; the circular of 21 January speaks of the Samsun basin. As Akçam quotes them, neither names the Trebizond vilayet; Kieser's 'Black Sea coast' is wider. Distance: the cable gives 30-50 km, but the circular ten days later sends the Samsun Greeks to villages in Sivas, Kastamonu, Ankara and Bolu, far beyond that band, and the deportees went by Kavak and Havza to Çorum, Amasya and beyond (H6-028). Companion orders: Enver's 50-km evacuation order of December 1916 (Hofmann 2011, pp. 59-60) and the 50-km notice of November 1916 relayed to the Metropolitan of Kerasous, repeated by Vehip Pasha on 20 November 1916 (Emmanouilidis' memoir, via Çilingir, p. 54). The instructions of 3 February 1917 left the choice of places to the army and extended the removals to townspeople (AK-003). The Ottoman security rationale is given under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H6-T03).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Talat's cable (BOA DH.ŞFR 71/234) and the Security Directorate's circular (72/62), as quoted by Akçam 2012, p. 111", "Neutral sources: Akçam 2012; Kieser 2018"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "order issued"}]}, {"id": "H7-012", "date": "1917-01-10", "date_end": "1917-01-13", "date_text": "10-13 January 1917", "precision": "day", "title": "Mass expulsion of the Greeks of Samsun town", "summary": "In Samsun, deportation of notables was followed by mass removal on 10, 11 and 13 January 1917; the columns were mostly women and children (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 391). A postwar investigation by the American Ralph Chesbrough (8 August 1919) suggested that about 5,000 Greeks and a similar number of Armenians were 'eliminated' from Samsun 'by massacre, expulsion, and flight to the hills'; Morris & Ze'evi add that Samsun's Turks also suffered heavy wartime losses in combat and from disease. In all, they write, 'tens of thousands of Greeks were marched inland from the Pontus coastline' in late 1916 and early 1917 (p. 391, after Germanos's letter of 20 December 1916 to the American missionary Mary Graffam at Sivas). The Metropolitan of Amaseia, Germanos Karavangelis, reported about 30,000 deportees from his diocese sent towards Ankara, most of whom died. Çetinoğlu, after Germanos's letters, writes that on 9 January 80 Samsun notables were arrested and sent to Havza, and that the general deportation began the same day from the Greek suburb of Kadıköy, 4,000 people being sent to Havza and then Çorum. The first of the Legation's reports (Brown & Ion 1918, pp. 48-50) dates the arrest of eighty of the richest Greeks of Samsun to 27 December 1916; the same day the army surrounded the town, the people of Upper Samsun (Kadıköy) were called to the square to hear the governor and seized, and the same was done at Aylaskoy. They were marched through the night over the snow, by Mount Tsoumbouz and Kavak to Havza, four days without food, then on to Çorum. More arrests followed on 10, 11 and 13 January and on 17 and 18 January 1917.", "people": ["Germanos Karavangelis, Metropolitan of Amaseia"], "sources_primary": ["[US official, post-war] Ralph Chesbrough, 'Report on Political, Economic and Commercial Conditions at Samsoun...', 8 August 1919, USNA RG 59, 867.00, Roll 7 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 59)", "[Greek, to an American missionary] Germanos to Mary Graffam, 20 December 1916, and Graffam to Peet, 24 December 1916, LC, HM Sr. Papers, Reel 22 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 61)", "[Greek diplomatic] Greek Legation reports of 14 and 29 January and 7 and 29 February 1917 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 58)", "[Patriarchate compilation] Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (1919), pp. 120-122 (via Morris & Ze'evi nn. 58, 61, and Travis 2017)", "[Greek diplomatic] Greek Legation, Constantinople, communication no. 548, reports of January-February 1917, in Brown & Ion 1918, pp. 48-50"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 391", "Çetinoğlu, 'T.C.'nin soykırımlar geleneği ve Pontos soykırımı' [Turkish writer], p. 15 (after Germanos's letters to the Patriarchate)", "Akçam, The Young Turks' Crime against Humanity (Princeton UP, 2012), p. 112 (in January 1917 some 80 notables of Samsun arrested and 4,000 inhabitants deported, first to Havza and later to Çorum, into the former homes of deported Armenians) [Turkish-born scholar abroad]"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The 30,000 is the Metropolitan's. An Ottoman report gives 13,406 Greeks sent inland from Samsun (H7-053). Chesbrough's 5,000 covers massacre, expulsion and flight together. Akçam (2012, p. 112) gives the same 80 notables and 4,000 deportees as Çetinoğlu, and adds that they were settled in the houses of deported Armenians; both rest on Germanos' letters, Akçam through Yerasimos (1994). The Legation report dates the arrest of the 80 notables to 27 December 1916 (Brown & Ion 1918, p. 48); Çetinoğlu has 9 January and Akçam 'the first month of 1917'. The report names the stop before Çorum 'Kaphzan, in the Province of Sebastia, 80 kilometres from Samsoun', which by the route and the distance is Havza.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Chesbrough (US); Mary Graffam (US missionary) as recipient; Morris & Ze'evi", "Greek sources: Germanos Karavangelis; Greek Legation; Patriarchate", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Çetinoğlu (transmitter of Germanos's letters; flagged)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "deported from"}, {"name": "Ankara (Angora)", "lat": 39.933, "lon": 32.86, "role": "destination region"}]}, {"id": "H7-014", "date": "1917-01-20", "date_text": "20 January 1917", "precision": "day", "title": "Pallavicini: 'Death awaits them all'", "summary": "Ambassador Pallavicini reported: 'The situation of the deported is for despair. Death awaits them all.' The same letter says he had warned the Grand Vizier that the persecution could take 'the form and dimensions of the Armenian persecutions'. Austria-Hungary was then the Ottoman Empire's ally.", "people": ["Johann von Pallavicini"], "sources_primary": ["[Austrian diplomatic] Pallavicini, HHStA PA XII, Liasse 467 LIV 'Griechenverfolgungen in der Türkei 1916-1918', Nr. 6/P., Constantinople, 20 Jan 1917 (as cited by greek-genocide.net, 'The Samsun Deportations', n. 5)"], "sources_modern": ["Çetinoğlu, 'T.C.'nin soykırımlar geleneği ve Pontos soykırımı' [Turkish writer], p. 15 (Turkish translation, citing HHStA PA Türkei XII, Liasse 467 LIV, Nr. 6/P.)"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Pallavicini (Austro-Hungarian embassy report, HHStA)", "Greek sources: greek-genocide.net (transmits the reference and the English)", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Çetinoğlu (Turkish translation; not an independent check)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "report written"}], "disputed": "The letter is known only from the English translation on greek-genocide.net ('The Samsun Deportations', n. 5), which gives the Vienna archive reference. Çetinoğlu's Turkish version cites the same file and is not an independent witness. Enepekides published the Austrian documents on the Pontic Greeks, but neither his edition nor the original in Vienna was consulted for this atlas."}, {"id": "AK-003", "date": "1917-01-16", "date_end": "1917-03-20", "date_text": "16 January – 20 March 1917", "precision": "day", "title": "Rules for the emptied Greek villages, 1917: Muslim refugees settled, Greek property kept", "summary": "On 16 January 1917 the Interior Ministry's directorate for tribes and immigrants told Trebizond and Canik that Muslim immigrants were being settled in the areas the Greeks had left, above all on the coast, and that the Greeks' movable goods were to be kept in churches and depots. On 30 January its inspectors reported that the immigrants and refugees at Bafra had been badly settled, without proper homes, land or seed. On 3 February a list of instructions to Canik laid down that the army would decide which Greek townships and people were to be removed, and extended the removals on the coast to people from the towns. On 8 February the directorate complained to Samsun that no committees had been formed to keep the evacuees' goods and livestock, that the depots had not been sealed and that houses had been wrecked. Instructions of 13 and 24 February, and of 20 March to the Canik commission for abandoned property, ruled that, unlike Armenian property, the Greeks' property was not to be liquidated: it was to be kept, or left with relatives and partners, and only perishable goods were to go to the immigrants and the army against receipt. Akçam, who quotes these cables, concludes that the government did not mean the deportees to return, since Muslims were settled in their villages at once, and notes that the Greek property was handled by the commissions set up for Armenian property (Akçam 2012, pp. 112-113, 118-119, 354).", "sources_primary": ["[Ottoman document] Directorate of Tribal and Immigrant Affairs to Trebizond and Canik, 16 January 1917, BOA DH.ŞFR 72/28 (Akçam 2012, p. 118)", "[Ottoman document] Directorate of Tribal and Immigrant Affairs to Canik, 30 January 1917, BOA DH.ŞFR 72/124 (Bafra; Akçam 2012, p. 354)", "[Ottoman document] Security Directorate to Canik, 3 February 1917, BOA DH.ŞFR 72/148 (Akçam 2012, p. 113, n. 58)", "[Ottoman document] Directorate of Tribal and Immigrant Affairs to Samsun, 8 February 1917, BOA DH.ŞFR 72/181 (Akçam 2012, p. 112, n. 54)", "[Ottoman document] Directorate of Tribal and Immigrant Affairs to Canik, 13 February 1917, BOA DH.ŞFR 72/229, and to the Canik Liquidation Commission, 20 March 1917, BOA DH.ŞFR 74/69 (Akçam 2012, p. 119)", "[Ottoman document] Directorate of Tribal and Immigrant Affairs to Canik, 24 February 1917, BOA DH.ŞFR 73/69 (Akçam 2012, pp. 118-119; one of his notes misprints the year as 1916)", "[Greek consular report] Samsun, February 1917, in Brown & Ion 1918, pp. 18, 53-54 (the tobacco auction; title deeds promised to refugees)"], "sources_modern": ["Akçam, The Young Turks' Crime against Humanity (Princeton UP, 2012), pp. 112-113, 118-119, 354 [Turkish-born scholar abroad]"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The cables state the rules; the cable of 8 February itself reports unguarded goods and wrecked houses, and the Austrian and Greek reports describe looting and burning (H6-028). Akçam also cites a cable to Trebizond of 30 July 1917 (BOA DH.ŞFR 78/154) for coastal populations in some areas largely left in place (p. 113). Whether the kept property was returned after 1918 is not covered by these documents. A Greek Legation report from Samsun of February 1917 says that the tobacco of villages emptied but not burnt (Aylaskoy, Andreanta, Tekkeköy, Kadıköy) was put up for auction by the government, which kept the proceeds, and that Turkish refugees settled in eight Samsun villages were promised title deeds to the houses and land (Brown & Ion 1918, pp. 18, 53-54).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Interior Ministry cables to Trebizond, Canik and Samsun (BOA DH.ŞFR), as quoted by Akçam 2012", "Neutral sources: Akçam 2012 (interpretation)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "seat of the Canik district; instructions of Jan-Mar 1917"}, {"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "cable of 16 Jan 1917"}, {"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.567, "lon": 35.906, "role": "Muslim immigrants badly settled (30 Jan 1917)"}]}, {"id": "H7-053", "date": "1917-03-12", "date_text": "12 March 1917 (12 March 1333)", "precision": "day", "title": "An Ottoman count: 13,406 Greeks sent inland from Samsun", "summary": "A report by Refet (Rafet) Bey, the commander at Samsun, gives 13,406 Greeks (Rum) sent inland from Samsun in the deportations of winter 1916-17. It is a lower limit from the Ottoman side, to set beside the Greek figures of about 20,000 (Greek Legation) and 30,000 (Karavangelis).", "people": ["Rafet Bey (Refet Bele)"], "sources_primary": ["[Ottoman document] cipher telegram, BOA DH.ŞFR 548/1, 12 March 1333 (1917)", "Ottoman cipher telegram, BOA DH.ŞFR 548/1, 12 March 1333 (1917)"], "sources_modern": ["Biber, in Millî Mücadele'nin Yerel Tarihi 1918-1923 (TÜBA), ch. 13, p. 430 n. 24", "Biber, 'Karadeniz'de Millî Mücadele Yıllarında Rum Nakline Giden Süreç (1921)', in Millî Mücadele'nin Yerel Tarihi 1918-1923 (TÜBA), ch. 13, p. 430 n. 24"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "A count of those moved, not of the dead, which may not cover the whole Samsun district or every convoy. It is an admission by the state, and so is given here although it reaches us through a Turkish historian. Other Ottoman-side figures: Talat estimated in late 1917 that more than 93,000 Greeks had been deported to the interior and 164,000 to Greece during the war, for all the Ottoman Greeks (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 392, after Suny). A table 'according to official sources' of Greeks deported in 1914–17 gives Canik 62,000 and Trabzon 29, out of 213,483 in all (Çilingir 2016, p. 53, without a source); if genuine, its figure for the whole Canik sancak is far above Refet's for Samsun. Its provincial figures add up to 201,312, not the printed 213,483.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Refet's report, BOA DH.ŞFR 548/1; Talât's 1917 estimate (via Morris & Ze'evi)", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Biber (TÜBA), as transmitter", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Çilingir (table of 1914-17 deportees; source not given; flagged)", "Ottoman state records: Refet's report, BOA DH.ŞFR 548/1", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Biber (TÜBA), as transmitter of the document reference"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "port from which the deportees were sent inland"}, {"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "port from which the deportees were sent inland"}]}, {"id": "PT-003", "date": "1917-03", "date_end": "1917-12-16", "date_text": "March – December 1917", "precision": "month", "title": "The Patriarchate's record: Samsun and Bafra after the winter deportations (burnings, Turkish villagers armed, the Metropolitan removed)", "summary": "The Patriarchate's compilation continues the Samsun record after January 1917. In March and April 1917 Turkish villagers burnt Ayak-lalan, Hizirlou and Arandjak, and the army burnt Karaman, Dikedjik and Karadja-klisse and drove out their people; women were raped at Tzartak-Gueriz, Touzeren and other villages, and at Tangarlou fourteen people were killed. Kadıköy and Aylaskoy were turned into settlements for Muslim refugees. In October 1917 the Bafra authorities armed the Turkish villagers, and killings followed at several villages. On 15 October 1917 Metropolitan Germanos was sent under escort to Constantinople for not handing over Greek deserters who had attacked the Turkish village of Tcharson; he returned after the armistice. On 16 December 1917 an order at Bafra gave the Greeks ten days before deportation in midwinter (Patriarchate 1919, pp. 123-125).", "people": ["Germanos Karavangelis"], "sources_primary": ["[Patriarchate compilation] Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (Constantinople 1919), pp. 123-125"], "sources_modern": ["Greek Patriarchate, Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (Constantinople, 1919), English edition, Part III (Pontus) [Patriarchate compilation, Greek, 1919, for the peace settlement]"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "This rests on one Greek compilation. The deserters' attack on Tcharson is the Ottoman ground for the Metropolitan's removal, as the book reports it. How far the order of December 1917 was carried out is not stated.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Patriarchate compilation (1919)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "villages burnt, Mar-Apr 1917"}, {"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.567, "lon": 35.906, "role": "Turkish villagers armed, Oct 1917; deportation order 16 Dec 1917"}]}, {"id": "H7-056", "date": "1918-02", "date_end": "1918-04", "date_text": "After the Russian withdrawal from Trebizond (February 1918)", "precision": "month", "title": "Trebizond after the Russian withdrawal: orders to deport all Greeks, and irregulars in the villages (Catholic witness)", "summary": "The Capuchin superior at Trebizond, Father Lorenzo da Montemarciano, recorded in his diary that after the Russian withdrawal the city's commander issued orders, said to have come from Constantinople, that all Greek men and women, the old and the children, were to be deported inland ('deportation inland means slow death', he noted); when Metropolitan Chrysanthos asked what the government feared from women and children, the governor answered that he had to follow orders, which also mentioned forced labour. Bands of Turkish irregulars gathered in the villages around Trebizond, 'burning, killing, destroying', and many Greeks left for Batum (Kyriakidis 2026, pp. 62-63).", "people": ["Lorenzo da Montemarciano", "Chrysanthos, Metropolitan of Trebizond"], "sources_primary": ["[Catholic missionary] F. Lorenzo da Montemarciano, Memorie della Missione di Trebizonda del tempo della Guerra mondiale (AGC, AC 149), pp. 190v, 194r, 223v-224r, 225v-226v (via Kyriakidis 2026)", "[Patriarchate compilation] Chrysanthos's letter of 12 Oct 1918, Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (1919), pp. 112-116"], "sources_modern": ["Kyriakidis (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias Press, 2026), pp. 62-63"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Kyriakidis does not date the diary entries precisely. Chrysanthos's letter of 12 October 1918 (printed under 'Chryssostomos') says that Ahmed Bey, president of the Muslim committee, went to Rize and launched the attack: the Greek villages from Rize to Platana were destroyed, Rize in a day, with four of its 2,000 Greeks left; Sourmena paid 70,000 roubles to stay; Tsita, Kapıköy, Livera, Hamsiköy and Santa defended themselves; 'scarcely 2,300 Christians remained' in his diocese (Patriarchate 1919, pp. 113-116). That last figure conflicts with Chrysanthos's later account that about half were saved (C3-045), and may be a misprint for 23,000.", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Capuchin missionary's diary (via Kyriakidis 2026, a Greek historian)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon (Trapezous)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "orders issued; diary written"}, {"name": "Batumi (Batum)", "lat": 41.645, "lon": 41.636, "role": "Greeks fled there"}]}, {"id": "H7-017", "date": "1918-11", "date_end": "1920", "date_text": "November 1918 to 1920", "precision": "month", "title": "The French commissioner at Trebizond, Paul Lépissier, reports renewed persecution of the Greeks (1919-1920)", "summary": "Paul Lépissier, the French High Commission's delegate (vice-consul) at Trebizond from November 1918, placed gendarmerie inspectors in the coastal towns and inland, and reported in detail. According to Georganopoulos, who read his reports, he recorded that the Ottoman authorities and local Muslims, who were armed, obstructed the return of Greek refugees from Russia and killed returnees as alleged revolutionaries; that in May 1919 600 Pontians on the ship Pinios were prevented from landing; that by early June 1919 only 5,000-6,000 Greeks and 300 Armenians had returned to the Trebizond area; and that on 4 June 1919 organised persecutions of Greeks had begun around Kerasous, Merzifon and Bulancak. The French gendarmerie officer Ancery and Colonel Chardigny reported in the same sense. In 1919 the religious leaders of Trebizond (the Metropolitan's representative Matthaios Kofidis, the Protestant pastor Stapleton, Mgr Arzeruni and the Capuchin fathers Lorenzo and Cirillo) jointly protested to Lépissier about raids on Christian villages (Kyriakidis 2026, pp. 63-64). Lépissier was forced out of Trebizond on Ankara's orders in 1920.", "people": ["Paul Lépissier", "Pierre-Auguste Chardigny", "Matthaios Kofidis", "Lorenzo da Montemarciano", "Cirillo da Erzerum"], "sources_primary": ["[French official] Lépissier's reports, French diplomatic archives (AMAE; CADN, consulat de Trébizonde, carton 77) (not consulted)", "[Catholic missionary] Fra Cirillo Giovanni Zohrabian, A servizio dei fratelli, pp. 330-331, 360-362 (via Kyriakidis 2026)"], "sources_modern": ["Georganopoulos, 'The Evidence of the French Commission in Pontus on the Anti-Hellenic Persecutions after the End of the First World War (1919-1920)', in Akçam, Kyriakidis & Chatzikyriakidis (eds), The Genocide of the Christian Populations in the Ottoman Empire and Its Aftermath (1908-1923) (Routledge, 2023), pp. 75-84", "Kyriakidis (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias Press, 2026), pp. 63-64, 68", "Kévorkian, Parachever un génocide (Odile Jacob, 2023), chapter on the vilayet of Trebizond (Lépissier expelled on Ankara's orders; e-text without page numbers)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The 'commission' was the French commissioner's post with a few gendarmerie officers, not an investigative commission. The French envoy Damien de Martel, briefed by Lépissier in 1920, thought he 'greatly dramatized the events' (Georganopoulos p. 84). Georganopoulos also notes French policy in 1919-20 was not favourable to Greek claims.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: French official reports (Lépissier, Ancery, Chardigny); Catholic missionaries (Zohrabian)", "Greek sources: Georganopoulos 2023; Kyriakidis 2026 (historians)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon (Trapezous)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "French commissioner's post"}, {"name": "Giresun (Kerasous)", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.39, "role": "organised persecutions reported, June 1919"}, {"name": "Merzifon", "lat": 40.874, "lon": 35.463, "role": "organised persecutions reported, June 1919"}, {"name": "Bulancak", "lat": 40.938, "lon": 38.231, "role": "organised persecutions reported, June 1919"}]}, {"id": "H7-015", "date": "1918-11-04", "date_text": "Motion dated 2 November 1918, read on 4 November 1918", "precision": "day", "title": "Ottoman Chamber: a motion by Greek deputies speaks of 550,000 Greeks 'killed and annihilated'", "summary": "After the armistice of Mudros, the Greek deputy for Aydın, Emmanouil Emmanouilidis, with Tokinidis (Çatalca) and Vangel (İzmir), put a question to the new government. As transcribed by Çilingir from the minutes, it listed among the acts of the previous five years: one million Armenians killed; 250,000 Greeks expelled beyond the Ottoman borders and their property confiscated; 'after the war [began], 550,000 more Greeks killed and annihilated (katl ve imha edilmiş) on the coasts and in the hinterland of the Black Sea, the Dardanelles, the Marmara and the islands and elsewhere', their property seized; and 250,000 men of the labour battalions dead of hunger and privation. The figure covers all coastal Greeks in 1914-18, not the Pontus alone.", "people": ["Emmanouil Emmanouilidis"], "sources_primary": ["[Ottoman parliamentary minutes] Meclis-i Mebusan Zabıt Ceridesi, 11th sitting, 4 Teşrinisani 1334 (4 Nov 1918), p. 109, as transcribed by Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], pp. 53-54"], "sources_modern": ["Akçam, A Shameful Act (2006; pbk 2007), p. 107 (as cited on Wikipedia)", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (New York and Athens: Caratzas, 2011), p. 102 (after Akçam, Armenien und der Völkermord (1996), p. 87: Greek and Armenian MPs 'referred to 550,000 Greeks and one million Armenians murdered', while Turkish parliamentarians called the figures exaggerated, 'admitting overall losses of 500,000 to 600,000 victims')", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 385 (uses 'as many as 550,000' for Greeks uprooted during the war)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The motion, as transcribed, says 'killed and annihilated', not 'deported'. It is a claim by Greek deputies, not a count. Morris & Ze'evi treat 550,000 as the number of Greeks uprooted during the war, citing Rendel. Whom the Turkish deputies' 500,000-600,000 covered is not clear in Hofmann's summary.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: the claim of Greek deputies (Emmanouilidis, Tokinidis, Vangel)", "Ottoman state records: Ottoman Chamber minutes, 4 Nov 1918, p. 109 (as the record)", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Çilingir (transcription; flagged)", "Neutral sources: Akçam 2006; Hofmann 2011; Morris & Ze'evi"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "Ottoman Chamber of Deputies"}]}, {"id": "H7-016", "date": "1919", "date_text": "1919", "precision": "year", "title": "The Patriarchate's Black Book: 257,019 Pontic Greeks deported in 1914-18", "summary": "The Ecumenical Patriarchate's Μαύρη Βίβλος διωγμών και μαρτυρίων του εν Τουρκία Ελληνισμού (1914-1918) gives the number deported during the war by metropolis: Amaseia 89,370; Chaldia 64,582; Trebizond 38,434; Neocaesarea 27,216; Kolonia 19,938; Rhodopolis 17,479; total 257,019. These are deportees, not deaths. The same book gives 774,235 deported for all Ottoman Greeks, 1913-18.", "sources_primary": ["[Patriarchate compilation] Μαύρη Βίβλος (Constantinople, 1919), pp. 409-413, as tabulated by greek-genocide.net; English edition Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (1919)"], "sources_modern": ["Agtzidis 2005, p. 156 (Puaux's 257,019 given as 'victims') [Greek]"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The figures were compiled for the peace conference. They are counts of the deported, sometimes misquoted as deaths. For all Ottoman Greeks Hofmann (2011, p. 102) gives the Patriarchate's total of wartime deportees as 490,063 and Puaux's (from Patriarchate statistics) as 773,915. Agtzidis 2005, p. 156, gives Puaux's 257,019 as 'victims' (θύματα) of the Asia Minor Pontus, an example of the deportation counts being read as deaths. The 1919 English edition (Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918) has no deportation table; each diocese heading gives only communities and inhabitants (e.g. Amaseia 340 communities, 131,181 inhabitants; Chaldia 145, 77,845; Trebizond 73, 58,734; Neocaesarea 195, 67,424; Kolonia 91, 52,855; Rodopolis 47, 20,657).", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Ecumenical Patriarchate, Μαύρη Βίβλος (1919)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "published"}]}, {"id": "H7-057", "date": "1920", "date_end": "1921", "date_text": "1920-1921 (date not given)", "precision": "year", "title": "Rıza Nur's instruction to Topal Osman: 'leave no stone on stone in the Greek villages' (memoir)", "summary": "Rıza Nur, health minister in the Ankara government, wrote in his memoirs that when Topal Osman was reproached for robbing the people he defended him, told him 'Ağa, clean the Pontus well', and, when Osman said he was sparing churches and good buildings in case they were needed, told him to destroy them too and scatter their stones 'so that they cannot say there was a church here'; Osman agreed (Rıza Nur, Hayat ve Hatıratım, vol. 3, pp. 163-164, as quoted by Çetinoğlu and by Ayşe Hür).", "people": ["Rıza Nur", "Topal Osman Ağa"], "sources_primary": ["[Turkish memoir] Rıza Nur, Hayat ve Hatıratım, vol. 3 (İşaret, 1992), pp. 163-164, quoted in Çetinoğlu, 'T.C.'nin soykırımlar geleneği ve Pontos soykırımı' [Turkish writer], p. 15, and in Ayşe Hür's article reprinted by Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], p. 227"], "sources_modern": ["Çetinoğlu, 'T.C.'nin soykırımlar geleneği ve Pontos soykırımı' [Turkish writer], p. 15", "Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], p. 227"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "A memoir written years later by a minister who fell out with Kemal; it is an admission by a member of the Ankara government, but its date and accuracy are uncertain.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Rıza Nur's memoir (Turkish perpetrator-side account; admission)", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Çetinoğlu; Ayşe Hür (via Çilingir) (transmitters; flagged)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Ankara (Angora)", "lat": 39.933, "lon": 32.86, "role": "where the conversation is placed (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "H7-020", "date": "1920-08-13", "date_text": "13 August 1920", "precision": "day", "title": "Giresun: Topal Osman holds the Christian men for ransom", "summary": "Osman Ağa ('Topal Osman'), mayor of Giresun and leader of an irregular force later made the 47th regiment, imprisoned the Christian men of Giresun on the night of 13 August 1920; thereafter 'every evening five or six Christians' were shot until the community paid a ransom of 300,000 Turkish lira, and Greek men were deported inland from Giresun and Samsun 'at the order of Mustafa Kemal' (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 401, after British records). A Capuchin missionary's report of the period blames Topal Osman's 'approximately six hundred Laz' for many assassinations at Giresun (Kyriakidis 2026, pp. 66-67). In October 1920 Osman met Kemal at Ankara and was made governor general of the coast from İnebolu to Hopa (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 404).", "people": ["Topal Osman Ağa"], "sources_primary": ["[British] Minutes of the 45th and 46th meetings of the A.G.S. and Armenian and Greek Representatives, 1 and 15 Sept 1920, FO 371/5214; A.G.S. memorandum, 20 Oct 1920, FO 371/5057 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 129)", "[Catholic missionary] Fra Cirillo Giovanni Zohrabian, A servizio dei fratelli, pp. 344-346 (via Kyriakidis 2026)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), pp. 401, 404", "Kyriakidis (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias Press, 2026), pp. 66-67"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: British High Commission's Armenian-Greek Section (which drew on Greek and Armenian representatives); Catholic missionary report; Morris & Ze'evi"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Giresun (Kerasous)", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.39, "role": "killings and ransom"}]}, {"id": "H7-022", "date": "1921-01-12", "date_text": "12 January 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "Nureddin's circular No. 2082 (12 January 1921): deportation of Christian men aged 16-50 and new labour conscription", "summary": "On 12 January 1921 Nureddin issued circular No. 2082. The Turkish writer Çilingir (2016, p. 117), from Turkish military documents, gives it as announcing that Christian men aged 16 to 50 able to bear arms in Samsun, Ordu, Amasya, Tokat and Şarkikarahisar would be deported, and that those born in 1901 would be conscripted into labour battalions; Çetinoğlu quotes it as ordering labour-battalion recruits from the Ordu sancak to be gathered at Şarkikarahisar and those from Samsun at Amasya, with punishment for slack officials and 'distant exile' for any Christian who fled and for those who hid them. Güzel (2006, p. 151, as cited on Wikipedia) reads it as a proposal to deport Greek men to Amasya, Tokat and Şebinkarahisar. Early in 1921 Greek men were again conscripted; to take younger boys the authorities forged orphans' birth certificates to make them 'one to four years older than their real age' (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 404, after a relief worker's report from Sivas).", "people": ["Nureddin Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["[Turkish military] order No. 2082, 12 Jan 1921: as cited by Güzel, Dünden Bugüne Yunanistan'ın Pontus Hedefi (2006), p. 151 (as cited on Wikipedia); by Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], p. 117; and by Çetinoğlu, 'Pontos'taki Düzmece İstiklal Mahkemeleri ve Yargılamaları' (posted 7 Jan 2020; reprinted in Çilingir 2016, pp. 229ff.) [Turkish writer], p. 7 (after Pontus Meselesi (1922), pp. 398-399)", "[NER] William Hawkes, 'Report of Conditions in Sivas', 5 June 1922, USNA RG 59, 867.4016, Roll 47 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 158)"], "sources_modern": ["Güzel 2006, p. 151", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 404"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The readings of order No. 2082 differ: places of origin (Çilingir: Samsun, Ordu, Amasya, Tokat, Şarkikarahisar) or collecting and destination points (Güzel: to Amasya, Tokat and Şebinkarahisar; Çetinoğlu: recruits gathered at Şarkikarahisar and Amasya). Nureddin's stated reason (a feared Greek landing at Samsun) is under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H7-T04).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: order No. 2082, 12 Jan 1921", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Güzel 2006 (transmitter)", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Çilingir 2016; Çetinoğlu (transmitters; flagged)", "Neutral sources: Morris & Ze'evi 2019; Hawkes (NER)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amasya (Amaseia)", "lat": 40.654, "lon": 35.833, "role": "planned destination"}, {"name": "Tokat", "lat": 40.314, "lon": 36.554, "role": "planned destination"}, {"name": "Şebinkarahisar (Kolonia)", "lat": 40.288, "lon": 38.424, "role": "planned destination"}]}, {"id": "H7-059", "date": "1921-04", "date_end": "1921-12", "date_text": "From early April 1921", "precision": "month", "title": "Trebizond 1921: notables arrested, men deported; Greeks shelter in the Catholic monastery", "summary": "Early in April 1921 the persecution of the Greeks of Trebizond began openly: notables, among them the journalist Nikos Kapetanidis and the Metropolitan's representative Matthaios Kofidis, were imprisoned on charges of working for an independent Pontus and later condemned to death, and the men over sixteen were deported, many dying of hunger and exhaustion; frightened Greeks repeatedly took refuge in the Catholic monastery (Kyriakidis 2026, p. 75, citing Father Cirillo's memoir and Fotiadis). An undated note sent to the Apostolic Delegation reports that at Zonguldak the Kemalist authorities began deporting the Orthodox Greek men in early November 1921 (H7-062).", "people": ["Matthaios Kofidis", "Nikos Kapetanidis", "Cirillo da Erzerum"], "sources_primary": ["[Catholic missionary] Fra Cirillo Giovanni Zohrabian, A servizio dei fratelli, pp. 371-373 (via Kyriakidis 2026)"], "sources_modern": ["Kyriakidis (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias Press, 2026), p. 75"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Kyriakidis (p. 75) covers the paragraph with one note (n. 120), citing Father Cirillo's memoir (pp. 371–373) and, for comparison, Fotiadis (p. 370); only the shelter in the Catholic monastery is clearly Cirillo's, and the arrests, the death sentences and the deportation of the men over sixteen have no separate reference. The arrests of early 1921 in Bafra, Amasya, Merzifon, Erbaa, Samsun and Alaçam are from Fotiadis (p. 365; n. 119).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Capuchin missionary memoir (Zohrabian)", "Greek sources: Kyriakidis; Fotiadis"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon (Trapezous)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "arrests; men deported; Capuchin monastery"}]}, {"id": "B1-001", "date": "1921-04-05", "date_end": "1921-06-07", "date_text": "5 April – early June 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "Spring 1921: the villages around Bafra and Alaçam are destroyed and the men of Alaçam arrested", "summary": "The Central Army began its first operations in the Bafra district on 5 April 1921 (Çetinoğlu). US naval officers at Samsun quoted an American missionary that about 100 Greek villages south of Bafra had been destroyed in a few days from 17 May, the villagers murdered and 'the priests... crucified', and reported that the campaign was 'under strict control of the military' and 'directed by high authority—probably Angora'. A British compilation records that the whole male Christian population of Alaçam above the age of 12 was imprisoned in May 1921 and then massacred; a Greek paper of 13 May 1921 described the prison of Bafra as full of Greeks arrested without cause.", "people": ["Nureddin Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["CO USS Overton to Bristol, diary for May 1921, 7 June 1921, USNA RG 84, vol. 440 (via Morris & Ze'evi)", "British High Commission, Armenian-Greek Section records, in Yeghiayan (ed.), British Reports on Ethnic Cleansing in Anatolia, p. 236 (via Hofmann)", "Eleftheros Typos, 13 May 1921 (via Fotiadis, via Hofmann's 'Abriss')"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 405, 407-408", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), p. 74", "Çetinoğlu, 'TC'nin soykırımlar geleneği ve Pontos soykırımı' (Turkish writer), pp. 15-16 (5 April 1921)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The 'about 100 villages' figure is a missionary's, relayed by the US officers. The Alaçam statement reached the British through the Greek Patriarchate. The 5 April date for the first operations rests on Çetinoğlu; Çilingir puts it in April.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: US naval reports (via Morris & Ze'evi); British A.G.S. records (via Hofmann)", "Greek sources: Eleftheros Typos; Patriarchate information behind the A.G.S. record", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Çetinoğlu (date of first operations; flagged)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "about 100 villages to the south destroyed from 17 May (US officers)"}, {"name": "Alaçam", "lat": 41.608, "lon": 35.598, "role": "men over 12 imprisoned and killed, May 1921"}, {"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "US station ship; source of the reports"}]}, {"id": "B1-002", "date": "1921-05-26", "date_end": "1922-03-15", "date_text": "26 May 1921 – 15 March 1922", "precision": "day", "title": "Harput, Malatya, Arapgir and Diyarbakır: what the Near East Relief recorded", "summary": "Mark Ward, NER director at Harput, recorded 20,526 deportees passing through Mezre-Harput between May 1921 and February 1922 (his pamphlet gives 20,378 to January 1922), about 18,000 of them Greeks. Yowell and Ward reported that of 30,000 deportees who passed Sivas in early summer 1921, 5,000 died before Harput and 5,000 escaped; of the 20,000 who reached Harput, 2,000 died there and 3,000 were scattered in the vilayet; of 15,000 sent on to Diyarbakır, 3,000 died on the way and 1,000 in the town, 2,000 stayed and 9,000 were sent towards Bitlis. Ward's pamphlet puts 4,000 deportees at Malatya in autumn and winter, of whom about 2,000 died of hunger or typhus. Stanley Hopkins met 12,000 Greeks being driven south between Harput and Malatya on 1 September 1921 and about 10,000 around 1 October, one group of about 2,000 women alone. At Arapgir the missionaries saw a convoy of about 1,000 old people, women and children in late autumn 1921 and took in about 20 children left by the road.", "people": ["Mark H. Ward", "Forrest D. Yowell", "Stanley E. Hopkins", "Ethel Thompson", "J. Herbert Knapp", "Bessie Bannerman Murdoch"], "sources_primary": ["Ward, diary extracts (attached to Bristol to SecState, 12 July 1922) and 'Memorandum of Greek Deportations' (FO 371/7879)", "Yowell and Ward to Jackson (US consul at Aleppo), 5 Apr 1922, NA RG 59, 867.4016, roll 46", "Mark H. Ward, The Deportations in Asia Minor 1921-1922 (Anglo-Hellenic League, London 1922), p. 3 (as summarised on greek-genocide.net, 'Map of Greek deportations: Mark Ward's testimony')", "Rendel memorandum, p. 6 (Hopkins)", "Murdoch and Knapp, report on Arabkir, 14 June 1922"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 422-426", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), p. 78", "Kévorkian, Parachever un génocide: Mustafa Kemal et l'élimination des rescapés arméniens et grecs (e-text without page numbers; chapter on the vilayet of Trebizond and section 'Les déportations des populations grecques') (Ward's diary of 26 May – 21 Sept 1921 lists 11,109 deportees)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Ward's total is 20,526 in Morris & Ze'evi (p. 426) and 20,378 in his pamphlet, for slightly different periods. Bafra is not named in his table; the Bafra women's convoys reached Malatya and Hekimhan in late November and early December (B1-017, B1-019), which fits his Malatya figures. 'Osmaniye' in his table (23 Oct, 6 Nov, 8 Dec 1921) has not been identified.", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Near East Relief (Ward, Yowell, Hopkins, Knapp, Murdoch); British FO"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sivas", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.015, "role": "stage"}, {"name": "Malatya", "lat": 38.355, "lon": 38.309, "role": "4,000 held; about 2,000 died (Ward)"}, {"name": "Arapgir", "lat": 39.041, "lon": 38.495, "role": "convoys seen by missionaries"}, {"name": "Harput", "lat": 38.706, "lon": 39.256, "role": "NER station; 20,526 recorded"}, {"name": "Mamuret-ul-Aziz (Elazığ)", "lat": 38.675, "lon": 39.222, "role": "NER hospital at Mezre"}, {"name": "Diyarbakır", "lat": 37.914, "lon": 40.23, "role": "onward destination"}, {"name": "Bitlis", "lat": 38.401, "lon": 42.108, "role": "onward destination; 9,000 sent towards it"}, {"name": "Van", "lat": 38.494, "lon": 43.38, "role": "onward destination of some"}]}, {"id": "H7-025", "date": "1921-06", "date_end": "1922-06", "date_text": "Summer 1921 to 1922", "precision": "month", "title": "The death marches from Samsun to Harput, Diyarbakır and Bitlis", "summary": "Deportees from the coast were marched via Amasya and Sivas to Harput and on to Diyarbakır, Bitlis and Van. An estimated 70,000 Pontus deportees passed through Sivas, where the relief worker Theda Phelps saw 1,000-2,000 Christians arrive each week (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 421). The Near East Relief officers at Harput, Major Forrest D. Yowell and Dr Mark Ward, reported that of 30,000 deportees who passed Sivas in early summer 1921, 5,000 died before Harput and 5,000 escaped; of the 20,000 who reached Harput, 2,000 died there and 3,000 were scattered in the vilayet; of 15,000 sent on to Diyarbakır, 3,000 died on the way and 1,000 in the town, 2,000 stayed and 9,000 were sent towards Bitlis (p. 425). Ward recorded 20,526 deportees through Mezre-Harput between May 1921 and February 1922, about 18,000 of them Greeks (p. 426; his pamphlet gives 20,378 for a shorter period). The relief worker Ethel Thompson wrote that 'The Turks had given them no food on the 500 mile trip from Samsun'; those who survived were those who could pay the guards for food (p. 422). There were local exceptions: the vali of Diyarbakır let the Near East Relief shelter and feed Greek deportees and, in October-December 1921, allowed some to settle and work, and a missionary wrote that 'the Turkish Government helped us by every means in their power' (p. 424 n. 286).", "people": ["Forrest D. Yowell", "Mark H. Ward", "Ethel Thompson", "Theda Phelps", "Edith Wood"], "sources_primary": ["[NER] Yowell and Ward to Jackson, 5 April 1922, USNA RG 59, 867.4016, Roll 46 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 294)", "[NER] 'Extracts from the Diary of Dr. Mark Ward', attached to Bristol to SecState, 12 July 1922; Ward, 'Memorandum of Greek Deportations', May 1922, FO 371/7879 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 295)", "[NER] 'Statement of Ethel Thompson of Boston', 4 August 1922, USNA RG 59, 867.4016, Roll 47 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 279)", "[NER] Theda Phelps to the State Department, 2 June 1922 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 278)", "[Missionary] Emily Wade, 'To whom it may concern', 23 June 1922, RG 84 vol. 465 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 286)", "[NER, via greek-genocide.net] Ward's pamphlet tally of 20,378 deportees"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), pp. 421-426", "Shenk, 'Ethnic Cleansing, American Women, and the Admiral', in Shirinian (ed.), Genocide in the Ottoman Empire: Armenians, Assyrians, and Greeks, 1913-1923 (Berghahn, 2017), pp. 201-204 (Ward and Yowell: at least 14,000 Greek women, children and old men dead to their local knowledge)", "Shenk, America's Black Sea Fleet (Naval Institute Press, 2017), pp. 109-110 (not local)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The waypoints between Samsun and Sivas and between Harput and Bitlis are the usual road route, not confirmed for every convoy. The Yowell-Ward figures are on p. 425; '70,000 through Sivas' is an estimate (p. 421).", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Near East Relief reports (Yowell, Ward, Thompson, Phelps) via Morris & Ze'evi and Shenk 2017"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "1921-22 march: waypoint 1"}, {"name": "Kavak", "lat": 41.079, "lon": 36.044, "role": "1921-22 march: waypoint 2"}, {"name": "Havza", "lat": 40.97, "lon": 35.662, "role": "1921-22 march: waypoint 3"}, {"name": "Amasya (Amaseia)", "lat": 40.654, "lon": 35.833, "role": "1921-22 march: waypoint 4"}, {"name": "Tokat", "lat": 40.314, "lon": 36.554, "role": "1921-22 march: waypoint 5"}, {"name": "Sivas (Sebasteia)", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.016, "role": "1921-22 march: waypoint 6"}, {"name": "Malatya", "lat": 38.355, "lon": 38.309, "role": "1921-22 march: waypoint 7"}, {"name": "Harput", "lat": 38.706, "lon": 39.252, "role": "1921-22 march: waypoint 8"}, {"name": "Diyarbakır", "lat": 37.91, "lon": 40.24, "role": "1921-22 march: waypoint 9"}, {"name": "Bitlis", "lat": 38.401, "lon": 42.108, "role": "1921-22 march: waypoint 10"}]}, {"id": "B1-003", "date": "1921-06", "date_end": "1921-07", "date_text": "Late June – July 1921", "precision": "month", "title": "Dilmitzoglou: the second convoy burned in the church of Selamelik", "summary": "According to the Bafra notable Ioannis Dilmitzoglou, the second convoy of about 500 Bafra men, among them the merchants George Kouzloo (killed on the way, his head taken to the under-governor) and Kyriako Solomonoglou, was shut in the church of the village of Selamelik and burned alive; a few escaped and told of it. A semi-official Athens statement of 8 September 1921 said that 'at Bafra the male population were strangled in the church and schools'.", "people": ["George Kouzloo", "Kyriako Solomonoglou"], "sources_primary": ["Central Council of Pontus, Black Book: The Tragedy of Pontus 1914-1922 (Athens 1922), sect. IV 'The Tragedy of Baffra in Pontus, by an eye-witness... translated from the Turkish text written in Greek characters', signed John Dilmitoglou, Athens 13 Oct 1921, 'Second convoy'", "Greek semi-official statement, Athens, 8 Sept 1921 (via Hofmann)", "Fotiadis, K. E., The Genocide of the Pontian Greeks (Thessaloniki: Stamoulis 2015; English ed., also cited as 2019), section 'Kemalist deportations from the kaza Bafra', as reproduced on the Virtual Genocide Memorial (VGM) Bafra page (no page numbers)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 408", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), p. 71"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "One Greek witness, repeated by Morris & Ze'evi; we found no American or other foreign report of this burning. Ankara attributed the Bafra dead to fighting with bands; that account is under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H7-T05).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Dilmitzoglou (Black Book; Fotiadis); Athens statement", "Neutral sources: Morris & Ze'evi (repeating)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Selamelik", "lat": 41.45, "lon": 35.833, "role": "church burning, about 500 men"}]}, {"id": "B1-004", "date": "1921-06", "date_end": "1921-07", "date_text": "Summer 1921", "precision": "month", "title": "Dilmitzoglou: the third convoy (680) taken to the church of Kocasu, the fourth sent to Elbistan", "summary": "When the under-governor divided the remaining men into those liable to military service and those to be exiled, over 500 young men aged 15–35 reported to the recruiting office. The third convoy, of 400 recruits and 280 exiles (680 in all), among them the banker Demosthenes Dilmitzoglou, was taken to the church of the village the witness calls Kodje-sou (Morris & Ze'evi: Kavdje-son); 'as none escaped we know neither details nor the manner of their destruction'. A fourth convoy of 35 was sent safely to 'a town near Marash called Elvistan' (Elbistan), from where its members telegraphed friends.", "people": ["Demosthenes Dilmitzoglou", "Abraham Mavrides", "Anastasios Azzoglou", "Constantine Azzoglou"], "sources_primary": ["Central Council of Pontus, Black Book: The Tragedy of Pontus 1914-1922 (Athens 1922), sect. IV 'The Tragedy of Baffra in Pontus, by an eye-witness... translated from the Turkish text written in Greek characters', signed John Dilmitoglou, Athens 13 Oct 1921, 'Third convoy', 'Fourth convoy'"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 408 (680 'murdered in a church at Kavdje-son')"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "Some accounts give 680–780; the witness gives 680 (400 + 280) and does not know how they died. Morris & Ze'evi's 'murdered' goes beyond the witness. Kocasu has not been located.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Dilmitzoglou (Black Book)", "Neutral sources: Morris & Ze'evi (repeating)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kocasu (BB 'Kodje-sou'; M&Z 'Kavdje-son')", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "killing site; village not located, plotted at Bafra (district level)"}, {"name": "Elbistan", "lat": 38.206, "lon": 37.198, "role": "destination of the fourth convoy (35 people)"}, {"name": "Maraş", "lat": 37.585, "lon": 36.937, "role": "reference town"}]}, {"id": "B1-005", "date": "1921-06", "date_end": "1921-09", "date_text": "Summer 1921", "precision": "month", "title": "Dilmitzoglou: villages of the Bafra plain burned with their people (Muğamlı, Sürmeli and others)", "summary": "The witness says that at Muğamlı ('Mooamli'), three hours from Bafra, all 250 inhabitants were shut in the house of Dzigaloglou and burned alive, and that Sürmeli, Akgüney ('Ak konney'), Kızılgöl and Ada 'suffered the same fate'. Morris & Ze'evi report that at Sürmeli 300 were herded into houses 'and burnt alive', and that around Bafra the deportations inland 'were regularly accompanied by mass murder'. A Black Book note adds that at Bafra the men were made to pay 50–2,000 pounds each, then deported, and 200 were killed near the town within a week.", "sources_primary": ["Central Council of Pontus, Black Book: The Tragedy of Pontus 1914-1922 (Athens 1922), sect. IV 'The Tragedy of Baffra in Pontus, by an eye-witness... translated from the Turkish text written in Greek characters', signed John Dilmitoglou, Athens 13 Oct 1921", "Central Council of Pontus, Black Book: The Tragedy of Pontus 1914-1922 (Athens 1922), sect. III 'A few short notes of the Turkish cruelties... June, July and August 1921' (Athens, 15/17 Oct 1921) and supplementary notes (Athens, 25 Sept 1921)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 409"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "No exact dates. Turkish accounts say the villages destroyed had supplied the bands (B1-T05). Kızılgöl and Ada have not been located.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Black Book", "Neutral sources: Morris & Ze'evi"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Muğamlı (Altınay)", "lat": 41.614, "lon": 35.817, "role": "250 burned in a house (Dilmitzoglou)"}, {"name": "Sürmeli", "lat": 41.479, "lon": 35.951, "role": "300 burned in houses (Morris & Ze'evi)"}, {"name": "Akgüney", "lat": 41.529, "lon": 35.721, "role": "'same fate' (Dilmitzoglou)"}]}, {"id": "B1-006", "date": "1921-06", "date_text": "June 1921", "precision": "month", "title": "The Patriarch's report: seven priests of Alaçam and Bafra crucified at Gözköy", "summary": "According to the Ecumenical Patriarch, seven priests from Alaçam, Bafra and their environs were arrested, tortured in public and crucified in the marketplace of Gözköy ('Guioz-Keuy').", "sources_primary": ["Ecumenical Patriarchate, via British A.G.S. records (Yeghiayan, p. 236, via Hofmann)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 417", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), p. 74"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "May be another version of the seven priests axed at Elezli (B1-010); place and manner differ.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Patriarchate", "Neutral sources: Morris & Ze'evi; Hofmann (repeating)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Gözköy ('Guioz-Keuy')", "lat": 41.608, "lon": 35.598, "role": "crucifixion of seven priests; village not located, plotted at Alaçam"}]}, {"id": "B1-007", "date": "1921-06", "date_end": "1921-10-13", "date_text": "Summer 1921 (before 13 Oct)", "precision": "month", "title": "Escapes: to Mount Nebyan, and by sailing ship to Midye in Thrace", "summary": "Dilmitzoglou writes that four wounded men fled by night to Mount Nebyan ('Nepien'), 'the centre of the Greek refugees'; that men escaping from Bafra through the military cordon joined their companions there; and that about 500 women and children were saved with the help of men from Nebyan. He himself was rescued with about 100 others by a sailing ship and taken to Midye (Medea, today Kıyıköy) on the Black Sea coast of Eastern Thrace, then held by Greece.", "people": ["Ioannis (John) Dilmitzoglou"], "sources_primary": ["Central Council of Pontus, Black Book: The Tragedy of Pontus 1914-1922 (Athens 1922), sect. IV 'The Tragedy of Baffra in Pontus, by an eye-witness... translated from the Turkish text written in Greek characters', signed John Dilmitoglou, Athens 13 Oct 1921", "Toynbee Papers 50 (title of the same text, via Morris & Ze'evi n. 182)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 408 n. 182"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The 500 saved are a Greek figure. Turkish accounts treat Nebyan as a rebel base (B1-T05). Nebiyan Dağı rises to 1,285 m, the second-highest peak of the Bafra district (PeakVisor).", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Dilmitzoglou (Black Book)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "escape route: waypoint 1"}, {"name": "Nebyan Dağı", "lat": 41.35, "lon": 36.0, "role": "mountain refuge of the Bafra bands (approximate position)"}, {"name": "Midye (Kıyıköy), Eastern Thrace", "lat": 41.63, "lon": 28.095, "role": "escape by sea: waypoint 2, end point"}]}, {"id": "B1-008", "date": "1921-06", "date_end": "1921-09-30", "date_text": "Mid-June – end of September 1921", "precision": "month", "title": "Totals for Bafra: 14,010 deported, at least 9,140 dead (Gavriilidis)", "summary": "Sam Topalidis, citing the Bafra tobacco merchant and deportee Antonios Gavriilidis (1924, via Hionides 1996), gives 14,010 men, women and children deported from Bafra between mid-June and the end of September 1921, of whom at least 9,140 were killed directly or died of hunger or disease.", "people": ["Antonios Gavriilidis"], "sources_primary": ["Gavriilidis, Η μαύρη εθνική συμφορά του Πόντου 1914-1922 (Athens 1924)"], "sources_modern": ["Hionides, The Greek Pontians of the Black Sea (Boston 1996)", "Topalidis, S., 'A History of Bafra, Pontos', pontosworld.com (no. 792)"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "Topalidis writes 'at least 9,140 (at least 83%)', but 9,140 of 14,010 is 65%; one number is wrong. Compare the US reports' 6,000 + 2,500 women and children (B1-015, B1-018) and Yerasimos's three men's convoys of 500–600 (B1-009).", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Gavriilidis 1924 (via Hionides and Topalidis)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "origin"}]}, {"id": "H7-029", "date": "1921-06-02", "date_end": "1921-08-16", "date_text": "2 June - 15/16 August 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "The Kavak road: convoys of deported men shot (June-August 1921; 1,300 on 15-16 August)", "summary": "Convoys of men deported from Samsun and Bafra were fired on by their escorts on the road to Kavak. A western report, Rendel's Foreign Office memorandum, claimed that 1,300 Greeks were murdered in the gorge on 15 or 16 August 1921; the Greek representative to the League of Nations put the dead of at least two convoys shot near Kavak at 'at least 900' (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 408). The missionary Gertrude Anthony reported that many from the first and third groups of Samsun men had been massacred near Kavak (Shenk 2017, p. 203). On 2 June 1921 the mutasarrıf of Canik and the commander of the 10th Division reported to the Central Army that a convoy of 766 Greeks sent from Samsun to Kavak had been attacked in the Canik mountains and that many Greeks had been killed and wounded (Çilingir, p. 117); in the Grand National Assembly the Lazistan deputy Ziya Hurşit said that the first convoy from Samsun was attacked six hours out of the town (ibid., p. 118). Further counts: 330 and 660 of two convoys (Black Book), and 550 executed on 26 June and 1,300 on 15 August (Stanley Hopkins of the NER).", "sources_primary": ["[British] Rendel, 'Turkish Massacres and Persecutions of Minorities since the Armistice', 20 March 1922, FO 371/7876 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 188)", "[League of Nations] communiqué to member states, 14 Nov 1921, FO 371/6536 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 187)", "[Turkish official] Canik mutasarrıf Sezai and 10th Division commander İsmail to the Central Army, 2 June 1921 (ATASE), via Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], p. 117, and Çetinoğlu, 'Pontos'taki Düzmece İstiklal Mahkemeleri ve Yargılamaları' (posted 7 Jan 2020; reprinted in Çilingir 2016, pp. 229ff.) [Turkish writer], p. 8", "[Turkish parliamentary] TBMM Gizli Celse Zabıtları, vol. 2, 29 Oct 1921, pp. 403-409 (Ziya Hurşit), via Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], p. 118"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 408", "Shenk, 'Ethnic Cleansing, American Women, and the Admiral', in Shirinian (ed.), Genocide in the Ottoman Empire: Armenians, Assyrians, and Greeks, 1913-1923 (Berghahn, 2017), p. 203", "Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], pp. 117-118", "See also B1-011, B1-013"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The counts overlap and should not be added. The Ottoman report of 2 June 1921 attributes the attack to 'brigands' and Ankara later said the Kavak dead fell in battle; both claims are under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H7-T05).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Rendel (FO); League of Nations; Anthony (US missionary); Hopkins (NER, via B1)", "Greek sources: Greek representative to the League; Black Book (via B1)", "Ottoman state records: Canik and 10th Division report of 2 June 1921 (deaths in a convoy: admission); TBMM minutes", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Çilingir; Çetinoğlu (transmitters; flagged)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kavak", "lat": 41.079, "lon": 36.044, "role": "killings in the gorge"}]}, {"id": "H7-023", "date": "1921-06-09", "date_text": "9 June 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "Greek navy bombards İnebolu", "summary": "Greek warships shelled İnebolu, the port through which supplies reached Ankara, on 9 June 1921. Morris & Ze'evi (p. 407) call it a light shelling that 'produced no casualties'; Count Schmeccia of Lloyd Triestino at Samsun, formerly a representative of the Italian High Commission, called it merely a Turkish 'excuse for the massacres'. They add that the shelling 'may indeed have hastened the deportation process, but it wasn't the cause of the campaign, which was launched weeks before'.", "sources_primary": ["[US Navy, quoting an Italian] 'Diary of Station Ship at Samsun, USS Brooks', entry for 14 July 1921, USNA RG 84 vol. 438 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 175)", "[British Navy] Knox-Little, HMS Sportive, letter of proceedings 27 July-3 Aug 1921, FO 371/6498 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 174)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 407", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), pp. 8, 12 (date 9 June 1921 from Turkish documents)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The date is confirmed by the Turkish documents (Biber; Korucu & Dağlıoğlu; H6-055). Ankara's use of the danger of a Greek landing as a reason for the deportations is under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H7-T04).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Morris & Ze'evi; Schmeccia (Italian) via USS Brooks; HMS Sportive", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Biber (TÜBA) (date)", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Korucu & Dağlıoğlu (date; flagged)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "İnebolu", "lat": 41.975, "lon": 33.76, "role": "bombarded"}]}, {"id": "H7-026", "date": "1921-06-16", "date_text": "16 June 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "Ankara decrees the deportation of Greek men of military age from the Pontic coast", "summary": "On 3 June 1921 the defence minister Fevzi (Çakmak) told the Central Army that the government had decided to move armed Greeks of the coast inland; the Council of Ministers' order of 12 June relocated Greeks aged 15 to 50 from the coast and was put into effect on 16 June (decree No. 941 is dated 16 June); the coast had been declared a war zone from 12 June, and in July the area of relocation was widened (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, pp. 12-13; Çilingir, pp. 117-118). US sources give 16-50, British 15-55. On 16 June police began to round up Samsun's Greek males aged 15-50, and on the 19th about 2,000 were marched inland (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 410). Nureddin's order No. 2245 of 19 June sent armed Greek men to the Ergani Madeni, Malatya and Maraş sancaks and, from Sivas, to Gürün and Darende. An Interior Ministry order of 25 June did not recommend sending women and children inland, but on Nureddin's orders they were sent too (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 25). In Samsun seventy Turkish notables telegraphed Kemal that it was 'against their religion to massacre' women and children, the mufti issued a fatwa against it, and Kemal replied that exiling Samsun's Greeks was 'a merciful act' (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 410); in July relocation was suspended at Samsun after Muslim notables complained of massacres. The memoirist Tepeyran puts the number deported from Samsun at nearly 21,000. By November 1921 the Central Army's records counted 63,844 Greeks moved inland (Biber, after Balcıoğlu); another breakdown from the same records gives 27,995 from Samsun, 14,000 from Amasya, 8,500 from Giresun, 4,910 from Ordu, 1,448 from Sivas, 1,000 from Tokat, 571 from Çorum and 550 from Sinop, 58,974 in all, relocated to Sivas, Tokat, Yozgat, Çorum and Şebinkarahisar (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 25).", "people": ["Nureddin Pasha", "Mustafa Kemal"], "sources_primary": ["[Ankara government] decree of 16 June 1921 and BCA 941/95-12 (12 June 1921), via Biber (TÜBA) ch. 13, p. 436 nn. 75-77", "[Turkish military] Nureddin's order No. 2245, 19 June 1921 (Pontus Meselesi (1922), p. 396, via Çetinoğlu, 'Pontos'taki Düzmece İstiklal Mahkemeleri ve Yargılamaları' (posted 7 Jan 2020; reprinted in Çilingir 2016, pp. 229ff.) [Turkish writer], p. 7); Fevzi to the Central Army, 3 June 1921 (via Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], pp. 117-118)", "[Central Army records] via Balcıoğlu, İki İsyan (2003), pp. 86-127, cited by Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), p. 25", "[US Navy] USS Overton notes, 16-17 June 1921; USS Scorpion to Bristol, 19 June 1921 (via Morris & Ze'evi nn. 207-208)", "[British] A.G.S. minutes, 31 Aug 1921, FO 371/6548 (Samsun notables' telegram; via Morris & Ze'evi n. 204)", "[Turkish memoir] Ebubekir Hazım Tepeyran, Belgelerle Kurtuluş Savaşı Anıları (1982), p. 81 (c. 21,000 deported; as cited on Wikipedia)"], "sources_modern": ["Mango, Atatürk (1999), p. 330", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 410", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), pp. 12-13, 25", "Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], pp. 117-118"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The two Central Army figures differ (63,844 by November 1921; 58,974 in the breakdown) and may cover different dates. Turkish historiography's description of the measure as a lawful wartime relocation is under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H7-T04). Critics point to the inclusion of women and children and the mortality on the marches; the Interior Ministry's own order of 25 June did not cover women and children.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: decree of 16 June 1921; BCA 941/95-12; orders Nos 2245 and of 25 June 1921; Central Army records (numbers moved: admissions)", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Biber (TÜBA); Balcıoğlu; Tepeyran 1982", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019; Çilingir 2016; Çetinoğlu (transmitters; flagged)", "Neutral sources: Mango 1999; Morris & Ze'evi 2019; US naval reports; British A.G.S."], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "main point of departure"}, {"name": "Amasya (Amaseia)", "lat": 40.654, "lon": 35.833, "role": "first stage inland"}]}, {"id": "B1-009", "date": "1921-06-16", "date_end": "1921-06-28", "date_text": "16–28 June 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "The first convoys of men leave Samsun, Bafra and Alaçam", "summary": "On 16 June 1921 police began to round up the Greek men of Samsun aged 15–50; on the 19th, US officers reported, about 2,000 were marched inland. Stefanos Yerasimos gives the convoy dates: Samsun on 16 and 17 June (the second, of about 700, reached Amasya) and 25 June; Bafra on 17, 21 and 24 June, each of about 500–600 men; Alaçam on 18, 21 and 22 June (one of about 1,000). Kévorkian dates the call-up of the Bafra men to 28 June. From September old people, women and children were sent too.", "people": ["Nureddin Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["Ankara decree of 16 June 1921 (see H7-026)", "USS Overton, notes for 16-17 June 1921; USS Scorpion to Bristol, 19 June 1921 (via Morris & Ze'evi)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 410", "Yerasimos, Milliyetler ve Sınırlar, p. 412 (via Çetinoğlu and Biber)", "Biber, ch. 13 in the TÜBA history series, p. 437, nn. 79-80", "Çetinoğlu, 'TC'nin soykırımlar geleneği ve Pontos soykırımı' (Turkish writer), p. 16", "Kévorkian, Parachever un génocide: Mustafa Kemal et l'élimination des rescapés arméniens et grecs (e-text without page numbers; chapter on the vilayet of Trebizond and section 'Les déportations des populations grecques')"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Biber gives the third Alaçam convoy as 22 June in one place and 20 June in another. A Bafra convoy on 17 June comes a day before the round-up of Saturday 18 June (B1-010); Kévorkian's 28 June is later still.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: US naval reports (via Morris & Ze'evi); Yerasimos; Kévorkian", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Biber (transmitter of dates)", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Çetinoğlu (transmitter of dates)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "origin, 16, 17, 25 June"}, {"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "origin, 17, 21, 24 June"}, {"name": "Alaçam", "lat": 41.608, "lon": 35.598, "role": "origin, 18, 21, 22 June"}, {"name": "Amasya", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "destination of the 17 June Samsun convoy"}]}, {"id": "H7-024", "date": "1921-06-18", "date_end": "1921-09-19", "date_text": "18 June – 19 September 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "Bafra district, June-September 1921: the men seized and killed, villages burned, women and children deported", "summary": "On the morning of Saturday 5/18 June 1921 (Julian/Gregorian; the 'June 5' of Morris & Ze'evi, p. 408, is the Julian date) troops, gendarmes and armed local Turks surrounded Bafra and seized the Greek men, who were marched off in convoys. The killing of the convoys in churches rests on a single Greek eyewitness, the Bafra notable Ioannis Dilmitzoglou, whose account (signed Athens, 13 October 1921) is printed in the 1922 Black Book: about 535 men taken to the church at Suludere near Elezli (Morris & Ze'evi's 'Blezli'), the seven priests killed with an axe at the door; about 500 burned in the church of Selamelik; and a third convoy of 680 (400 recruits and 280 exiles) taken to the church of 'Kodje-sou' (Morris & Ze'evi's 'Kavdje-son'), whose fate the witness did not know. Morris & Ze'evi's figures (p. 408) cite this same text from the Toynbee Papers. Villages were burned with their people: 300 at Sürmeli (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 409) and 250 at Muğamlı (the same witness). On 8 July 1921 the US destroyer Brooks reported that in the Bafra area 'as many as 90 percent of deported Greeks have been killed' (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 409, n. 200); dated before any women were deported, it concerns the men's convoys of June. On 3 August the mutasarrıf of Bafra told a US officer that the deportation 'of all remaining Greeks, including women and children, had been ordered by Angora' (p. 407). US destroyers then reported about 6,000 women and children deported from Bafra around 31 August and 2,500 more on 19 September (p. 408; Shenk 2017, pp. 200-201). These convoys and those described by Fotiadis (16 September, 4,120 people; 24 September, 1,900) may be the same convoys dated differently, so the figures must not be added up.", "people": ["Ioannis Dilmitzoglou", "Nureddin Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["[Greek eyewitness] Dilmitzoglou, 'The Tragedy of Baffra in Pontus, by an eye-witness... translated from the Turkish text written in Greek characters', signed Athens, 13 Oct 1921, in Central Council of Pontus, Black Book: The Tragedy of Pontus 1914-1922 (Athens, 1922), archive.org item blackbooktragedy00281prich (no page numbers), sect. IV; the same text in Bodl. MS Toynbee Papers 50 (via Morris & Ze'evi nn. 182, 186)", "[US Navy] USS Brooks to Bristol, 8 July 1921, USNA RG 84 vol. 438 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 200)", "[US Navy] USS Overton, 'Samsun Diary', 3 Aug 1921, RG 84 vol. 440 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 178)", "[US Navy] CO USS McFarland to Bristol, 1 Sept 1921; CO USS Sturtevant to Bristol, 20 Sept 1921, RG 84 vol. 438 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 192)", "[British] enclosure in Lindley's despatch No. 286, 3 June 1922, FO 371/7879 ('June 5'; via Morris & Ze'evi n. 185)", "Dilmitzoglou, 'The Tragedy of Baffra in Pontus', in Central Council of Pontus, Black Book: The Tragedy of Pontus 1914–1922 (Athens 1922), sect. IV, leaves 30–32 (archive.org scan; no printed page numbers, cited by section and scan leaf)", "USS Brooks to Bristol, 8 July 1921, USNA RG 84 vol. 438 (via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 200)", "CO USS McFarland to Bristol, 1 Sept 1921; CO USS Sturtevant to Bristol, 20 Sept 1921 (via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 192)", "Rendel memorandum, 20 March 1922, p. 5 (Kavak; via Hofmann 2011, p. 75)", "Valavanis 1925"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), pp. 407-409", "Shenk, 'Ethnic Cleansing, American Women, and the Admiral', in Shirinian (ed.), Genocide in the Ottoman Empire: Armenians, Assyrians, and Greeks, 1913-1923 (Berghahn, 2017), pp. 200-201", "See also B1-003, B1-004, B1-005, B1-010, B1-013, B1-014, B1-015, B1-017, B1-018, B1-019", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 408–409", "Shenk, 'Ethnic Cleansing, American Women, and the Admiral', in Shirinian (ed.), Genocide in the Ottoman Empire: Armenians, Assyrians, and Greeks, 1913–1923 (2017), pp. 200–201", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), p. 75", "See also B1-003, B1-004, B1-010, B1-011, B1-013, B1-015, B1-017–B1-019", "Fotiadis 2015/2019 (via the Virtual Genocide Memorial)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Ankara's version is under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H7-T05, H6-T08). The witness writes '5th/18th of June 1921, Saturday morning'; 5 June, the date in Morris & Ze'evi, is the Julian date. The church burnings rest on one Greek witness, whose text Morris & Ze'evi's figures also cite (p. 408). He gives 680 for the Kocasu convoy, not the '680–780' sometimes quoted, and does not say how they died. The '90 percent' is the report of the USS Brooks of 8 July 1921, not of Yowell or Ward, and concerns the men. The US and Fotiadis convoys may overlap. 'Soulou Dere' may be the Suludere rural district of Bafra (Nişanyan); Elezli and Kocasu are not in Nişanyan.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Dilmitzoglou (Black Book; Toynbee Papers)", "Neutral sources: US naval reports (Brooks, Overton, McFarland, Sturtevant); Morris & Ze'evi; Shenk 2017", "Greek sources: Dilmitzoglou (Black Book 1922); Valavanis 1925; Fotiadis", "Neutral sources: US Navy (Brooks, McFarland, Sturtevant); Rendel (FO); Morris & Ze'evi 2019 (repeating Dilmitzoglou for the churches); Shenk 2017; Hofmann 2011"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.906, "role": "town surrounded, men seized (18 June 1921); women and children deported (Aug-Sept 1921)"}, {"name": "Elezli / Suludere (not located; plotted at Bafra)", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.906, "role": "killing of about 535 men (single Greek witness)"}, {"name": "Selamelik", "lat": 41.45, "lon": 35.833, "role": "church burning, about 500 men (single Greek witness)"}, {"name": "Sürmeli", "lat": 41.479, "lon": 35.951, "role": "300 herded into houses and burned (Morris & Ze'evi p. 409)"}, {"name": "Muğamlı (Altınay)", "lat": 41.614, "lon": 35.817, "role": "250 burned in a house (single Greek witness)"}, {"name": "Alaçam", "lat": 41.611, "lon": 35.596, "role": "men's convoys, June 1921 (B1-009)"}, {"name": "Kavak", "lat": 41.079, "lon": 36.044, "role": "killing of men, 15–16 Aug 1921"}]}, {"id": "B1-010", "date": "1921-06-18", "date_text": "18 June 1921 (5 June Old Style)", "precision": "day", "title": "Bafra surrounded and the men seized; Dilmitzoglou's account of the killings at Elezli (Suludere)", "summary": "On the morning of Saturday 5/18 June 1921 troops and armed local Turks surrounded Bafra and demanded the surrender of the men. According to the account of the Bafra notable Ioannis Dilmitzoglou, written in Turkish in Greek characters and printed in translation in the Black Book, about 535 men were tied back to back and taken to the church at Suludere near the village of Elezli; the seven priests were killed with an axe at the church door, the rest shot, bayoneted or axed, and Nicolas Jordanoglou paid his last 300 Turkish lira to be shot. Four wounded men escaped to Mount Nebyan. The Patriarchate reported that the Greek notables had first been invited to a dinner at the house of one Efrem Aga and murdered there.", "people": ["Ioannis (John) Dilmitzoglou", "Zemil Bey (under-governor of Bafra, as named by the witness)", "Nicolas Jordanoglou", "the priest Papayanni"], "sources_primary": ["Central Council of Pontus, Black Book: The Tragedy of Pontus 1914-1922 (Athens 1922), sect. IV 'The Tragedy of Baffra in Pontus, by an eye-witness... translated from the Turkish text written in Greek characters', signed John Dilmitoglou, Athens 13 Oct 1921", "The same text in the Toynbee Papers, Bodleian MS Toynbee 50 (as cited by Morris & Ze'evi, n. 182, 186)", "Greek Patriarchate, 'Bulletin', 31 Aug 1921 (dinner at Efrem Aga's)", "Enclosure in Lindley's despatch no. 286, 3 June 1922, FO 371/7879 ('June 5')", "Fotiadis, K. E., The Genocide of the Pontian Greeks (Thessaloniki: Stamoulis 2015; English ed., also cited as 2019), section 'Kemalist deportations from the kaza Bafra', as reproduced on the Virtual Genocide Memorial (VGM) Bafra page (no page numbers)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 408", "Virtual Genocide Memorial, 'Kaza Bafra / Πάφρα' (last modified 22 Aug 2026), read via the site's REST API"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The witness writes '5th/18th of June 1921, Saturday morning', and 18 June 1921 (New Style) was a Saturday; Morris & Ze'evi's 'June 5', after a British enclosure, is the Julian date. The same text opens with attacks on the villages 'on the 5th of July', a slip. Morris & Ze'evi's figures for 'Blezli', Selamelik and Kavdje-son (p. 408, n. 186) come from this same text, so the numbers rest on one Greek witness. Kyriakidis (p. 61, after Shenk) describes Bafra deportees of October 1921 shot, burned and knifed in three groups, which looks like a misdated echo of the same account. 'Soulou Dere' may be the Suludere rural district of Bafra (Nişanyan); Elezli is not in Nişanyan.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Dilmitzoglou's account (Black Book; Toynbee Papers; Fotiadis); Patriarchate bulletin", "Neutral sources: Morris & Ze'evi (repeating the same account); British FO enclosure"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "town surrounded, men seized"}, {"name": "Elezli / Suludere (BB 'Soulou Dere of the village of Elezli'; M&Z 'Blezli')", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "killing site; village not located, plotted at Bafra (district level)"}]}, {"id": "B1-011", "date": "1921-06-26", "date_end": "1921-08-16", "date_text": "26 June – 15/16 August 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "The Samsun–Bafra convoys shot on the road near Kavak", "summary": "The Black Book's notes list men's convoys from the Samsun–Bafra district of about 2,000, 1,901, 2,000, 500 and 620; on a descent near Kavak the escort fired on them, killing 330 of the first convoy and 660 of the third, and the survivors were sent on stripped 'to Malatia, Harpout, Mamouret-oul-Aziz, and Alpistan'. A deportee of the third Samsun convoy wrote that '660 were killed' as the column descended a mountain; Cosmos Lilidas said his convoy, which left Samsun around 20 June, was fired on near 'Djinbush', '292 remaining out of the thousand'. Stanley Hopkins of the NER reported 550 Greeks executed at Kavak on 26 June and 1,300 on 15 August; Rendel's Foreign Office memorandum has 1,300 shot near Kavak on 15 or 16 August 'in about two and a half hours'. The League of Nations circulated a Greek figure of at least 900.", "people": ["Topal Osman", "Stanley E. Hopkins", "Cosmos Lilidas"], "sources_primary": ["Central Council of Pontus, Black Book: The Tragedy of Pontus 1914-1922 (Athens 1922), sect. III 'A few short notes of the Turkish cruelties... June, July and August 1921' (Athens, 15/17 Oct 1921) and supplementary notes (Athens, 25 Sept 1921), 'District Samsoun-Baffra'", "Patriarchate summary of a letter from a Samsun deportee on Ayou-tepe; A.G.S. minutes 14 Sept 1921, FO 371/6548 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 282)", "USS Sands diary, 30 Nov 1921 (Lilidas; via Morris & Ze'evi n. 283)", "Hopkins trip reports, Sept–Oct 1921 (via Kyriakidis)", "Rendel, 'Turkish Massacres and Persecutions of Minorities since the Armistice', 20 Mar 1922, FO 371/7876, p. 5", "League of Nations communiqué, 14 Nov 1921, FO 371/6536"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 408, 423", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), p. 74", "Kyriakidis, 'Testimonies of American Charitable and Missionary Organizations', in Akçam, Kyriakidis et al. (eds), The Genocide of the Christian Populations in the Ottoman Empire, p. 52", "Yerasimos via Biber, ch. 13 in the TÜBA history series, p. 437 (330-701 for the first convoy)", "Kévorkian, Parachever un génocide: Mustafa Kemal et l'élimination des rescapés arméniens et grecs (e-text without page numbers; chapter on the vilayet of Trebizond and section 'Les déportations des populations grecques')", "Kévorkian, Parachever un génocide (Odile Jacob 2023), part III ch. 3, nn. 100, 103-104 (APC/PAJ, BIP, Ձ 374, August 1921; Ժ 473)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "330 and 660 make 990, consistent with 'at least 900'; Hopkins' 550 (26 June) and 1,300 (15 August) are separate killings; Kyriakidis also cites naval logbooks with 660 and 530 killed in two convoys. Kévorkian's 6,500 of 7,000 conscripts killed on the Samsun–Kavak road on 2 August, with the last 500 sent to Malatya, come from an Armenian Patriarchate Information Bureau report of August 1921 (Kévorkian, part III, ch. 3, n. 100); we found no second source for them. Kévorkian also gives the Samsun–Bafra convoys from another Patriarchate report (nn. 103–104), with 3,000 in the fourth convoy and 660 dead from the second. Ankara said the Kavak dead fell in battle (H6-T08).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Black Book; Patriarchate; Greek League delegate", "Neutral sources: Hopkins (NER); Rendel (FO); USS Sands; Yerasimos; Kévorkian", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Biber (transmitter)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "origin"}, {"name": "Kavak", "lat": 41.079, "lon": 36.044, "role": "killing site on the descent towards Kavak"}, {"name": "Çakallı (Samsun–Kavak road)", "lat": 41.21, "lon": 36.16, "role": "Black Book: women and children burned in houses at 'Tzakaly', four hours from Samsun (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "B1-012", "date": "1921-07-02", "date_end": "1921-07-19", "date_text": "2–19 July 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "Orders extend the deportations to all adult Christian men, then to all Ottoman Greeks", "summary": "An Armenian report of 18 July 1921 refers to a 2 July order from Ankara for the deportation of all 'adult male' Christians 'throughout the interior of Anatolia'; another report indicates that two weeks later Ankara ordered the 'immediate deportation of all Ottoman Greeks'. Ankara denied that orders had been issued to deport women and children. A British cabinet paper (CAB 24/131) said that Greeks aged 15–55 were being moved to areas near Syria, Mesopotamia and Diyarbakır.", "people": ["Mark L. Bristol"], "sources_primary": ["Head of the Armenian Protestant Community to Bristol, 18 July 1921; Bristol to SecState, 18 July 1921; Bristol to Izzet Pasha, 19 July 1921; Bristol to SecState, 2 Aug 1921 (Ankara's denial); all USNA RG 84, vol. 438 (via Morris & Ze'evi, nn. 176-177)", "UK National Archives CAB 24/131 (via Biber, p. 436, n. 77)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 407", "Biber, ch. 13 in the TÜBA history series, p. 436, n. 77"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "No text of the 2 July order has been found.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Armenian sources: Armenian Protestant community report", "Neutral sources: US High Commission; British cabinet paper", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Biber (transmitter)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Diyarbakır", "lat": 37.914, "lon": 40.23, "role": "destination region named by the British paper"}, {"name": "Aleppo", "lat": 36.202, "lon": 37.134, "role": "'areas near Syria' (direction only)"}]}, {"id": "B1-013", "date": "1921-07-04", "date_end": "1921-07-26", "date_text": "4–26 July 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "Near East Relief workers meet the first convoys between Samsun, Amasya and Sivas", "summary": "Wilford W. Fuller of the Near East Relief, leaving Sivas on 4 July 1921, met five convoys of Greek men deported from Samsun; of the first two, of 900, only 200 reached Sivas, naked and robbed, saying the others had been killed at Kavak. On 26 July he met about 3,000 women and children driven inland by gendarmes on the road between Amasya and Merzifon. On 8 July the US destroyer Brooks reported that in the Bafra area 'as many as 90 percent of deported Greeks have been killed'.", "people": ["Wilford W. Fuller"], "sources_primary": ["Fuller, as reported in Rendel's memorandum (FO 371/7876), p. 6 (via Hofmann), and in Heideman (ed.), Black Sea: A Naval Officer's Near East Experience (via Çetinoğlu)", "USS Brooks to Bristol, 8 July 1921, USNA RG 84, vol. 438 (via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 200)"], "sources_modern": ["Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), p. 78", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 409", "Çetinoğlu, 'TC'nin soykırımlar geleneği ve Pontos soykırımı' (Turkish writer), p. 16"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The '90 percent' report is dated 8 July 1921, so it concerns the men's convoys of June, not the women and children deported from late August. It is not by Yowell or Ward (NER at Harput). The officer could not leave Samsun freely; the figure may count the missing as dead.", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: NER (Fuller) via Rendel; US Navy (USS Brooks)", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Çetinoğlu (transmitter of Fuller's July account; flagged)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sivas", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.015, "role": "convoys arriving"}, {"name": "Amasya", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "women and children on the road, 26 July"}, {"name": "Merzifon", "lat": 40.873, "lon": 35.463, "role": "women and children on the road, 26 July"}, {"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "area of the '90 percent' estimate"}]}, {"id": "H7-060", "date": "1921-07-18", "date_end": "1921-10-15", "date_text": "18 July - 15 October 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "Western protests to Ankara over the Pontic deportations and executions", "summary": "On 18 July 1921 the US High Commissioner Bristol telegraphed Mustafa Kemal asking him to prevent the expulsion of women, children and the old from Samsun; Yusuf Kemal replied on 21 July. On 22 September the French, British and Italian representatives (Fourcade, Rumbold and Garroni) telegraphed the Ankara government their concern about the relocation; these texts, with Yusuf Kemal's note of 15 September to the Entente representatives, are reproduced in Ankara's own book Pontus Meselesi (1922) (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 21). In October Rumbold and the other Allied High Commissioners sent Ankara a protest telegram on the forced migration and the executions in the eastern Black Sea region (Rumbold to Curzon, 15 October 1921, via Çetinoğlu).", "people": ["Mark L. Bristol", "Horace Rumbold", "Yusuf Kemal (Tengirşenk)"], "sources_primary": ["[Ankara government publication] Pontus Meselesi (TBMM Hükümeti Matbuat Müdüriyet-i Umumisi, 1922; facsimile ed. Y. Kurt, 1995), final chapter (Bristol's telegram of 18 July 1921; Yusuf Kemal's reply of 21 July; note of 15 Sept; joint telegram of 22 Sept 1921), as described by Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), p. 21", "[British] Rumbold to Curzon, 15 Oct 1921, Documents on British Foreign Policy, via Çetinoğlu, 'Pontos'taki Düzmece İstiklal Mahkemeleri ve Yargılamaları' (posted 7 Jan 2020; reprinted in Çilingir 2016, pp. 229ff.) [Turkish writer], p. 9", "[US] Bristol to SecState, 18 July 1921; Bristol to Izzet Pasha, 19 July 1921 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 177)"], "sources_modern": ["Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), p. 21", "Çetinoğlu, 'Pontos'taki Düzmece İstiklal Mahkemeleri ve Yargılamaları' (posted 7 Jan 2020; reprinted in Çilingir 2016, pp. 229ff.) [Turkish writer], p. 9", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), pp. 407, 416"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Rumbold also told the Patriarchal vicar (6 Oct 1921) that both sides were sending massacre allegations and that only peace would end them (Çetinoğlu, after Bilgiç).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: US, British, French and Italian diplomatic acts", "Ottoman state records: Ankara's 1922 publication (as the record)", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Korucu & Dağlıoğlu; Çetinoğlu (transmitters; flagged)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "High Commissions"}, {"name": "Ankara (Angora)", "lat": 39.933, "lon": 32.86, "role": "recipient"}]}, {"id": "H7-027", "date": "1921-07-23", "date_end": "1921-08-01", "date_text": "From 23-24 July 1921, about eight to nine days", "precision": "day", "title": "Merzifon: Topal Osman's men and local Turks kill Christians over a week", "summary": "From 23-24 July 1921 Topal Osman's irregulars spent four days pillaging and killing in Merzifon, followed by four or five days of violence by locals assisted by gendarmes and troops. Men and boys sheltering in the French school were taken out and shot and girls abducted, and the school was torched; about 400 Armenian houses were burned, while the Greek quarter was left untouched (Morris & Ze'evi, pp. 411-413). Before the massacre the town had 2,000-2,500 Christians; 'almost all the men were killed', and the remaining Greeks were deported (p. 413). Estimates of the dead: 'more than a thousand' (p. 411); 950 (the Armenian-Greek Section of the British High Commission) and about 1,200 (Bristol and the missionary Gertrude Anthony) (p. 413, n. 223); 'maybe 1,000 or 1,500', though 'no one could say for sure' (Anthony, via Shenk 2017, p. 203). In May 1922 only 14 Armenian and Greek men were left in the town (Theda Phelps, ibid.).", "people": ["Topal Osman Ağa", "Yusuf Kemal (Tengirşenk)", "George E. White (Anatolia College)"], "sources_primary": ["[US missionary] D. M. Hosford, 'Brief Memorandum of Recent Conditions and Events in Northern Anatolia, particularly in Marsovan', 6 Dec 1921, FO 371/6538 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 223)", "[US missionary] Gertrude Anthony to Bristol, 1 Nov 1921, USNA RG 84 vol. 438 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 223 and Shenk 2017)", "[British] A.G.S., 'Deportations and Massacre on Black Sea Littoral, and Massacre at Marsovan', FO 371/6534 (950 killed; via Morris & Ze'evi n. 223)", "[US] Bristol Papers, War Diary, entry for 1 Nov 1921 (about 1,200; via Morris & Ze'evi n. 223)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), pp. 411-413", "Shenk, 'Ethnic Cleansing, American Women, and the Admiral', in Shirinian (ed.), Genocide in the Ottoman Empire: Armenians, Assyrians, and Greeks, 1913-1923 (Berghahn, 2017), p. 203", "Kyriakidis, in Akçam, Kyriakidis & Chatzikyriakidis (eds), The Genocide of the Christian Populations in the Ottoman Empire and Its Aftermath (1908-1923) (Routledge, 2023), ch. 2 (American missionary testimonies)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The toll is an estimate, 950–1,500 according to the source; no count exists. Ankara's denial ('legend', p. 413) is under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H7-T06).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: American missionaries (Hosford, Anthony, Phelps); Bristol; British A.G.S.; Morris & Ze'evi; Shenk", "Greek sources: Kyriakidis 2023 (historian)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Merzifon", "lat": 40.874, "lon": 35.463, "role": "massacre"}]}, {"id": "H7-028", "date": "1921-08", "date_text": "August 1921", "precision": "month", "title": "Two refugees' account: Trabzon's Greek men summoned for 'fortification work', then deported and shot", "summary": "Two refugees from the Pontus told the British that in August 1921 the Trabzon authorities ordered all adult Greek men to report for paid labour on fortifications; 500 or more who did were imprisoned and their homes pillaged, then deported in groups of fifty, marched for seven days 'without as much as a morsel of bread', and one night taken away in groups of ten and shot (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 423).", "sources_primary": ["[Greek refugees, British file] 'Report Made from Information Given by John Eufremides and George Isaridi, Refugees from Pontus, November 1921', FO 371/7876 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 284)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 423"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Two witnesses, recorded by the British; no other source for this episode was found.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: two Pontic refugees' testimony", "Neutral sources: British file; Morris & Ze'evi"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon (Trapezous)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "men detained and deported"}]}, {"id": "B1-014", "date": "1921-08-03", "date_end": "1921-08-08", "date_text": "3–8 August 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "The mutasarrıf of Bafra: all remaining Greeks, women and children included, to be deported", "summary": "On 3 August 1921 the commander of USS Overton recorded that the mutasarrıf of Bafra had told him that the deportation 'of all remaining Greeks, including women and children, had been ordered by Angora', reinforced by Nureddin Pasha's instruction to proceed 'with all dispatch' or cease to be mutasarrıf; the officer judged it 'part of an official plan which contemplates extermination of the Greeks'. On 8 August the women and girls of Bafra were collected, 'stripped and violated', many forced to convert; those who refused were led off 'to different unknown places'. Dilmitzoglou describes groups of 1,000–1,500 women and children held for days without food in the yard of the Bafra church and then driven towards Kastamonu. Only the sick who paid bribes were allowed to stay (USS Williamson).", "people": ["Nureddin Pasha", "Mark L. Bristol"], "sources_primary": ["USS Overton, 'Samsun Diary', 3 Aug 1921, USNA RG 84, vol. 440 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 178)", "Enclosure in Lindley's despatch no. 286, 3 June 1922, FO 371/7879 (8 August)", "CO USS Williamson to Bristol, 28 Sept 1921, RG 84, vol. 440", "Central Council of Pontus, Black Book: The Tragedy of Pontus 1914-1922 (Athens 1922), sect. IV 'The Tragedy of Baffra in Pontus, by an eye-witness... translated from the Turkish text written in Greek characters', signed John Dilmitoglou, Athens 13 Oct 1921"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 407-408"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Ankara told Bristol (2 Aug 1921) and Bristol's aide that it had not ordered the deportation of women (Morris & Ze'evi pp. 407, 415); its later admissions are in B1-022. The Kastamonu direction is in the Greek account only and may be a separate early drive west.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: US Navy (Overton, Williamson); British FO enclosure", "Ottoman state records: the mutasarrıf's statement and Nureddin's order (admissions, as reported by a US officer)", "Greek sources: Dilmitzoglou"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "women and children held in the church yard"}, {"name": "Kastamonu", "lat": 41.376, "lon": 33.776, "role": "direction in which women were driven (Dilmitzoglou)"}]}, {"id": "H7-061", "date": "1921-08-18", "date_text": "18 August 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "Pope Benedict XV asks Kemal to cancel the exile of 5,000 women and children near Samsun", "summary": "After appeals from the president of the Pontian Association, Dr Thoidis, the Pope had the Apostolic Delegate, Mgr Angelo Dolci, telegraph Mustafa Kemal on 18 August 1921 asking for the cancellation of the order to exile 5,000 women and children from the areas near Samsun. In March 1921 the Pope had already asked Kemal, through Cardinal Gasparri, to protect the lives and property of Christians; Kemal replied that Christians under the Grand National Assembly were calm and accused Greeks and Armenians of crimes (Kyriakidis 2026, pp. 76-79). On 24 July 1921 Dolci wrote that the Delegation was receiving reports of massacres from all the Christian communities and that 'Christians kept being murdered' (p. 78).", "people": ["Benedict XV", "Angelo Maria Dolci", "Mustafa Kemal"], "sources_primary": ["[Vatican] ASV, Arch. Deleg. Turchia, Busta I.F., Amministrazione Mgr Dolci, Telegrammi 1921: Dolci to Mustafa Kemal, 18 Aug 1921 (via Kyriakidis 2026, p. 79 n. 131)", "[Vatican] Propaganda Fide, Nuova Serie vol. 692, Dolci to Van Rossum, 24 July and 18 Aug 1921 (via Kyriakidis 2026, p. 78)"], "sources_modern": ["Kyriakidis (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias Press, 2026), pp. 76-79"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The papal telegram is a neutral document; the number 5,000 came from the Pontic appeals.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Vatican diplomatic correspondence (Dolci)", "Greek sources: the Pontian Association's appeal (source of the 5,000)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "women and children threatened with exile"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "Apostolic Delegation"}]}, {"id": "B1-015", "date": "1921-08-31", "date_end": "1921-09-08", "date_text": "31 August – 8 September 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "About 6,000 women and children deported from Bafra", "summary": "Some 6,000 Greek women and children were deported from Bafra around 31 August 1921 (USS McFarland, 1 Sept). The McFarland's war diaries of 31 August – 8 September record 1,500 women and children of nearby villages, who had been living in Samsun, being deported, and say that, according to the American tobacco men, the deportation of 'some six thousand women and children from the Bafra district was well underway'.", "sources_primary": ["CO USS McFarland to Bristol, 1 Sept 1921; Bristol to SecState, 1 Sept 1921 (RG 84, vol. 438)", "USS McFarland war diaries, 31 Aug and 2, 3, 5, 8 Sept 1921, NA RG 45, box 832"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 408", "Shenk, 'Ethnic Cleansing, American Women, and the Admiral', in Shirinian (ed.), Genocide in the Ottoman Empire: Armenians, Assyrians, and Greeks, 1913-1923 (Berghahn 2017), pp. 200-201", "Kyriakidis, 'Testimonies of American Charitable and Missionary Organizations', in Akçam, Kyriakidis et al. (eds), The Genocide of the Christian Populations in the Ottoman Empire, p. 51"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The McFarland's Bafra figure was hearsay from the tobacco company men at Samsun. Route not given.", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: US naval reports"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "origin"}, {"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "1,500 village women and children deported"}]}, {"id": "B1-017", "date": "1921-09-16", "date_end": "1921-11-25", "date_text": "16 September – c. 25 November 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "Fotiadis: the convoy of 16 September, 4,120 deported, 1,270 reach Malatya", "summary": "According to Fotiadis, 4,120 people, among them 150 orphans from the American orphanage, were deported from Bafra on 16 September 1921 and walked for 70 days by way of Hacıhamza, Osmancık, Çorum, Mecitözü, Turhal, Tokat, Sivas, Tecer Han, Deliktaş, Kangal and Hekimhan to Malatya. 130 of the orphans and 2,700 women, children and old people died of cold, hunger and exhaustion; at Sivas the Americans took the 20 surviving orphans; 1,270 reached Malatya, where most died in a typhus epidemic (4,120 − 2,700 − 130 − 20 = 1,270). American sources confirm a convoy of this size on this road: a party of Americans met a column of 4,000 Bafra women and children near Sivas, with about seventy old men, 'little clothing, many... without shoes and apparently... no food' (USS McFarland, October 1921); a traveller reported to the British a convoy of about 4,000 Greeks deported from Samsun to Tokat (9 Nov 1921); and Theda Phelps at Sivas met a Bafra woman who had made her two children walk the snowy mountain paths because 'the children who were being carried were all freezing to death'.", "people": ["Theda Phelps"], "sources_primary": ["Fotiadis, K. E., The Genocide of the Pontian Greeks (Thessaloniki: Stamoulis 2015; English ed., also cited as 2019), section 'Kemalist deportations from the kaza Bafra', as reproduced on the Virtual Genocide Memorial (VGM) Bafra page (no page numbers); Fotiadis introduces Dilmitzoglou's account before these figures, but see 'disputed'", "CO USS McFarland to Bristol, ? Oct 1921, RG 84, vol. 440 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 193)", "A.G.S. minutes, 9 Nov 1921 (Yeghiayan, p. 234, via Hofmann)", "Phelps to the State Department, 2 June 1922, NA 867.4016/582"], "sources_modern": ["Virtual Genocide Memorial, 'Kaza Bafra / Πάφρα' (last modified 22 Aug 2026), read via the site's REST API", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 409, 421", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), p. 77", "Shenk, 'Ethnic Cleansing, American Women, and the Admiral', in Shirinian (ed.), Genocide in the Ottoman Empire: Armenians, Assyrians, and Greeks, 1913-1923 (Berghahn 2017), pp. 203-204"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The numbers 4,120, 1,270, 2,700 and 1,900 are not in Dilmitzoglou's account as printed in the Black Book, which was signed on 13 October 1921, before the convoy could have reached Malatya, so Fotiadis took them from a source he does not name; Antonios Gavriilidis, a Bafra deportee to Malatya who wrote in 1924, may be that source. The US reports have about 6,000 deported on 31 August and 2,500 on 19 September; Fotiadis' 16 and 24 September may be other convoys, or the same ones dated differently (in the Julian calendar they would be 29 September and 7 October). The McFarland's '4,000 near Sivas' fits the 4,120 best. The convoys should not be added up. Some stages of the route have not been located: 'Turtmen Tayi' and 'Batman Tayi' (mountains), 'Kaynarhan', 'Cenkel' and 'Tuzla' (south of Sivas).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Fotiadis (figures and route)", "Neutral sources: USS McFarland; A.G.S. traveller's report; Phelps (corroboration of a convoy of about 4,000 on this road)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "waypoint 1: departure, 16 Sept 1921"}, {"name": "Hacıhamza", "lat": 41.06, "lon": 34.61, "role": "waypoint 4 (approximate position)"}, {"name": "Osmancık", "lat": 40.975, "lon": 34.807, "role": "waypoint 5"}, {"name": "Çorum", "lat": 40.55, "lon": 34.955, "role": "waypoint 6"}, {"name": "Mecitözü", "lat": 40.52, "lon": 35.295, "role": "waypoint 7"}, {"name": "Ezinepazar", "lat": 40.75, "lon": 36.03, "role": "waypoint 9: 'Inebazar' (identification with Ezinepazar uncertain; approximate)"}, {"name": "Turhal", "lat": 40.388, "lon": 36.081, "role": "waypoint 11"}, {"name": "Tokat", "lat": 40.314, "lon": 36.554, "role": "waypoint 12"}, {"name": "Sivas", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.015, "role": "waypoint 13: 20 surviving orphans taken into the American orphanage; Americans met a column of 4,000 Bafra women and children near Sivas (USS McFarland, Oct 1921)"}, {"name": "Tecer (Tecer Han)", "lat": 39.44, "lon": 37.21, "role": "waypoint 15: 'Tecirhan' (approximate)"}, {"name": "Deliktaş", "lat": 39.4, "lon": 37.32, "role": "waypoint 16 (approximate)"}, {"name": "Kangal", "lat": 39.234, "lon": 37.39, "role": "waypoint 17"}, {"name": "Hekimhan", "lat": 38.816, "lon": 37.933, "role": "waypoint 18"}, {"name": "Malatya", "lat": 38.355, "lon": 38.309, "role": "waypoint 19: end, after 70 days (about 25 Nov 1921); 1,270 arrive"}]}, {"id": "B1-018", "date": "1921-09-19", "date_end": "1921-09-28", "date_text": "19–28 September 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "2,500 more deported from Bafra; local Muslim dissent", "summary": "A further 2,500 Greek women and children were deported from Bafra on 19 September 1921 (USS Sturtevant, 20 Sept). The Sturtevant's commander, Lt Cdr E. G. Haas, noted some local Muslim opposition to the Bafra deportations and wrote that he could understand the deportation of adult men 'as an inevitable consequence of the war', but that 'to treat poor women and helpless children... in such a cruel and inhumane manner is an... unpardonable sin against civilization'. A Turkish notable told an American businessman that 'what had happened made him ashamed to be a Turk'.", "people": ["E. G. Haas"], "sources_primary": ["CO USS Sturtevant to Bristol, 20 Sept 1921 (RG 84, vol. 438)", "Haas, USS Sturtevant, diary entry 21 Sept 1921 (RG 84, vol. 440)", "CO USS Williamson to Bristol, 28 Sept 1921"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 408-409"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "May be the same convoy as Fotiadis's 4,120 of 16 September, counted differently.", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: US naval reports"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "origin"}]}, {"id": "B1-019", "date": "1921-09-24", "date_end": "1921-12-03", "date_text": "24 September – c. 3 December 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "Fotiadis: the 'eighth convoy', 1,900 deported, 800 reach Hekimhan", "summary": "Fotiadis writes that the eighth convoy, of 1,900 people, left Bafra on 24 September 1921. Between Deliktaş and Kangal 310 froze to death and 790 died of disease and exhaustion; after a 70-day march 800 reached Hekimhan. About 400, the most exhausted, were left there, where almost all died; the rest were sent on to Malatya and Harput, where almost all of them died too.", "sources_primary": ["Fotiadis, K. E., The Genocide of the Pontian Greeks (Thessaloniki: Stamoulis 2015; English ed., also cited as 2019), section 'Kemalist deportations from the kaza Bafra', as reproduced on the Virtual Genocide Memorial (VGM) Bafra page (no page numbers)"], "sources_modern": ["Virtual Genocide Memorial, 'Kaza Bafra / Πάφρα' (last modified 22 Aug 2026), read via the site's REST API"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "We found no independent report of this convoy, and Fotiadis does not name his source (see B1-017).", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Fotiadis"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "waypoint 1: departure, 24 Sept 1921"}, {"name": "Sivas", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.015, "role": "waypoint 2 (same road as the 16 Sept convoy)"}, {"name": "Deliktaş", "lat": 39.4, "lon": 37.32, "role": "waypoint 3: between Deliktaş and Kangal 310 froze and 790 died (approximate)"}, {"name": "Kangal", "lat": 39.234, "lon": 37.39, "role": "waypoint 4"}, {"name": "Hekimhan", "lat": 38.816, "lon": 37.933, "role": "waypoint 5: 800 arrive after 70 days; about 400 left here, most died"}, {"name": "Malatya", "lat": 38.355, "lon": 38.309, "role": "waypoint 6: rest sent on"}, {"name": "Harput", "lat": 38.706, "lon": 39.256, "role": "waypoint 7: rest sent on; 'almost all eventually succumbed'"}]}, {"id": "B1-020", "date": "1921-09-25", "date_text": "Report of 25 September 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "The Black Book: the last 1,600 women and children of Alaçam driven inland via Boyabat towards Osmancık", "summary": "The Black Book's supplementary notes (Athens, 25 September 1921) say the remaining 1,600 women and children of Alaçam were deported inland; they had reached Boyabat ('Boivat'), 127 km from Alaçam, most having died on the way of illness and ill-treatment, and their destination was Osmancık. The same notes report 17 orphans of the Bafra orphanage dead of starvation and left unburied for days.", "sources_primary": ["Central Council of Pontus, Black Book: The Tragedy of Pontus 1914-1922 (Athens 1922), sect. III 'A few short notes of the Turkish cruelties... June, July and August 1921' (Athens, 15/17 Oct 1921) and supplementary notes (Athens, 25 Sept 1921) (supplementary notes; p. 22 according to Karas & Tsilfidis)"], "sources_modern": ["Karas & Tsilfidis, 'List and map of deportations', greek-genocide.net (2022)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Greek source only. The Black Book's '120 km from Angora' for Osmancık is wrong (about 200 km).", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Black Book"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Alaçam", "lat": 41.608, "lon": 35.598, "role": "waypoint 1: departure"}, {"name": "Boyabat", "lat": 41.468, "lon": 34.767, "role": "waypoint 2: reached by 25 Sept 1921"}, {"name": "Osmancık", "lat": 40.975, "lon": 34.807, "role": "waypoint 3: stated destination"}, {"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "orphanage: 17 orphans dead of hunger"}]}, {"id": "H7-031", "date": "1921-10", "date_end": "1921-11-03", "date_text": "October to 3 November 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "The Grand National Assembly debates Nureddin's conduct; he is removed", "summary": "Deputies in the Grand National Assembly at Ankara attacked Nureddin's conduct in Koçgiri and the Pontus. On 5 October 1921 the Lazistan deputies Ziya Hurşit and Osman Bey brought an interpellation on 'banditry in Samsun', and on 29 October, in secret session, they accused Nureddin of having ordered the deportation of Greek women and children beyond the scope of the relocation order, of killings in the middle of Samsun in front of Americans and other foreigners, of attacks on the first convoy six hours out of Samsun, and of putting under town arrest the 56 notables of Samsun, among them its mayor and mufti, who had appealed to Kemal against the deportation of women and children; they said that his use of irregular bands had driven Greeks to the mountains. The Trabzon deputy Hafız Mehmet said he had seen a battalion of the Giresun regiment loot and burn villages under the pretext of collecting arms. Nureddin was relieved of command on 3 November 1921 on charges including the use of irregular forces in Koçgiri and Samsun, accepting a bribe from a Pontic Greek, ordering public pillage during the relocation, and harming Muslims by driving the Pontic Greeks to the mountains; Mustafa Kemal blocked his trial, and he later commanded the First Army in 1922.", "people": ["Nureddin Pasha", "Mustafa Kemal"], "sources_primary": ["[Turkish parliamentary] TBMM Gizli Celse Zabıtları, vol. 2 (Türkiye İş Bankası Kültür Yayınları, 1985), 5 Oct 1921, p. 280, and 29 Oct 1921, pp. 403-409; 16-17 Jan 1922, pp. 622, 627 (via Çilingir pp. 118-120 and Korucu & Dağlıoğlu pp. 13-14, 22-23)", "[Turkish documents] Nureddin's dismissal and defence, via Balcıoğlu, İki İsyan (2003), pp. 273-283"], "sources_modern": ["Mango, Atatürk (1999), p. 330", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), pp. 13-14, 22-23", "Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], pp. 118-120 (verbatim extracts of the 29 Oct 1921 session)", "Morris & Ze'evi 2019, p. 404 ('Kemal knew how to get the worst out of his subordinates'); p. 406 (Nureddin charged for 'the mal-execution of his orders', as told to US diplomats); p. 415 (Yusuf Kemal: 'local officials' exaggerated zeal') [international scholars, Israel]", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019, p. 27 (Nureddin 'one of the primary perpetrators') [Turkish, critical scholarship]", "Hofmann 2011, p. 76 (no action against Osman after Merzifon) [international scholar, Germany]"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The debate concerned both Koçgiri (where Ziya Hurşit said many Muslim villages had been burned) and the Pontus; the Samsun deportations, the women and children and the 56 notables were central to it (Çilingir's extracts; Korucu & Dağlıoğlu). The deputies also blamed Nureddin for driving Greeks to the mountains and some claimed Muslim villages had been burned: those claims are under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H7-T08). Morris & Ze'evi write that 'Kemal knew how to get the worst out of his subordinates' (p. 404), that Nureddin was charged with 'the mal-execution of his orders', as US diplomats were told (p. 406), and quote Yusuf Kemal on 'local officials' exaggerated zeal' (p. 415); Korucu & Dağlıoğlu (p. 27) call Nureddin 'one of the primary perpetrators'; Hofmann (2011, p. 76) notes that no action was taken against Osman after Merzifon.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: TBMM Gizli Celse Zabıtları, vol. 2 (the record); Nureddin's dismissal (Balcıoğlu)", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Çilingir 2016; Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019 (transmitters; flagged)", "Neutral sources: Mango 1999"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Ankara (Angora)", "lat": 39.933, "lon": 32.86, "role": "Grand National Assembly"}]}, {"id": "B1-021", "date": "1921-10-13", "date_text": "13 October 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "The Bafra witness writes in Turkish: the Turkish-speaking Orthodox of Bafra", "summary": "The fullest account of the Bafra killings, by the notable Ioannis Dilmitzoglou, was written 'in the Turkish text written in Greek characters' (Karamanlidika) and translated for the Black Book (signed Athens, 13 October 1921). The Orthodox of Bafra town were Turkish-speaking: the 1911 Greek consular census marks them so, and the village list of the Centre for Asia Minor Studies says that 'the native Greeks and the Cappadocian Greeks only spoke Turkish', while settlers from elsewhere in the Pontus spoke Pontic. No source we have found treats them differently from Pontic-speakers in 1921: their men were killed and their women and children deported like the rest.", "people": ["Ioannis (John) Dilmitzoglou"], "sources_primary": ["Central Council of Pontus, Black Book: The Tragedy of Pontus 1914-1922 (Athens 1922), sect. IV 'The Tragedy of Baffra in Pontus, by an eye-witness... translated from the Turkish text written in Greek characters', signed John Dilmitoglou, Athens 13 Oct 1921 (heading)", "Greek consular census 1911 (Kitromilides & Alexandris 1984-85, Table VI, row 243, by ditto mark)"], "sources_modern": ["pontosworld.com, '12. Πάφρα - Bafra' (KMS list after Samouilidis 1992)", "Virtual Genocide Memorial, 'Kaza Bafra / Πάφρα' (last modified 22 Aug 2026), read via the site's REST API"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The census entry is Kitromilides & Alexandris 1984–85, Table VI, p. 41, row 243, 'Bafra' (2,822 inhabitants), where the language column carries a ditto mark of the 'Turcophone' printed at row 237. Some deportees were forced to convert (B1-014), but no source shows that speaking Turkish spared anyone.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Black Book; Greek consular census; KMS list"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "Turkish-speaking Orthodox community"}, {"name": "Athens", "lat": 37.984, "lon": 23.728, "role": "where the account was signed and translated"}]}, {"id": "H7-032", "date": "1921-10-22", "date_text": "October 1921 (Rumbold's covering note of 22 October)", "precision": "day", "title": "The British High Commission's Armenian-Greek Section: 420 of 720 Greek villages of the Pontus destroyed (October 1921)", "summary": "A report of the Armenian-Greek Section of the British High Commission at Constantinople, 'Deportations and Massacre on Black Sea Littoral, and Massacre at Marsovan' (covering note from Rumbold to Curzon, 22 October 1921), stated that of the 720 majority-Greek villages in the Pontus, 420 had been 'burnt or destroyed, the men killed or deported, and the women removed', and the other 300 partially destroyed, with the men deported (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 416, n. 247). Earlier, in May 1921, US officers at Samsun had relayed a missionary's report that about 100 Greek villages south of Bafra were destroyed within a few days from 17 May (Morris & Ze'evi, pp. 405, 408; B1-001).", "sources_primary": ["[British] Armenian-Greek Section of the British High Commission, 'Deportations and Massacre on Black Sea Littoral, and Massacre at Marsovan', covering note Rumbold to Curzon, 22 Oct 1921, FO 371/6534 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 247)", "[US Navy] C. S. Joyce, Diary of USS Fox, Samsun, 12-28 May 1921; CO USS Overton to Bristol, diary for May 1921 (via Morris & Ze'evi nn. 165, 181, 183)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), pp. 405, 408, 416"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "The 'about 100 villages' is a missionary's estimate for the Bafra area in May 1921, not a count for the Samsun district in October.", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: British High Commission's A.G.S. report (drawing on Greek and Armenian information); US naval diaries; Morris & Ze'evi"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "district"}, {"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.906, "role": "about 100 villages south of the town destroyed, May 1921"}]}, {"id": "H7-062", "date": "1921-11", "date_end": "1922-05", "date_text": "From early November 1921", "precision": "month", "title": "Zonguldak: the Greek Orthodox men deported (Catholic missionary note)", "summary": "A note from the Catholic mission at Zonguldak, sent to the Apostolic Delegation, reports that from early November 1921 the Kemalist authorities deported the male Greek Orthodox population to distant provinces: about 750 men and youths, boys of 14 among them, leaving about 2,500 old men, women and small children without support; the missionaries asked that their authorship be kept secret (Kyriakidis 2026, Text 4, pp. 159-160).", "sources_primary": ["[Catholic missionary] Note, ASV, Arch. Deleg. Turchia, 115, fasc. 612, pp. 29r-30r (printed as Text 4 in Kyriakidis 2026)"], "sources_modern": ["Kyriakidis (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias Press, 2026), pp. 83, 159-160"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Zonguldak lies west of the Pontus proper. Kyriakidis's narrative (p. 83) says the note concerns 'the whole Orthodox Greek male population' without naming Zonguldak; the note itself is about Zonguldak.", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Catholic missionary note (Vatican archives)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Zonguldak", "lat": 41.456, "lon": 31.799, "role": "men deported from"}]}, {"id": "B1-022", "date": "1921-11", "date_end": "1922-02", "date_text": "Late 1921 – February 1922", "precision": "month", "title": "Nureddin and Yusuf Kemal admit the deportation of women and children; Bafra Turks armed", "summary": "In his defence against the Assembly's charges, Nureddin Pasha said that the women 'had helped their men in thought, body and property', that 'the Greeks in our country are a snake; the poison of these snakes is the women', and that 'therefore we did the same to them as to the men; we did not separate them from their children either'. He also said that local Turks had been organised in security units, with 600 armed men from Kavak and 1,000 from Bafra. In February 1922 the foreign minister Yusuf Kemal, who had earlier told the Americans that Ankara had not ordered the deportation of women, told a journalist that 'the military command... has judged it necessary to deport them'. These are admissions; the justifications are given under 'What the Turkish sources say' (B1-T03, H7-T14, B1-T08).", "people": ["Nureddin Pasha", "Yusuf Kemal (Tengirşenk)"], "sources_primary": ["Nureddin Pasha's statement of defence (in Balcıoğlu 2000, pp. 273-275)", "Yusuf Kemal to a journalist, Feb 1922 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 243); Dunn's conversation with Yusuf Kemal (Morris & Ze'evi nn. 241-242)"], "sources_modern": ["Balcıoğlu, İki İsyan: Pontos, Koçgiri; Bir Paşa: Nurettin Paşa (Ankara: Nobel 2000), pp. 273-275 (via Çilingir)", "Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Turkish-language work), p. 130", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 415"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The date of Nureddin's statement is not given (after his removal on 3 November 1921).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Nureddin's statement and Yusuf Kemal's (admissions)", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Balcıoğlu (transmitter); Çilingir (Turkish writer, transmitter)", "Neutral sources: Morris & Ze'evi"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "1,000 armed local Turks (Nureddin)"}, {"name": "Kavak", "lat": 41.079, "lon": 36.044, "role": "600 armed local Turks (Nureddin)"}]}, {"id": "B1-023", "date": "1921-11", "date_end": "1922", "date_text": "1921–1922", "precision": "year", "title": "Where the deportees were sent: the Central Army's list and the Greek testimonies", "summary": "The Central Army's records count 63,844 Greeks moved inland by November 1921, to Sivas, Tokat, Yozgat, Çorum, Şebinkarahisar, Elazığ, Ergani, Malatya and Maraş. Testimonies in the Exodus volumes of the Centre for Asia Minor Studies, listed by Karas & Tsilfidis, place deportees from Bafra, Samsun and Havza in Sivas in 1922 and deportees from Alaçam in Malatya (May 1921), and record two villages of the Havza–Vezirköprü area ('Hodjas', perhaps Koçaş, June 1921; 'Karatzoren', summer 1921) as deported 'to Aleppo'. Kévorkian notes that the destinations named by the Arapgir missionaries were all to the south, perhaps with a view to a final expulsion into the French or British mandates.", "sources_primary": ["Central Army records (via Balcıoğlu 1991, p. 109)", "KMS, Η Έξοδος vol. B (2004), p. 358; vol. C (2013), pp. 77, 99; vol. E (2016), p. 501 (via Karas & Tsilfidis)"], "sources_modern": ["Biber, ch. 13 in the TÜBA history series, p. 438, n. 97", "Karas & Tsilfidis, 'List and map of deportations', greek-genocide.net (2022)", "Kévorkian, Parachever un génocide: Mustafa Kemal et l'élimination des rescapés arméniens et grecs (e-text without page numbers; chapter on the vilayet of Trebizond and section 'Les déportations des populations grecques')"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "'To Aleppo' in a 1921 testimony may mean the southern road rather than arrival in French-mandate Syria. Kévorkian's reading of intent is an interpretation.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Central Army records (numbers moved: an admission)", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Biber; Balcıoğlu (transmitters)", "Greek sources: KMS testimonies", "Neutral sources: Kévorkian"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sivas", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.015, "role": "destination"}, {"name": "Tokat", "lat": 40.314, "lon": 36.554, "role": "destination"}, {"name": "Yozgat", "lat": 39.82, "lon": 34.808, "role": "destination"}, {"name": "Çorum", "lat": 40.55, "lon": 34.955, "role": "destination"}, {"name": "Şebinkarahisar", "lat": 40.288, "lon": 38.424, "role": "destination"}, {"name": "Mamuret-ul-Aziz (Elazığ)", "lat": 38.675, "lon": 39.222, "role": "destination"}, {"name": "Ergani", "lat": 38.268, "lon": 39.76, "role": "destination"}, {"name": "Malatya", "lat": 38.355, "lon": 38.309, "role": "destination"}, {"name": "Maraş", "lat": 37.585, "lon": 36.937, "role": "destination"}, {"name": "Koçaş (Gr. Hotzas)", "lat": 41.043, "lon": 35.487, "role": "origin of a deportation recorded as 'to Aleppo' (identification uncertain)"}, {"name": "Aleppo", "lat": 36.202, "lon": 37.134, "role": "destination named in two KMS testimonies"}]}, {"id": "H7-034", "date": "1922", "date_text": "1922", "precision": "year", "title": "Central Council of Pontus, Black Book: an 'Exterminated Population' of 303,238 by the end of 1921", "summary": "The bilingual (English and French) Black Book of the Central Council of Pontus (Athens, 1922) gives, in a table headed 'General description of the victims of Pontus according to ecclesiastical provinces and Greek communities', a column 'Exterminated Population' ('Population détruite'): Amaseia 134,078; Chaldia 64,582; Trebizond 38,434; Neocaesarea 27,216; Kolonia 21,448; Rhodopolis 17,479. The printed total, in the table and in the text ('a Population of 303238 men has been exterminated by the fire and the steel'), is 303,238; the rows add to 303,237. The communities (815) and schools (960) add up; the church column is printed 1,134 but its rows (603 + 135 + 127 + 182 + 53 + 74) add to 1,174. Each district list is headed 'Exterminated by the confiscation of their fortunes, exile, violations, cold, hunger, assassination and massacre' ('dépouillés de leurs biens confisqués, morts en exil, par la faim et le froid, violés, pillés, assassinés, tués et massacrés'), so the column counts the dispossessed and exiled as well as the dead. Four of the six figures are identical to the Patriarchate's 1919 count of those deported in 1914-18; only Amaseia (+44,708) and Kolonia (+1,510) differ. The text names as proof the massacres near Kavak, Djimbouch Han, Selamelik and Kiz Alan, the 'holocausts' at Ada, Karapertchine, Tekke Keuy and the French school at Merzifon, and the hangings at Amasya.", "sources_primary": ["[Pontic émigré advocacy body] Κεντρικό Συμβούλιο Πόντου, Livre noir. La tragédie du Pont 1914-1922 / Black Book: The Tragedy of Pontus 1914-1922 (Athens, 1922), p. 19 table and text after the district lists (Central Council of Pontus, Black Book: The Tragedy of Pontus 1914-1922 (Athens, 1922), archive.org item blackbooktragedy00281prich (no page numbers))"], "sources_modern": ["Agtzidis 2005, p. 201 (the table reproduced; the Black Book dated January 1922 and published by the Pontic organisations of Athens) [Greek]"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The identity with the 1919 deportation figures, and the book's own definition of 'exterminated' (confiscation, exile, violation, cold, hunger, murder and massacre), show that 303,238 is not a count of deaths.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Central Council of Pontus, Black Book (1922)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Athens", "lat": 37.976, "lon": 23.736, "role": "published"}]}, {"id": "B1-024", "date": "1922", "date_text": "1921–1922 (USS Sands war diary)", "precision": "year", "title": "Bafra emptied: 321 Greeks brought from Samsun for a possible Allied visit", "summary": "According to the war diary of USS Sands, after the deportations and massacres no Greeks were left in the Bafra district, and the Turks sent 321 Greeks there from Samsun in case an Allied commission came to check the charges.", "sources_primary": ["NARA, war diary of USS Sands (DD-243) (via Kyriakides & Nikoloudis, n. 70)"], "sources_modern": ["Kyriakides & Nikoloudis, The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians (Vatican archives volume), p. 57"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Date of the diary entry not given.", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: US naval war diary (via a Greek scholarly volume)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "emptied of Greeks"}, {"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "origin of the 321"}]}, {"id": "B1-025", "date": "1922-01", "date_end": "1922-02-16", "date_text": "January – February 1922", "precision": "month", "title": "Winter 1922: the women in the mountains deported; the Bafra hinterland swept", "summary": "In January 1922, after an amnesty, about a thousand women who had sheltered in the mountains returned to Samsun, many wounded and starving; officials promised them safety, but soon 'the whole band was deported to the interior'. In February 1922 Turkish forces directed by Fethi swept the hinterland of Bafra and captured those hiding in the mountains; according to a telegram from 21 Greek and Circassian refugees from Bafra, the interior minister offered rewards for heads and 'five sacks full of heads were brought to him at Baffra'. The same telegram says Circassians of Bafra saved about 140 Greek neighbours by hiding them in cellars.", "people": ["Fethi (Okyar), interior minister"], "sources_primary": ["Enclosure in Lindley's despatch no. 286, 3 June 1922: translation of a telegram from 21 Greek and Circassian refugees from Bafra, forwarded by the sub-governor of Tyroloé, Thrace, FO 371/7879", "British and US reports on the January 1922 returnees (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 251)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 409-410, 416"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The 'sacks of heads' is the refugees' allegation. Morris & Ze'evi's 'Fethi' is presumably the interior minister Fethi (Okyar).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Bafra Greek refugees' telegram", "Neutral sources: Circassian co-signatories; British FO; Morris & Ze'evi"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "about 1,000 women returned and were deported (Jan 1922)"}, {"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "sweep of the hinterland (Feb 1922)"}, {"name": "Nebyan Dağı", "lat": 41.35, "lon": 36.0, "role": "mountain refuges (approximate)"}, {"name": "Çorlu (Tyroloé), Eastern Thrace", "lat": 41.159, "lon": 27.8, "role": "where the refugees' telegram was sent from"}]}, {"id": "H7-033", "date": "1922-02-12", "date_end": "1922-02-16", "date_text": "12-16 February 1922 (Osman's raid on 15 February)", "precision": "day", "title": "A survivor's account: women and children burned in houses in the villages above Ordu (February 1922)", "summary": "On 15 February 1922 two to three hundred irregulars led by Osman Ağa rode into 'Ordu' (the report concerns the villages above the town), largely emptied of men. According to a survivor, they torched all but two houses, into which about 170 women and children and a handful of men were herded and burned; about fifteen young girls were taken aside, abused and killed the next day, though five or six were rumoured spared 'for the harem of the Pasha'. Nine neighbouring villages received the same treatment (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 416).", "people": ["Topal Osman Ağa", "Topal Osman"], "sources_primary": ["[US missionary transmission of a survivor's account] 'Destruction of Greek Villages above Ordou, February 12-16, 1922', attached to Riggs to Bristol, 21 June 1922, USNA RG 59, 867.4016, Roll 47 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 254)", "'Destruction of Greek Villages above Ordou, February 12–16, 1922', attached to Riggs to Bristol, 21 June 1922, USNA RG 59, 867.4016, Roll 47 (via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 254)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 416", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 416"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "One survivor's account, forwarded by an American missionary; the villages are not named, and we found no other report of this raid. The Armenian Patriarchate also reported an army attack on Greek villages of the Ordu kaza, including Yanakandon, dated '22 March 1922' in Kévorkian's text, which may be a slip for 1919 (Kévorkian 2023, part III, ch. 3, n. 51, a report of March 1919).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: survivor's account", "Neutral sources: forwarded by an American missionary (Riggs) to Bristol; Morris & Ze'evi", "Neutral sources: transmitted by the American missionary Riggs to Bristol; Morris & Ze'evi 2019, p. 416"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Ordu (Kotyora), villages above the town (approximate)", "lat": 40.984, "lon": 37.879, "role": "killings and burnings, 12-16 Feb 1922"}, {"name": "Ordu (Kotyora)", "lat": 40.984, "lon": 37.879, "role": "Greek villages in the hills above the town (not individually located)"}]}, {"id": "H7-038", "date": "1922-03", "date_end": "1922-06", "date_text": "March-June 1922", "precision": "month", "title": "Yowell and Ward expelled from Harput; their reports reach the press", "summary": "Expelled from Harput in March 1922, Yowell and Ward reported to the US consul at Aleppo (5 April 1922) that the Turkish authorities 'were frank in their statements that it was the intention to have all the Greeks die' (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 425). In May they published their findings in the New York Times and the Times of London; the British High Commission called the reports evidence of 'the deliberate attempt of the Angora Government to exterminate the Greek population of Anatolia' (p. 425). Rumbold, citing Ward, wrote that 'the Turks appear to be working on a deliberate plan to get rid of minorities', and that Turkish officials had told Ward that 'in 1915 Turks had not made a clean job of massacres' (p. 419). Admiral Bristol, the US High Commissioner, called the publication a British 'intrigue' and acted to discredit the reports (p. 427), though he had protested to Ankara about the deportations in July 1921. Harold Jaquith of the NER, touring eastern Anatolia that summer, saw 'thousands of shallow mounds' along the roads and found deportees removed from towns before his visits (pp. 427-428).", "people": ["Forrest D. Yowell", "Mark H. Ward", "Mark L. Bristol", "Horace Rumbold", "Harold Jaquith"], "sources_primary": ["[NER] Yowell and Ward to Jackson, 5 April 1922; New York Times, 6 May 1922 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 294)", "[British] Rumbold to FO (No. 241), 10 May 1922, FO 371/7876 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 267); Henderson, 'Turkey Annual Report 1922', FO 371/9176 (n. 289)", "[US] Bristol Papers, War Diary, 24 May 1922 (n. 305); Henderson to Balfour, 18 July 1922, FO 371/7880 (Jaquith; n. 309)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), pp. 419, 424-428", "Shenk 2017", "Shenk, 'Ethnic Cleansing, American Women, and the Admiral', in Shirinian (ed.), Genocide in the Ottoman Empire: Armenians, Assyrians, and Greeks, 1913-1923 (Berghahn, 2017), p. 201"], "confidence": "high", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Near East Relief (Yowell, Ward, Jaquith); British High Commission; US press; Morris & Ze'evi; Shenk"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Harput", "lat": 38.706, "lon": 39.252, "role": "NER station"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "US High Commission"}]}, {"id": "B1-026", "date": "1922-03", "date_end": "1922-04-05", "date_text": "March – April 1922", "precision": "month", "title": "The reports leave by the south: Harput relief workers reach Aleppo and Beirut", "summary": "Expelled from Harput in March 1922 (Ward left on 15 March), Yowell and Ward reported to the US consul at Aleppo, Jesse B. Jackson, on 5 April 1922. In late March Jackson reported that American missionaries arriving in Aleppo from Harput said that 'at least 75,000 Greeks' had been deported from the Pontus in the previous months, of whom only '20,000' had survived; the British consul at Beirut forwarded the account of a Harput missionary (3 April 1922).", "people": ["Jesse B. Jackson", "Forrest D. Yowell", "Mark H. Ward", "Ruth Parmelee"], "sources_primary": ["'Deportations and Atrocities in Samsoun', 19 Apr 1922, quoting Jackson's report of 23 Mar 1922 (RG 84, vol. 466)", "Yowell and Ward to Jackson, 5 Apr 1922", "Satow (Beirut) to Curzon, 3 Apr 1922, FO 371/7876"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 415, 422, 424-425", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), p. 78"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "75,000 deported and 20,000 survivors is a second-hand estimate; compare 63,844 moved (Central Army) and Rendel's 'well over 35,000' for 1921 (Morris & Ze'evi p. 415).", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: US consul at Aleppo; NER; British consul at Beirut"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Harput", "lat": 38.706, "lon": 39.256, "role": "departure of the expelled relief workers"}, {"name": "Aleppo", "lat": 36.202, "lon": 37.134, "role": "US consulate (Jackson)"}, {"name": "Beirut", "lat": 33.894, "lon": 35.502, "role": "British consulate (Satow)"}]}, {"id": "H7-035", "date": "1922-03-20", "date_text": "20 March 1922", "precision": "day", "title": "The Rendel memorandum (Foreign Office)", "summary": "George W. Rendel of the Foreign Office compiled a 'Memorandum on Turkish Massacres and Persecutions of Minorities since the Armistice' (20 March 1922). Its sentence that 'over 500,000 Greeks were deported, of whom comparatively few survived' concerns the World War I period and all Ottoman Greeks (Hofmann 2011, p. 103, quoting p. 1 of the memorandum); Morris & Ze'evi (p. 385) cite a similar phrase from Rendel's memorandum of 28 December 1921 for the 'as many as 550,000' Greeks uprooted during the war. For the Pontus in 1921 Rendel estimated that 'well over 35,000 Greeks' had been deported, the balance being 'massacred... in circumstances of utmost barbarity' (28 December 1921; Morris & Ze'evi, p. 415), and his memorandum of 20 March 1922 recorded 1,300 men shot near Kavak on 15 or 16 August 1921 (p. 408).", "people": ["George W. Rendel"], "sources_primary": ["[British official] Rendel memorandum, 20 March 1922, FO 371/7876", "[British official] Rendel, 'Atrocities in Asia Minor Etc. Protests Received by His Majesty's Government, and Action Taken', 28 Dec 1921, FO 371/7875 (via Morris & Ze'evi nn. 18, 245)"], "sources_modern": ["Travis, Genocide in the Middle East (2010), p. 287 (quotation)", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (New York and Athens: Caratzas, 2011), p. 103", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), pp. 385, 408, 415"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The 500,000 is not a Pontic figure: it covers all Ottoman Greeks deported in 1914-18.", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Rendel memoranda, FO 371/7875 and 7876 (via Hofmann, Morris & Ze'evi, Travis)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "reports compiled from British sources in Turkey"}]}, {"id": "H7-036", "date": "1922-04-10", "date_end": "1922-04-15", "date_text": "10-15 April 1922", "precision": "day", "title": "Samsun: further deportations of women, children and the old", "summary": "On 10 or 11 April 1922 a reported 1,324 Greeks, 'mostly women and children... [with] a few old men', were deported from Samsun; some 'were said to be so weak they could not walk out to the truck for their bread'. Another 1,462 women and children, many recently down from the mountains, were deported inland on 15 April (Morris & Ze'evi, pp. 417-418, after the diary of USS Fox). The wife of a Turkish officer later reported that Greeks had been 'marched into the hills near Kavak and murdered' (pp. 418-419). By September 1922 virtually no Greeks remained in Samsun.", "people": ["Webb Trammell (CO, USS Fox)"], "sources_primary": ["[US Navy] CO USS Fox, Webb Trammell, to Bristol, diary entry for 10 April 1922, RG 84 vol. 462; CO USS Childs, diary, 18 May 1922; E. G. Haas, USS Sturtevant, 1 May 1922 (via Morris & Ze'evi nn. 264-265)", "[US Navy] reports of USS Fox, April 1922, via Shenk, America's Black Sea Fleet (2017), p. 103 (not local)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), pp. 417-419", "Shenk 2017 (book), p. 103"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "1,324 (Morris & Ze'evi) or about 1,300 (Shenk).", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: US naval diaries (Fox, Childs, Sturtevant); Morris & Ze'evi; Shenk"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "deported from"}]}, {"id": "H7-037", "date": "1922-05", "date_text": "May 1922", "precision": "month", "title": "Trabzon: all Greek men from 15 upwards deported", "summary": "In early May 1922 Rumbold reported that at Trabzon 'the whole Greek male population from the age of 15 upwards' was 'being deported', in groups of fifty every few days (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 419). The American Herbert Adams Gibbons, of Robert College and the Christian Science Monitor, wrote on 20 May 1922 that Trabzon was left with 'half a dozen priests and ten other men and boys', not one doctor or teacher, and that the Turks were now seizing boys of 11 to 14; he also named the mayor, Hussein Effendi, and the vali as opponents of the persecution (p. 420). The Capuchin superior Father Lorenzo wrote from Trabzon on 14 May 1922 that every week groups of men were deported inland by way of Erzurum and that most died of hunger or disease (Kyriakidis 2026, p. 82).", "people": ["Herbert Adams Gibbons", "Horace Rumbold", "Lorenzo da Montemarciano"], "sources_primary": ["[British] Rumbold to FO (No. 242), 10 May 1922, FO 371/7876; Rumbold to FO, 27 May 1922, FO 371/7878 (via Morris & Ze'evi nn. 269-270)", "[American journalist] Gibbons, 'Trebizond, Asiatic Turkey', 20 May 1922, FO 371/7878 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 274)", "[Catholic missionary] Father Lorenzo, Trebizond, 14 May 1922, ASV, Arch. Deleg. Turchia, 115, fasc. 612, p. 26rv (via Kyriakidis 2026, p. 82 and Text 3)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), pp. 419-421", "Kyriakidis (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias Press, 2026), pp. 82-83"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Rumbold (British); Gibbons (American journalist); Father Lorenzo (Catholic missionary); Morris & Ze'evi; Kyriakidis 2026"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon (Trapezous)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "deported from"}, {"name": "Erzurum", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.266, "role": "route of the men's convoys (Father Lorenzo, 14 May 1922)"}, {"name": "Cevizlik (Maçka)", "lat": 40.806, "lon": 39.613, "role": "internment camp for boys (Gibbons; Rumbold)"}]}, {"id": "H7-064", "date": "1922-05-08", "date_end": "1922-05-30", "date_text": "8, 14 and 30 May 1922", "precision": "day", "title": "Catholic reports from Trebizond, May 1922: no Greek men left on the coast", "summary": "The Capuchin superior at Trebizond, Father Lorenzo, wrote on 8 May 1922 that there were hardly any Armenians left in the city and only women and children among the Greeks; that in Samsun there were no men but a few elderly; that inland no Greek villages remained, their people killed and the villages looted; and that at Giresun Topal Osman, with about a hundred men, killed and looted unpunished, 'supported by the Turkish government and honoured by the Muslim population'. On 14 May he added that at İnebolu, Samsun and Giresun only the old, women and children remained, and that every week groups of men were deported from Trebizond via Erzurum, most dying of hunger or disease; on 30 May he asked that his letter not be mentioned, since 'it could cost me my life' (Kyriakidis 2026, pp. 82-84).", "people": ["Lorenzo da Montemarciano", "Topal Osman Ağa"], "sources_primary": ["[Catholic missionary] Father Lorenzo, Trebizond, 8 May 1922 (AGC, H 97, Trapezus, II, fasc. II; Text 2), 14 May 1922 (ASV, Arch. Deleg. Turchia, 115, fasc. 612, p. 26rv; Text 3), 30 May 1922 (AGC, H 97) (via Kyriakidis 2026)"], "sources_modern": ["Kyriakidis (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias Press, 2026), pp. 82-84, 151-158"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Capuchin missionary reports (Vatican and Capuchin archives)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trabzon (Trapezous)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "reports written"}, {"name": "Giresun (Kerasous)", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.39, "role": "Topal Osman's killings"}, {"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "no men left"}, {"name": "İnebolu", "lat": 41.975, "lon": 33.76, "role": "no men left"}]}, {"id": "H7-065", "date": "1922-06-10", "date_text": "10 June 1922 (figures to 15 May 1922)", "precision": "day", "title": "Interior minister Fethi in secret session: 5,051 Greeks dead in four months; 'starving them is the most effective measure'", "summary": "Answering an interpellation by the Trabzon deputy Ali Şükrü, the interior minister Ali Fethi (Okyar) told a secret session of the Grand National Assembly on 10 June 1922 that he had taken the most effective measures 'to cleanse (tathir etmek, temizlemek) these Greeks who have been a calamity to the country for eight years'. He reported that up to 15 May 1922 the operations had disposed of 13,175 people: 3,388 surrendered, 5,051 dead, 1,592 captured alive and 3,144 wounded; that the food depots had been destroyed, 'leaving them hungry; starving them is the most effective measure'; and that, according to the Samsun mutasarrıflık, the Greeks remaining in that zone would not reach even five percent of the Muslim population (Çilingir, p. 121, from the published minutes). Korucu & Dağlıoğlu give the same session's figures by period: 1,275 killed of 4,444 'neutralised' between 29 December 1921 and 20 March 1922, and 3,777 killed and 1,150 taken into custody between 20 March and 15 May 1922 (p. 15). In May 1922 Fethi also reported that two months of operations under Cemil Cahit had led to the death or capture of 15,000 Greeks (p. 25).", "people": ["Ali Fethi (Okyar)", "Ali Şükrü Bey", "Cemil Cahit (Toydemir)"], "sources_primary": ["[Turkish parliamentary] TBMM Gizli Celse Zabıtları, vol. 3 (Türkiye İş Bankası Kültür Yayınları, 1985), pp. 369, 372-374 (10 June 1922), via Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], p. 121, and p. 373 via Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), p. 15"], "sources_modern": ["Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], p. 121", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), pp. 15, 25"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The two transmitters agree on the totals (3,388 surrendered; 5,051/5,052 killed) but date the start of the period differently (29 January 1922 in Çilingir, 29 December 1921 in Korucu & Dağlıoğlu).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: TBMM secret-session minutes, 10 June 1922 (admissions: numbers killed; the aim of reducing the Greeks below 5% of the Muslims)", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Çilingir; Korucu & Dağlıoğlu (transmitters; flagged)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Ankara (Angora)", "lat": 39.933, "lon": 32.86, "role": "Grand National Assembly"}, {"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "zone of operations"}]}, {"id": "B1-027", "date": "1922-08", "date_end": "1923", "date_text": "Autumn 1922 – 1923", "precision": "month", "title": "Near East Relief takes the surviving Greek children from Malatya and Harput to Syria", "summary": "In June 1921 the NER at Malatya ran two orphanages with about 800 children and fed 1,700 people. In autumn 1922, as Ankara closed the interior, it moved its children south. Lester J. Wright, director at Malatya, wrote from Aleppo on 10 October 1922 that he had taken 214 children over the mountains from Malatya in seven days to the railhead at Jarabulus, in French territory, and that 'we have permission to move out Greek children... About 700 of them at Malatia and 100 at Harpoot. We had many more at Malatia last fall but thousands died during the winter... they were all refugees that were driven from their homes a year ago or more'; eleven men had been hired all winter just to bury the dead. On 27 October 1922 Wright was killed by Turkish irregulars in French territory while escorting 200 children, half Greek and half Armenian, from Harput to Aleppo; he was buried in Beirut. The NER described the whole movement as '5,000 children' trekking 500 miles from Harput into Syria, 'to be distributed among the orphanages there or to be sent on further to Constantinople or Greece'. In 1923 Greece agreed to take more than 16,000 NER orphans, many of them housed on Syros.", "people": ["Lester J. Wright", "Howard B. McAfee", "Emily Petty"], "sources_primary": ["Wright, letter from Aleppo, 10 Oct 1922 (family papers; Waukesha Freeman clipping)", "New York Times on Wright's death (late Oct 1922)", "The Near East: A Discussion Course for Students (1923), cover caption", "Caption: Emily Petty with 210 Greek and Armenian orphans from Harput to Aleppo, 1923 (via johnsanidopoulos.com)"], "sources_modern": ["Krikorian & Taylor, 'Attribution of Genocide Photographs', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), pp. 401-405", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 459", "Near East Relief Museum, 'Near East Relief in Aleppo' and tag 'Syra' (neareastmuseum.com)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "How many of the Malatya Greek children were from Bafra is not known; Wright's description fits the Bafra and Samsun women's convoys that reached Malatya in late 1921. We have not found the ships and dates of the voyage from Beirut to Greece.", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Near East Relief letters and records"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Malatya", "lat": 38.355, "lon": 38.309, "role": "waypoint 1: NER orphanages evacuated"}, {"name": "Jarabulus (Jerablus)", "lat": 36.817, "lon": 38.011, "role": "waypoint 2: railhead on the Euphrates, French territory (7 days from Malatya)"}, {"name": "Aleppo", "lat": 36.202, "lon": 37.134, "role": "waypoint 3: NER orphanages"}, {"name": "Beirut", "lat": 33.894, "lon": 35.502, "role": "waypoint 4: port; Wright buried here"}, {"name": "Syros (Ermoupoli)", "lat": 37.444, "lon": 24.942, "role": "waypoint 5: NER orphanage in Greece"}, {"name": "Harput", "lat": 38.706, "lon": 39.256, "role": "second origin (100 Greek children)"}, {"name": "Aintab (Gaziantep)", "lat": 37.066, "lon": 37.383, "role": "alternative route of a Malatya group"}, {"name": "Urfa", "lat": 37.159, "lon": 38.796, "role": "on Wright's return road Jarabulus–Urfa–Diyarbakır–Harput"}]}, {"id": "B1-028", "date": "1922-08-26", "date_end": "1922-11-01", "date_text": "26 August – 1 November 1922", "precision": "day", "title": "A Bafra deportee escapes from Malatya to Aleppo, Beirut and Athens", "summary": "Antonios Gavriilidis, a tobacco merchant of Bafra deported to Malatya, agreed on 26 August 1922 with two Armenian friends (Hosrob Derohanesian and Serkiz Kalaytzian) and the brothers Ioannis and Aristidis Lazaridis (not related to the author of this atlas) to escape. Disguised and under Turkish names, with two muleteers (the Turk Hadji Ali and the Kurd Pakash, paid 125 gold lira) and 45 loaded donkeys, they travelled by night, bribed a Kurdish chief who detained them, and reached Aleppo, where the Greek community helped them. They went on by train to Beirut, by French ship to Constantinople, and reached Athens on 1 November 1922.", "people": ["Antonios Gavriilidis", "Hosrob Derohanesian", "Serkiz Kalaytzian", "Ioannis Lazaridis", "Aristidis Lazaridis"], "sources_primary": ["Gavriilidis, Η μαύρη εθνική συμφορά του Πόντου 1914-1922 (Athens 1924), excerpted in translation on greek-genocide.net"], "sources_modern": ["greek-genocide.net, 'Antonios Gavriilidis' (testimonies)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The route between Malatya and Aleppo is not given; the NER orphan caravans of the same weeks went from Malatya over the mountains to Jarabulus on the Euphrates (B1-027).", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Gavriilidis 1924 (memoir)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Malatya", "lat": 38.355, "lon": 38.309, "role": "waypoint 1: escape, August 1922"}, {"name": "Aleppo", "lat": 36.202, "lon": 37.134, "role": "waypoint 2: helped by the Greek community"}, {"name": "Beirut", "lat": 33.894, "lon": 35.502, "role": "waypoint 3: by train"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "waypoint 4: by French ship"}, {"name": "Athens", "lat": 37.984, "lon": 23.728, "role": "waypoint 5: arrival, 1 Nov 1922"}]}, {"id": "B1-029", "date": "1922-10", "date_end": "1923-08-28", "date_text": "October 1922 – August 1923", "precision": "month", "title": "Greek refugees at Aleppo: 12,000 in January 1923, most shipped to Greece by August", "summary": "After Ankara ordered the remaining Christians out in October 1922, most went to the ports, but a few Greeks travelled by land southward toward Aleppo, sometimes offered the option of staying if they converted; 'practically all' those heading for Mersin, Alexandretta and Aleppo were robbed on the way. In January 1923 a US destroyer reported 50,000 Armenian refugees in Aleppo 'along with 12,000 Greeks', the figure on the Library of Congress photograph captioned 'Where 12,000 Greek refugees were fed by Americans'. In early April Bristol counted 60,000 refugees in Aleppo, 50,000 of them Armenians. By August 1923 most of the Greeks had been shipped to Greece, though 'the poorest and weakest in health, mostly women and children' remained, begging (NER, Beirut, 28 Aug 1923).", "people": ["Jesse B. Jackson"], "sources_primary": ["Jackson to SecState, 18 and 25 Nov, 4 and 11 Dec 1922; Morgan (Aleppo) to Curzon, 22 Nov 1922 (via Morris & Ze'evi)", "Jordan, CO USS Barry, to Bristol, 6 Jan 1923; USS Barry to Bristol, 7 Jan 1923 (RG 84, vols 483-484)", "Bristol, report for week ending 1 Apr 1923 (Bristol Papers)", "Hekinian to managing director NER, Beirut, 28 Aug 1923, FO 371/9098", "Library of Congress, Bain collection, item 2014715755 (caption as given on greek-genocide.net)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 458, 461, 463, 465", "Near East Relief Museum, 'Near East Relief in Aleppo' (8,000 Anatolian Greeks by 1923)", "Yıldırım, Diplomacy and Displacement (Routledge 2006), via Mac Con Uladh (Irish Times) and Greek Reporter, 29 Sept 2015 ('at least 17,000' in Syrian cities)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "None says how many were Pontic; most were probably from Cilicia and central Anatolia.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: US consul at Aleppo; US Navy; Bristol; NER; LoC caption; NER Museum", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Yıldırım 2006 (a number, via a journalist)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Aleppo", "lat": 36.202, "lon": 37.134, "role": "refugee centre; NER feeding"}, {"name": "Alexandretta (İskenderun)", "lat": 36.587, "lon": 36.173, "role": "port on the southern route"}, {"name": "Mersin", "lat": 36.8, "lon": 34.633, "role": "port on the southern route"}, {"name": "Beirut", "lat": 33.894, "lon": 35.502, "role": "NER office; port"}]}, {"id": "B1-030", "date": "1922-10", "date_end": "1923-04", "date_text": "October 1922 – April 1923", "precision": "month", "title": "Expulsion to the ports: Samsun and Mersin", "summary": "In early October 1922 Ankara instructed the provinces to let the remaining Christians go, 'really an order for them all to get out'; on the Black Sea coast they were told they had thirty days to leave or be marched inland. In November 1922, according to Fotiadis, 10,460 Christians of the Samsun area, including Bafra, were shipped out. In March 1923 the NER fed 9,000 refugees at Samsun one hot meal a day while 4,000 had no aid; 10,000 awaited passage at Samsun, 3,000 at Trabzon, 1,500 at Ordu, 500 at Ünye and 300 at Fatsa, and 8,000 at Alexandretta and 3,500 at Mersin. In mid-March a proclamation at Samsun threatened to send home those who did not leave within days; in April embarkations stalled over fares and Greece's refusal to take more until the exchange began. Bristol reported on 29 March that the refugees at Mersin had been allowed to stay until ships came.", "people": ["Mark L. Bristol"], "sources_primary": ["USS Overton, Samsun diary, Oct–Nov 1922; CO USS McFarland, Simpson, Litchfield, Overton diaries, March 1923 (via Morris & Ze'evi)", "Bristol to SecState, 28 and 29 Mar 1923, FRUS 1923, vol. II, docs 222-223"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 456-457, 462-464", "Fotiadis 2019 (via Topalidis, S., 'A History of Bafra, Pontos', pontosworld.com (no. 792)) for the 10,460"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The 10,460 of November 1922 rests on Fotiadis via Topalidis.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: US Navy; FRUS; Morris & Ze'evi", "Greek sources: Fotiadis (10,460)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "port: 9,000 fed, 10,000 waiting (Mar 1923)"}, {"name": "Trabzon", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "port: 3,000 waiting"}, {"name": "Ordu", "lat": 40.984, "lon": 37.879, "role": "port: 1,500 waiting"}, {"name": "Ünye", "lat": 41.127, "lon": 37.288, "role": "port: 500 waiting"}, {"name": "Fatsa", "lat": 41.031, "lon": 37.5, "role": "port: 300 waiting"}, {"name": "Mersin", "lat": 36.8, "lon": 34.633, "role": "port: 3,500 waiting"}, {"name": "Alexandretta (İskenderun)", "lat": 36.587, "lon": 36.173, "role": "port (French): 8,000 waiting"}]}, {"id": "H7-067", "date": "1923-02", "date_text": "1921 – February 1923", "precision": "month", "title": "A Turkish count: 11,198 Greeks killed and 10,886 surrendered in the Pontus operations (to February 1923)", "summary": "According to Turkish military records summarised by Balcıoğlu, the operations in the Pontus, from early 1921 until they ended in February 1923, killed 11,198 Greeks, and 10,886 surrendered (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 25). The General Staff's history of the War of Independence gives 11,188 killed (İstiklal Harbi Tarihi, vol. IV, p. 294, via Çetinoğlu). The records describe the dead as bandits killed in operations.", "sources_primary": ["[Turkish military records] via Balcıoğlu, İki İsyan (2003), pp. 86-127, cited by Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), p. 25", "[Turkish official history] Türk İstiklal Harbi, vol. IV: İstiklal Harbinde Ayaklanmalar 1919-1921 (Genelkurmay, 1974), p. 294, via Çetinoğlu, 'Pontos'taki Düzmece İstiklal Mahkemeleri ve Yargılamaları' (posted 7 Jan 2020; reprinted in Çilingir 2016, pp. 229ff.) [Turkish writer], p. 10"], "sources_modern": ["Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), p. 25", "Çetinoğlu, 'Pontos'taki Düzmece İstiklal Mahkemeleri ve Yargılamaları' (posted 7 Jan 2020; reprinted in Çilingir 2016, pp. 229ff.) [Turkish writer], p. 10", "H6-078 (11,198 via bianet)", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Gayrimüslimlerin hepsini mahvedecek miyiz?', bianet, 19 May 2018 (first in Agos, 18 May 2018): 11,198 Greeks killed in military operations between early 1921 and February 1923"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "A lower limit for those killed in operations, from the state's own count, and so an admission; its description of the dead as bandits is under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H7-T10). It does not include deaths on the deportation marches. Korucu & Dağlıoğlu (and bianet, 2018) give 11,198 killed; Çetinoğlu, twice, after the General Staff's history, and Çilingir (2016) give 11,188, so one reading is probably a slip; all give 10,886 surrendered.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Turkish military records and General Staff history (admission)", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Balcıoğlu (transmitter)", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Korucu & Dağlıoğlu; Çetinoğlu"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "centre of the operations"}]}, {"id": "H7-068", "date": "1923-09-20", "date_text": "20 September 1923", "precision": "day", "title": "Refet Pasha to the Apostolic Delegate: Greeks and Armenians 'will have to pay the price for their sins'", "summary": "After the Allied evacuation of Constantinople, the new Apostolic Delegate, Mgr Filippi, asked Refet Pasha to prevent reprisals against Christians. Refet replied that 'Latin Catholics have nothing to fear... As for Greeks and Armenians, they will have to pay the price for their sins' (a following phrase, 'I mean the guilty ones', is crossed out in Filippi's report). Filippi also reported that high Turkish officials such as Nureddin Pasha had publicly roused Muslim feeling against Christians (Kyriakidis 2026, pp. 100-101). In December 1923 the Capuchin Father Michele counted 2,000-2,500 Greek refugees still waiting to leave Samsun (p. 100).", "people": ["Refet Pasha (Bele)", "Mgr Filippi", "Nureddin Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["[Vatican] Filippi to Cardinal Gasparri, 20 Sept 1923, Segreteria di Stato, AA.EE.SS., Turchia 1923-1924, Pos. 23, fasc. 43, pp. 50r-51v; F. Michele, Samsun, 22 Dec 1923, ASV, Arch. Deleg. Turchia, 119, fasc. 674, p. 11r (via Kyriakidis 2026)"], "sources_modern": ["Kyriakidis (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias Press, 2026), pp. 100-101"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Vatican diplomatic report"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "conversation"}, {"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "2,000-2,500 Greek refugees awaiting departure, Dec 1923"}]}, {"id": "H7-042", "date": "1923-12", "date_text": "Late December 1923", "precision": "month", "title": "The last Pontic bands leave for Poti", "summary": "Kapsis records that the band leader Pantelis Anastasiadis, with 25 armed men and 300 civilians, reached Poti in Georgia on two schooners in late December 1923.", "people": ["Pantelis Anastasiadis"], "sources_modern": ["Kapsis, 1922: Η μαύρη βίβλος (1992), p. 313 (as cited on Wikipedia)"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Kapsis 1992"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Poti", "lat": 42.146, "lon": 41.672, "role": "arrival"}]}, {"id": "B1-032", "date": "1924-06", "date_end": "1925", "date_text": "Summer 1924 – 1925", "precision": "month", "title": "Exiles at Diyarbakır leave for Greece through Aleppo and Beirut (route example)", "summary": "Georgios Kopasis, a Greek of Sille near Konya deported in December 1921 through Niğde, Kayseri, Sivas, Malatya and Elazığ to Diyarbakır, tells how in summer 1924 the exiles there were certified as 'exchangeable', walked about twenty days to Aleppo, stayed four months, then four to five months in Beirut, helped by the Patriarchate of Antioch, and sailed for Greece in 1925 on a ship he believed was the 'Archangelos'. He was not from the Pontus, but he was held on the same road and in the same place as Pontic deportees.", "people": ["Georgios Kopasis"], "sources_primary": ["Testimony of Georgios Kopasis, excerpts in Ta Nea, 27 Sept 2003"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "One testimony; it shows that the Syrian route was used by exchangees from the south-east, not how many took it.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: survivor's testimony"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Malatya", "lat": 38.355, "lon": 38.309, "role": "waypoint 1 (inbound, 1922)"}, {"name": "Mamuret-ul-Aziz (Elazığ)", "lat": 38.675, "lon": 39.222, "role": "waypoint 2 (inbound)"}, {"name": "Diyarbakır", "lat": 37.914, "lon": 40.23, "role": "waypoint 3: exile; departure summer 1924"}, {"name": "Aleppo", "lat": 36.202, "lon": 37.134, "role": "waypoint 4: about 20 days' walk; 4 months"}, {"name": "Beirut", "lat": 33.894, "lon": 35.502, "role": "waypoint 5: 4–5 months"}, {"name": "Athens", "lat": 37.984, "lon": 23.728, "role": "waypoint 6: Greece, 1925 (port not stated)"}]}, {"id": "H7-043", "date": "1925", "date_text": "1925", "precision": "year", "title": "The figure of 353,000 Pontic Greek dead, 1914–1924, and how it was reached", "summary": "The figure most often given for the Pontic Greek dead is 353,000. It is in G. K. Valavanis, Σύγχρονος γενική ιστορία του Πόντου (Athens 1925), p. 24, and is repeated by Fotiadis, by Tsirkinidis (quoted in Hofmann 2011, p. 104), by Bartrop (2017, p. 140: 'an estimated 353,000 died'), by Shirinian (2017, p. 56: 'about 350,000'), by Çilingir, and in the Rhode Island Senate resolutions of 2010 and 2025. How it was reached: the Central Council of Pontus (Black Book, 1922) counted 303,238 victims up to 1922, including the deportees of four metropolises, and Pontic refugee writers added 50,000 'new martyrs' for 1922 to March 1924 (Agtzidis 2005, p. 201). We have not consulted Valavanis' book; the figure and page are known to us through Shirinian (2017, p. 73, n. 187, after Fotiadis).", "sources_primary": ["[Pontic historian, advocacy context] Valavanis, Σύγχρονος γενική ιστορία του Πόντου (Athens, 1925), p. 24"], "sources_modern": ["Shirinian, 'The Background to the Late Ottoman Genocides', in Shirinian (ed.), Genocide in the Ottoman Empire: Armenians, Assyrians, and Greeks, 1913-1923 (Berghahn, 2017), p. 73 n. 187 (Fotiadis gives 353,000, citing Valavanis (Athens, 1925), p. 24)", "Cibran & Çetinoğlu, The Pontos Question (English summary, January 2007) [Turkish writers] (Valavanis 1986 reprint, p. 263: 'Pontus human losses amounted to 353,000 by 1924')", "Rhode Island Senate resolution 10-R196 (2010), repeating 353,000", "Agtzidis 2005, Έλληνες του Πόντου, p. 201 (caption to the Black Book table: the 303,000 'used thereafter as a basis', plus 50,000 added by Pontic refugee intellectuals for 1922–24) [Greek]", "Shirinian, in Shirinian (ed.) 2017, p. 56 ('about 350,000' Pontian Greek dead) [Armenian diaspora, Canada]", "Bartrop, in Shirinian (ed.) 2017, p. 140 ('an estimated 353,000 died') [international scholar, Australia]", "Tsirkinidis, quoted in Hofmann 2011, p. 104 (roughly 353,000 Pontic victims) [Greek, via international scholar]"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Other estimates: greek-genocide.net calls 353,000 too high and suggests about 250,000; Jones (2010) gives 300,000–360,000 and Peterson (2004) 360,000 for 1914–23. The ranges by phase and source are in the chapter's figures table.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Valavanis 1925; Rhode Island resolution (repeats it)", "Greek sources: Agtzidis 2005, p. 201 (the derivation); repeaters Shirinian, Bartrop, Tsirkinidis"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Athens", "lat": 37.976, "lon": 23.736, "role": "published"}]}, {"id": "H7-045", "date": "1994-03-11", "date_text": "24 February 1994 (voted); 11 March 1994 (published)", "precision": "day", "title": "Greece: Law 2193/1994 makes 19 May the day of remembrance of the genocide of the Pontic Greeks", "summary": "Law 2193/1994 (ΦΕΚ Α΄ 32/11.3.1994), article 1: 19 May is the Day of Remembrance of the Genocide of the Greeks in Pontus. Article 2 leaves the ceremonies to a presidential decree (PD 99/1994, ΦΕΚ Α΄ 78/20.5.1994). The law does not name the perpetrator; a bill tabled on 20 May 2025 by a group of MPs proposed amending it to do so. Kritikakos (2026, p. 124) writes that the Hellenic Parliament declared 19 May after lobbying by Fotiadis and Charalambidis, with PASOK support.", "sources_primary": ["Law 2193/1994, ΦΕΚ Α΄ 32/11.3.1994, as cited in Hellenic Parliament αναφορές of Koulkoudinas (7 July 2026) and Giannoulis (29 Mar 2023)", "PD 99/1994, ΦΕΚ Α΄ 78/20.5.1994", "Explanatory memorandum of the 2025 bill (hellenicparliament.gr)"], "sources_modern": ["Sjöberg 2016, p. 73 (as cited on Wikipedia)", "Kritikakos 2026, p. 124 (19 May declared in 1994 after lobbying by Fotiadis and Charalambidis, with PASOK support)", "CNN Greece (cnn.gr, story 266462), 'Η Βουλή τιμά τη Γενοκτονία των Ελλήνων του Πόντου': the Hellenic Parliament 'στις 24 Φεβρουαρίου 1994' unanimously voted 19 May as the Day of Remembrance (law 2193/1994)"], "confidence": "high", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Law 2193/1994; PD 99/1994; Hellenic Parliament documents", "Neutral sources: Sjöberg 2016 (vote date)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Athens", "lat": 37.976, "lon": 23.736, "role": "Hellenic Parliament"}]}, {"id": "H7-046", "date": "1998-10-13", "date_text": "Published 13 October 1998", "precision": "day", "title": "Greece: Law 2645/1998 makes 14 September the day of remembrance of the genocide of the Greeks of Asia Minor", "summary": "Law 2645/1998 established 14 September as a day of national remembrance of the 'Genocide of the Greeks of Asia Minor by the Turkish State'. With the 1994 law it underpins the wider 'Greek genocide' framing. The designation was unanimous (Kritikakos 2026, p. 125); the presidential decree implementing the law was withdrawn in 2001 and re-signed without the word 'genocide' (Agtzidis 2010, p. 274).", "sources_primary": ["Law 2645/1998, ΦΕΚ Α΄ of 13 Oct 1998"], "sources_modern": ["Kritikakos 2026, p. 125 (the designation unanimous)", "Agtzidis 2010, p. 274 (the presidential decree implementing Law 2645/1998 withdrawn in 2001 and re-signed without the word 'genocide') [Greek]"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The Turkish foreign ministry's condemnation of the law is under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H7-T09).", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Law 2645/1998"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Athens", "lat": 37.976, "lon": 23.736, "role": "Hellenic Parliament"}]}, {"id": "H7-047", "date": "2007-12", "date_text": "December 2007 (press release of 15 December)", "precision": "month", "title": "International Association of Genocide Scholars resolution", "summary": "The IAGS resolved, with 83 percent of voting members in favour, that 'the Ottoman campaign against Christian minorities of the Empire between 1914 and 1923 constituted a genocide against Armenians, Assyrians, and Pontian and Anatolian Greeks', and called on Turkey to acknowledge it, apologise and make restitution.", "sources_primary": ["IAGS resolution and press release, Dec 2007 (copy at aga-online.org/wp-content/uploads/IAGS_Resolution.pdf)"], "sources_modern": ["Charny, in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds) 2011, pp. 33–37 (text of the resolution; opposition inside the IAGS) [international scholar]", "Kritikakos 2026, p. 43 (83%; the Weitz, Feinstein and Akçam letter, 'too general and without nuance'; Balakian's memo; after Sjöberg 2016, pp. 194–196)"], "confidence": "high", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: IAGS resolution and press release (aga-online.org copy)"], "main": true}, {"id": "H7-069", "date": "2009-04-30", "date_text": "30 April 2009", "precision": "day", "title": "South Australia: the Parliament passes a motion condemning the genocide of the Armenians, Pontian Greeks and Syrian Orthodox", "summary": "On 30 April 2009 the Parliament of South Australia (House of Assembly, per the Hansard reference) passed a motion moved by Michael Atkinson which condemns 'the genocide of the Armenians, Pontian Greeks, Syrian Orthodox and other Christian minorities', describes 'the genocide by the Ottoman state between 1915-1923 of Armenians, Hellenes, Syrian and other minorities in Asia Minor' as one of the greatest crimes against humanity, and calls on the Commonwealth Parliament to condemn it. The Turkish Embassy called it 'the work of an ethnic lobby' (19 June 2009) (Kritikakos, pp. 190-191).", "people": ["Michael Atkinson"], "sources_primary": ["Parliament of South Australia, House of Assembly, Hansard, 30 April 2009 (hansardsearch.parliament.sa.gov.au/daily/lh/2009-04-30), as cited by Kritikakos, Armenian, Greek, and Assyrian Genocide Recognition in Twenty-First Century Australia (Palgrave Macmillan, 2026), p. 216 n. 71"], "sources_modern": ["Kritikakos, Armenian, Greek, and Assyrian Genocide Recognition in Twenty-First Century Australia (Palgrave Macmillan, 2026), pp. 190-191"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "A state-parliament motion, not a law; it names the Pontian Greeks explicitly. The chamber is inferred from the Hansard reference ('lh', the lower house) and the House of Assembly travel report cited with it; Kritikakos says 'the Parliament of South Australia'.", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Parliament of South Australia (via Kritikakos 2026)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Adelaide", "lat": -34.921, "lon": 138.599, "role": "Parliament of South Australia"}]}, {"id": "H7-048", "date": "2010-03-11", "date_text": "11 March 2010", "precision": "day", "title": "Sweden: the Riksdag calls for recognition of the 1915 genocide including the Pontic Greeks", "summary": "On 11 March 2010 the Riksdag passed, by 131 votes to 130 and against the advice of its Foreign Affairs Committee, motion 2008/09:U332, which recognises as genocide the killing in 1915 of 'the Armenian, Assyrian/Syriac/Chaldean and Pontic Greek populations'. It is a statement to the government (tillkännagivande), not a law; the government did not adopt it.", "sources_primary": ["Riksdagen, written question 'Erkännande av folkmordet seyfo' (H8112535), restating the decision of 11 March 2010", "ANCA press release, 11 Mar 2010 (anca.org/?p=6061) (motion number and vote)"], "sources_modern": ["Kritikakos, Armenian, Greek, and Assyrian Genocide Recognition in Twenty-First Century Australia (Palgrave Macmillan, 2026), p. 44 (2010: 'Greeks (specifically the Pontic Greeks)'; Ingmar Karlsson's objection, Svenska Dagbladet, 3 April 2010)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The Swedish historian Ingmar Karlsson called the inclusion of the Pontic Greeks 'inaccurate historiography' and spoke of ethnic cleansing (Kritikakos p. 44).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Riksdag written question restating the decision", "Armenian sources: ANCA press release, 11 Mar 2010"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Stockholm", "lat": 59.327, "lon": 18.071, "role": "Riksdag"}]}, {"id": "H7-049", "date": "2010-04-28", "date_text": "28 April 2010 (and later years, to 15 May 2025)", "precision": "day", "title": "Rhode Island Senate commemorates the 'Pontian Genocide of 1914-1922'", "summary": "Senate resolution 10-R196 (2010-S 2815) of 28 April 2010 proclaimed 19 May 2010 'Greek Pontian Genocide Remembrance Day', for the 'Pontian Genocide of 1914-1922', with 353,000 'Pontian and Anatolian Greeks'. Similar resolutions followed (e.g. 14-R222, 1 May 2014), and R 299 (2025-S 1080) of 15 May 2025 named a 'Greek and Pontic Genocide Remembrance Day' for 1913-1923, again with 353,000.", "sources_primary": ["Rhode Island resolutions 10-R196 (28 Apr 2010; webserver.rilegislature.gov/PublicLaws/law10/res10/res10196.htm), 14-R222 (1 May 2014) and R 299 (15 May 2025; …/law25/res25/res25299.htm)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "One-chamber commemorative resolutions, not laws.", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Rhode Island Senate resolutions (rilegislature.gov)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Providence, Rhode Island", "lat": 41.824, "lon": -71.412, "role": "State Senate"}]}, {"id": "H7-050", "date": "2015-03-24", "date_text": "24 March 2015", "precision": "day", "title": "Armenia: the National Assembly condemns the genocide of the Greeks and Assyrians", "summary": "The National Assembly of Armenia adopted unanimously (117-0-0) a statement condemning 'the genocide of the Greeks and Assyrians, committed by the Ottoman Turkey in 1915-1923'.", "sources_primary": ["Armenpress, 24 March 2015"], "confidence": "high", "basis": "armenian", "basis_detail": ["Armenian sources: Armenpress; National Assembly of Armenia"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Yerevan", "lat": 40.177, "lon": 44.513, "role": "National Assembly"}]}, {"id": "H7-051", "date": "2015-04", "date_text": "By 22 April 2015", "precision": "month", "title": "Austria: the parliamentary groups' declaration on the Armenian genocide names the Pontic Greeks among the victims", "summary": "The joint declaration of the leaders of the six parliamentary groups (Schieder, Lopatka, Strache, Glawischnig, Dietrich, Strolz), titled on the genocide 'an den Armeniern', names among the victims of 'Gewalt, Mord und Vertreibung' tens of thousands of members of other Christian groups of the Ottoman Empire, the Aramaeans, Assyrians, Chaldeans and 'Pontos-Griechen'. The next sentence speaks of the duty 'die schrecklichen Geschehnisse als Genozid anzuerkennen und zu verurteilen'. The genocide it names explicitly is that of the Armenians; 'die Geschehnisse' can be read to cover all the victims, and Wikipedia and the Armenian National Committee of America count it as a recognition. We describe it as having named the Pontic Greeks among the victims, not as an explicit recognition.", "sources_primary": ["'Gemeinsame Erklärung der KOs der 6 Parlamentsparteien zum Genozid an den Armeniern' (copy at erinnern.at)", "salzburg24.at, 22 Apr 2015"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "An ambiguous text.", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: joint declaration (erinnern.at); salzburg24.at"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Vienna", "lat": 48.208, "lon": 16.36, "role": "Parliament"}]}, {"id": "H7-070", "date": "2023-05-11", "date_text": "11 May 2023", "precision": "day", "title": "Tasmania: the House of Assembly recognises the Armenian, Greek and Assyrian genocides", "summary": "On 11 May 2023 the Parliament of Tasmania (House of Assembly) passed a motion, proposed by Premier Jeremy Rockliff with support from Labor, Liberal and the Greens, that 'condemns the genocide of the Armenians, Assyrians and Greeks' and honours 'over 1 million Assyrians and Greeks' among the victims; Tasmania became the third Australian state to do so (Kritikakos, pp. 209-210).", "people": ["Jeremy Rockliff"], "sources_primary": ["Parliament of Tasmania, House of Assembly, Hansard, 11 May 2023, as cited by Kritikakos, Armenian, Greek, and Assyrian Genocide Recognition in Twenty-First Century Australia (Palgrave Macmillan, 2026), n. 120"], "sources_modern": ["Kritikakos, Armenian, Greek, and Assyrian Genocide Recognition in Twenty-First Century Australia (Palgrave Macmillan, 2026), pp. 209-210"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Names the Greeks generally, not the Pontic Greeks in particular.", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Parliament of Tasmania (via Kritikakos 2026)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Hobart", "lat": -42.882, "lon": 147.327, "role": "Parliament of Tasmania"}]}, {"id": "H7-052", "date": "2025-06-19", "date_text": "June 2025 (reported 19 June)", "precision": "day", "title": "Cyprus: the House of Representatives designates 19 May and recognises the genocide", "summary": "The Cypriot House of Representatives unanimously designated 19 May the 'Official Day of Remembrance and Honor for the Greeks of Pontus'. The Cyprus News Agency reported this as 'formally recognizing the genocide committed … by the Young Turks and Kemalists between 1914 and 1923'.", "sources_primary": ["Cyprus News Agency, 19 June 2025 (cna.org.cy/en/article/8634681)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The news report does not give the date of the plenary session or the form of the act. Syriac Press (27 May 2026) reports a memorial and measures for schools; we have not consulted the text.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Cyprus News Agency; Republic of Cyprus House of Representatives"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Nicosia", "lat": 35.172, "lon": 33.365, "role": "House of Representatives"}]}], "turkish": [{"id": "H7-004", "date": "1916", "date_end": "1918", "date_text": "1916-1918", "precision": "year", "title": "Greek bands raid Muslim villages around Çarşamba, 1916-18 (Turkish scholarship)", "summary": "Turkish historians Aydın and Duman describe Greek armed bands in the Çarşamba-Samsun area in 1916-18 that set road blocks, raided Muslim villages, attacked supply convoys and looted grain stores, with logistic support from the Russian command at Trebizond. One band leader of the Bafra area, known as Anton Pasha, was killed in 1917. The journalist-historian Ayşe Hür, in an article reprinted by Çilingir (pp. 226-227), writes from Turkish memoirs that the Russians armed Greeks of Bafra, that the band leader Vasil Usta (Vasilis Anthopoulos), landed at Samsun by a Russian torpedo boat, raided Turkish villages after the Russian withdrawal, and that Greek bands looted Turkish villages around Merzifon in November 1918.", "places": [{"name": "Çarşamba", "lat": 41.199, "lon": 36.723, "role": "area of band activity"}, {"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.906, "role": "area of band activity"}], "people": ["'Anton Pasha' (band leader)"], "sources_primary": ["Ottoman archival documents as used by Aydın & Duman (not consulted)"], "sources_modern": ["Aydın & Duman, 'I. Dünya Savaşı Yıllarında Çarşamba/Samsun ve Çevresinde Rum Çete Faaliyetleri', History Studies 4.2 (2012), pp. 39-51", "Ayşe Hür, 'Pontus'un gayri resmi tarihi' (Taraf), reprinted in Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], pp. 226-227 [Turkish writer relaying Turkish memoirs]", "Agtzidis 1992, p. 167 (corroborating, in general terms: 'Russian aid') [Greek]"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Ottoman archival documents as used by Aydın & Duman 2012", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Ayşe Hür (relaying Turkish memoirs; flagged)"], "main": false, "pending_category": true, "counter_evidence": "Partly corroborated, in general terms only: Morris & Ze'evi (p. 383) write that during the war bands of draft evaders 'occasionally attacked Turkish villagers and gendarmes', and that the Turks charged that they were armed from Russia (H7-063). The specifics here (road blocks, raids on convoys and grain stores, logistic support from the Russian command at Trebizond, Vasil Usta's raids) have no non-Turkish source. Greek sources describe the bands as self-defence.", "overlaps_with": ["H6-013"], "moved_to_main": ["Russian arms for the bands, as a general statement (Agtzidis 1992, p. 167) → H7-063 (chapter 10)"]}, {"id": "H7-T08", "date": "1919", "date_end": "1923", "date_text": "1919-1923", "precision": "year", "title": "Pontic bands attack Muslim villages: a rebellion organised from Greece", "summary": "Turkish accounts say that the Greek bands active in the mountains behind Bafra, Kavak and Çarşamba in 1919-23 attacked Muslim villages and were a rebellion organised from Greece (with the Mavri Mira and Pontus societies distributing arms). In the Grand National Assembly in 1921 deputies said that more than sixty Muslim villages had been burned in three months (Hafız Mehmet, 29 October 1921) and ministers gave figures for Muslims killed (H7-T10). Turkish official sources put the bands at 6,000-7,000 armed men at first and 25,000 later (Cibran & Çetinoğlu, after Turkish official history).", "places": [{"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.906, "role": "killings, burnings, deportations"}, {"name": "Kavak", "lat": 41.079, "lon": 36.044, "role": "killings in the gorge"}, {"name": "Çarşamba", "lat": 41.199, "lon": 36.723, "role": "area of band activity"}], "sources_primary": ["TBMM secret session, 29 Oct 1921 (Hafız Mehmet), via Çilingir p. 119", "Kemal's reports of May-June 1919 (H.T.V.D. no. 4, via Balcıoğlu and Cibran & Çetinoğlu)"], "sources_modern": ["Aydın & Duman 2012; Biber (TÜBA); Pontus Meselesi (1922)", "Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], p. 119", "Cibran & Çetinoğlu, The Pontos Question (English summary, January 2007) [Turkish writers]"], "confidence": "low", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Turkish accounts (TBMM statements; Aydın & Duman 2012; Pontus Meselesi)"], "main": false, "pending_category": false, "counter_evidence": "Partly corroborated (H7-063): a Greek band of nearly a hundred attacked a Turkish village near Samsun in early September 1921 (USS McFarland), with a similar raid in October; Col. Rawlinson put the armed Greeks in the mountains at 20,000-30,000 in May 1922. Against the rest: we found no non-Turkish count of Muslim victims; 'Western observers uniformly asserted that there was no Pontine rebellion' (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 406); Hafız Mehmet himself said he had seen a battalion of the Giresun regiment burn villages under the pretext of collecting arms, and the Lazistan deputies blamed Nureddin for driving Greeks to the mountains (H7-031), so some burned villages may have been burned by Turkish forces; Greek sources honour the bands as defenders of villages and refugees (H7-042; B1-007).", "overlaps_with": ["H6-013"]}, {"id": "H7-T03", "date": "1919-05", "date_text": "Late May 1919", "precision": "month", "title": "Kemal's report of 'forty guerrilla bands'", "summary": "Mustafa Kemal reported to Constantinople in late May 1919 that since the armistice 'forty guerrilla' bands, 'in an organized program', were killing Turks to 'establish a Pontus state' (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 384). His report of 22 May 1919, as summarised by Cibran & Çetinoğlu after Balcıoğlu, counted about forty Greek bands, thirty-three of them in Samsun, and about thirteen Turkish bands, six of them in Samsun.", "places": [{"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "landing"}], "people": ["Mustafa Kemal (Atatürk)"], "sources_primary": ["Kemal's reports to Constantinople, May 1919 (Shaw, From Empire to Republic, vol. 2, pp. 592-593, via Morris & Ze'evi n. 17); report of 22 May 1919, H.T.V.D. no. 4 (June 1953), doc. 69, via Balcıoğlu, Bir Paşa, p. 82, via Cibran & Çetinoğlu, The Pontos Question (English summary, January 2007) [Turkish writers]"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 384", "Cibran & Çetinoğlu, The Pontos Question (English summary, January 2007) [Turkish writers]"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Kemal's reports, May 1919"], "main": false, "pending_category": false, "counter_evidence": "Morris & Ze'evi, who report the claim (p. 384, after Shaw), write that 'the reality was different' (p. 383): the separatist agitation had resulted in 'almost no acts of overt rebellion' and very little anti-Turk terrorism, and they add that 'it is possible that many Turks believed these allegations' (p. 384). Kemal's own report counted Turkish bands as well (thirteen)."}, {"id": "B1-T01", "date": "1921", "date_text": "1921 (statement of the foreign minister)", "precision": "year", "title": "Yusuf Kemal: Greek bands burned Koçhisar and the Sakızcı quarter of Bafra", "summary": "The Ankara foreign minister Yusuf Kemal (Tengirşenk) said that Greek bands had burned Koçhisar in Bafra and the Sakızcı quarter, and that Muslims who could not flee were 'burned alive in their houses' (no place given for the latter).", "places": [{"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "Koçhisar and the Sakızcı quarter (not located separately)"}], "people": ["Yusuf Kemal (Tengirşenk)"], "sources_primary": ["Statement of Yusuf Kemal (via Biber, nn. 98-99, from Echos d'Orient)"], "sources_modern": ["Biber, ch. 13 in the TÜBA history series, p. 441"], "confidence": "low", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Ankara government statement via Biber"], "counter_evidence": "No non-Turkish source for these particular burnings was found. The Greek deputy Theotokas told the British in July 1921 that all the villages around Bafra had been burned by the government side (cited by Biber, p. 436), and US officers reported about 100 Greek villages south of Bafra destroyed from 17 May 1921 (B1-001).", "cross_ref": ["H6-013"], "main": false, "overlaps_with": ["H6-013"]}, {"id": "B1-T03", "date": "1921", "date_text": "1921", "precision": "year", "title": "An old Greek signalled a Greek warship near Bafra", "summary": "Nureddin Pasha justified deporting the old by the case of a 65-year-old Greek at 'Gümenez', left behind for his age, who waved a flag to a Greek torpedo boat; its men came ashore in boats, a group from Bafra arrived with their forces, 'old Bald Nikola' was hanged and the enemy fleet driven off. Yusuf Kemal cited the same case.", "places": [{"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "coast near Bafra ('Gümenez' not located)"}], "people": ["Nureddin Pasha", "Yusuf Kemal (Tengirşenk)"], "sources_primary": ["Nureddin Pasha's statement of defence (Balcıoğlu 2000, p. 275)", "Statement of Yusuf Kemal (via Biber, n. 99)"], "sources_modern": ["Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Turkish-language work), p. 130", "Biber, ch. 13 in the TÜBA history series, p. 441"], "confidence": "low", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Nureddin's and Yusuf Kemal's justifications"], "counter_evidence": "Partly in context: Greece blockaded the coast from 26 March 1921 and its warships were offshore. But one incident does not explain the deportation of women, children and orphans (B1-015, B1-017 to B1-019); and the hanging of the old man is itself an admission.", "cross_ref": ["H6-T06"], "main": false, "overlaps_with": ["H6-T06"]}, {"id": "H7-T04", "date": "1921-01", "date_end": "1922-06", "date_text": "January 1921 - June 1922", "precision": "month", "title": "Ankara's case for the deportations of 1921-22", "summary": "Ankara justified the deportation of Greek men from the coast by the danger of a Greek landing at Samsun (Nureddin's circular of 12 January 1921; the Council of Ministers' order of 12 June 1921 cites a Greek landing at Samsun), by the Greek bands, and by the Greek navy's shelling of İnebolu (June 1921) and later of Samsun (June 1922), which it cited as proof of Greek designs on the Pontus. The Turks also claimed that Pontic Greeks had joined the Greek army and that the deportations were meant to force the bands to give up their arms (Morris & Ze'evi, pp. 406-407). An Interior Ministry letter of 25 June 1921 described the measure as a security measure, not a deportation (tehcir) (Cibran & Çetinoğlu). Turkish historiography calls it a lawful wartime relocation (sevk/tehcir) of men of military age from a coast exposed to Greek attack; the Central Army's orders spoke of 'chastening' the bands and destroying the villages that sheltered them (H6-052).", "places": [{"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "main point of departure"}, {"name": "Amasya (Amaseia)", "lat": 40.654, "lon": 35.833, "role": "first stage inland"}, {"name": "İnebolu", "lat": 41.975, "lon": 33.76, "role": "bombarded"}], "people": ["Nureddin Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["Order No. 2082 (12 Jan 1921), via Güzel 2006, Çilingir and Çetinoğlu", "Council of Ministers' order of 12 June 1921 (via Çilingir p. 117)", "Decree of 16 June 1921 (its justification), via Biber", "'Answers by Mustapha Kemal Pasha (written by Yussuf Bey)', memorandum to Lt R. S. Dunn, 3 July 1921, LC, Bristol Papers 35 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 169); Allen Dulles to SecState, 28 June 1921 (n. 173)"], "sources_modern": ["Güzel, Dünden Bugüne Yunanistan'ın Pontus Hedefi (2006)", "Biber (TÜBA) ch. 13", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), pp. 406-407", "Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], p. 117", "Cibran & Çetinoğlu, The Pontos Question (English summary, January 2007) [Turkish writers]"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The orders themselves are told in the chapter (H7-022, H7-026); this entry gives their justification.", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: justifications in the Ankara documents; Güzel 2006; Biber (TÜBA)"], "main": false, "pending_category": false, "counter_evidence": "Partly corroborated: Greece blockaded the coast from 26 March 1921, and Greek warships shelled İnebolu on 9 June 1921 and Samsun on 7 June 1922 (H7-023, H6-061). Against the rest: the İnebolu shelling 'produced no casualties' and an Italian at Samsun called it 'an excuse for the massacres'; it 'may indeed have hastened the deportation process, but it wasn't the cause of the campaign, which was launched weeks before' (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 407); a convoy from Samsun was already on the road to Kavak by 2 June (H7-029); no Greek landing on the Pontic coast was attempted; 'Western observers uniformly asserted that there was no Pontine rebellion' (p. 406); the Interior Ministry's own order of 25 June 1921 did not cover women and children, yet they were deported on Nureddin's orders (H7-026); the deputy governor of Samsun proposed expelling all Greek villages so that Greeks would nowhere be a majority (H6-052), and in June 1922 the interior minister reported that the remaining Greeks would not reach five percent of the Muslims (H7-065); the Near East Relief officers reported that officials 'were frank in their statements that it was the intention to have all the Greeks die' (H7-038); and deputies in Ankara attacked Nureddin's conduct (H7-031).", "overlaps_with": ["H6-T06", "B1-T02", "B1-T08"]}, {"id": "B1-T05", "date": "1921-01-12", "date_end": "1921-12", "date_text": "1921", "precision": "month", "title": "Mount Nebyan as a rebel base; the villages destroyed had supplied the bands", "summary": "Biber writes that the Greeks of Samsun planned to gather on Nebyan Dağı above Bafra; that Ankara postponed a disarmament measure on 12 January 1921; that Greeks who fled to Nebyan clashed with the Central Army, some later surrendering their arms; that men who refused conscription joined the bands; and that the villages destroyed were those that supplied the bands, their people, women and children included, fleeing to the mountains or being sent inland.", "places": [{"name": "Nebyan Dağı", "lat": 41.35, "lon": 36.0, "role": "alleged rebel gathering place (approximate)"}, {"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "district"}], "sources_primary": ["Ottoman and Turkish archival documents (via Biber, nn. 57, 60)"], "sources_modern": ["Biber, ch. 13 in the TÜBA history series, p. 434"], "confidence": "low", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Biber (TÜBA) from Turkish archives"], "counter_evidence": "Partly corroborated: the Greek witness calls Nebyan 'the centre of the Greek refugees' (B1-007), and a US naval diary records a Greek band raid on a Turkish village (H6-079). The admission that villages were destroyed and women and children sent inland is told in the chapter (B1-005, B1-015). Against the rest: the women's and orphans' convoys were not band supporters in any ordinary sense, and Morris & Ze'evi write that 'Western observers uniformly asserted that there was no Pontine “rebellion,” either underway or in preparation' (p. 406).", "cross_ref": ["H6-T04", "H7-T08"], "main": false, "overlaps_with": ["H6-T04"]}, {"id": "H7-T13", "date": "1921-03", "date_text": "March 1921", "precision": "month", "title": "Mustafa Kemal's reply to Pope Benedict XV: the Christians are calm; the Greeks and Armenians commit crimes", "summary": "Replying in March 1921 to the Pope's appeal, sent through Cardinal Gasparri, to protect the lives and property of Christians, Mustafa Kemal telegraphed that he considered it his duty to safeguard the life of all, irrespective of nation or religion, that Christians in the areas under the Grand National Assembly were altogether calm, and that Greeks and Armenians were committing crimes (Kyriakidis 2026, p. 76, after Carolla).", "places": [{"name": "Ankara (Angora)", "lat": 39.933, "lon": 32.86, "role": "reply sent"}], "people": ["Mustafa Kemal", "Benedict XV"], "sources_primary": ["Kemal to the Pope, March 1921 (Carolla, La Santa Sede e la Questione Armena (2006), pp. 62, 220-221, doc. 99, via Kyriakidis 2026)"], "sources_modern": ["Kyriakidis (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias Press, 2026), p. 76"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Kemal's telegram (its claims)", "Neutral sources: Vatican record"], "main": false, "pending_category": false, "counter_evidence": "Within months Dolci reported that Christians 'kept being murdered' and the Pope asked Kemal to cancel the exile of 5,000 women and children near Samsun (H7-061). Dolci himself thought the papal intervention useful and rejected the view that it had only let Kemal reassure public opinion (Kyriakidis p. 77)."}, {"id": "H7-T05", "date": "1921-06-02", "date_end": "1921-09-15", "date_text": "2 June - 15 September 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "Ankara: the Kavak dead fell in battle with Greek bands, and the Bafra dead in fighting with bands", "summary": "On 15 September 1921 the Ankara foreign minister Yusuf Kemal told the Allied High Commissioners that the men killed at Kavak had been killed justifiably in battle after Greek bands attacked Turks first (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 408). As early as 2 June 1921 the mutasarrıf of Canik and the 10th Division reported that a convoy of 766 Greeks sent from Samsun to Kavak had been attacked by 'brigands' in the Canik mountains (Çilingir, p. 117); Turkish sources say the convoy was fired on by Greek bands (Cibran & Çetinoğlu). For Bafra, Ankara and Turkish historiography say the dead fell in fighting with bands based on Mount Nebyan, that Greek bands burned Koçhisar and the Sakızcı quarter of Bafra, and that the destroyed villages had supplied the bands (B1-T01, B1-T05).", "places": [{"name": "Kavak", "lat": 41.079, "lon": 36.044, "role": "killings in the gorge"}, {"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.906, "role": "killings, burnings, deportations"}], "people": ["Yusuf Kemal (Tengirşenk)", "Topal Osman"], "sources_primary": ["Youssouf Kemal to the High Commissioners, 15 Sept 1921, USNA RG 59, 860J.4016 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 189)", "Canik mutasarrıf Sezai and 10th Division commander İsmail to the Central Army, 2 June 1921 (via Çilingir p. 117)", "Statements of Yusuf Kemal (via Biber, from Echos d'Orient; B1-T01)", "Youssouf Kemal to the high commissioners, 15 Sept 1921, USNA RG 59, 860J.4016 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 189)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 408", "Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], p. 117", "Cibran & Çetinoğlu, The Pontos Question (English summary, January 2007) [Turkish writers]", "See also B1-T01, B1-T05", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 408", "Alpaslan 2007; Doğan 2014 (via H6-T08)"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Ankara government statements, 1921", "Turkish sources only: Ankara statements; Topal Osman's biographers"], "main": false, "pending_category": false, "counter_evidence": "The Kavak victims were deportees under escort: survivors and relief workers say the escort fired on the columns (Fuller saw only 200 of the first 900 reach Sivas; a deportee of the third Samsun convoy wrote that '660 were killed' as it descended a mountain; Hopkins reported 550 executed on 26 June and 1,300 on 15 August; Rendel recorded 1,300 shot in the gorge; H7-029, B1-011, B1-013). In the Grand National Assembly the Lazistan deputy Ziya Hurşit said the first convoy was attacked six hours out of Samsun and blamed Nureddin's use of irregular bands (H7-031); Turkish documents summarised by Korucu & Dağlıoğlu (p. 25) say the relocated Greeks were killed by militias, notably those of Topal Osman and Şaki Ali. At Bafra the Greek witness describes men tied together and killed in churches (B1-010, B1-003, B1-004), and US officers reported deportations 'regularly accompanied by mass murder' around Bafra (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 409). The convoy figures from different sources may overlap and should not be added. Greek bands did fight in these hills (H7-063, B1-007), so some deaths in combat are possible, but no non-Turkish source describes the Kavak or Bafra dead as combatants.", "merged_from": ["H7-T05"], "cross_ref": ["H7-T05", "H6-T08"], "overlaps_with": ["H6-T08"]}, {"id": "H7-T06", "date": "1921-07", "date_text": "July 1921", "precision": "month", "title": "Yusuf Kemal: the Merzifon massacre a 'legend'", "summary": "The Ankara foreign minister, Yusuf Kemal, announced that 'it is not exact that the population of Merzifoun has been massacred and dispersed': it was a 'legend'. Later the Nationalists said that 'the troops simply got from under control', or that brigands, not the army, were to blame, and that the offenders had been sent into battle 'and most... were killed' (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 413).", "places": [{"name": "Merzifon", "lat": 40.874, "lon": 35.463, "role": "massacre"}], "people": ["Yusuf Kemal (Tengirşenk)"], "sources_primary": ["Youssouf Kemal to the High Commissioner, 15 Sept 1921, RG 84 vol. 438 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 224); Gillespie to Bristol, 10 Jan 1922, quoting Yusuf Kemal (n. 225)", "Youssouf Kemal to the High Commissioner, 15 Sept 1921, USNA RG 84 vol. 438; Gillespie (US trade commissioner, Ankara) to Bristol, 10 Jan 1922 (via Morris & Ze'evi, nn. 224–225)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 413", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 413"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Ankara government statement, 1921", "Turkish sources only: Ankara government statements, 1921"], "main": false, "pending_category": false, "counter_evidence": "The American missionaries at Merzifon (Hosford, Anthony) and the staff of Anatolia College described the massacre in detail; the Armenian-Greek Section of the British High Commission estimated 950 killed, Bristol and Anthony about 1,200 (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 413, n. 223; H7-027). Ankara's own later explanations (troops out of control; brigands) concede that killings took place.", "merged_from": ["H7-T06"]}, {"id": "H7-T12", "date": "1921-07-16", "date_text": "16 July 1921 (forwarded by Dolci on 25 July)", "precision": "day", "title": "Yusuf Kemal's circular to the foreign representatives: a Greek plot, band massacres and Greek mobilisation", "summary": "In a circular of 16 July 1921 to the High Commissioners of France, Britain, Italy, Japan and the United States, the ministers of Spain, the Netherlands, Sweden and Denmark and the Apostolic Delegate, the Ankara foreign minister Yusuf Kemal wrote that the Greek army was laying waste the regions it occupied; that Greek secret societies, 'among others the famous society PONTOS', had provoked armed risings in the province of Samsun a few weeks earlier to draw Turkish forces from the front; that their bands, supplied from arms depots set up after the 1918 armistice, were massacring all Muslim villagers they found; that the Greek official gazette of 1 July had called up Greeks of the 1920 and 1921 classes, including Ottoman Greeks of Anatolia; and that the British were arming Greek and Armenian bands around Constantinople. The Ankara government declared that responsibility for any reprisals by the Turkish people would fall entirely on the Greeks (Kyriakidis 2026, pp. 75-76 and Text 1, pp. 149-151).", "places": [{"name": "Ankara (Angora)", "lat": 39.933, "lon": 32.86, "role": "issued"}, {"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "alleged armed risings"}], "people": ["Yusuf Kemal (Tengirşenk)", "Angelo Maria Dolci"], "sources_primary": ["Circular of the Ankara Minister of Foreign Affairs, 16 July 1921, transcribed in Dolci to Van Rossum, 25 July 1921, Propaganda Fide, Nuova Serie vol. 692, p. 433rv (printed as Text 1 in Kyriakidis 2026)"], "sources_modern": ["Kyriakidis (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias Press, 2026), pp. 75-76, 149-151"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Ankara government circular (its claims about others)", "Neutral sources: transmitted by the Apostolic Delegate (as the record)"], "main": false, "pending_category": false, "counter_evidence": "Western observers 'uniformly asserted that there was no Pontine rebellion' (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 406). The circular's warning that reprisals would be the Greeks' fault came as the deportations of men and then of women and children were under way (H7-024, H7-026)."}, {"id": "H7-T07", "date": "1921-08-20", "date_end": "1921-09-21", "date_text": "20 August - 21 September 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "The Amasya trials as the suppression of a separatist conspiracy", "summary": "Turkish historiography treats the Independence Tribunals of 1921 at Amasya as the lawful suppression of a separatist conspiracy for an independent Pontus. Ankara's agent in Constantinople, Hamid Bey, told Rumbold in October 1921 that those sentenced to death had engaged in separatist activities and collaborated with Greek forces; the Ankara government's book Pontus Meselesi (1922) presents the tribunal's reasoning in several cases, without naming the defendants, and says that officials who abused the population during weapons searches were punished by the tribunals.", "places": [{"name": "Amasya (Amaseia)", "lat": 40.654, "lon": 35.833, "role": "tribunal and executions"}], "people": ["Emin Bey (Gevecioğlu)"], "sources_primary": ["Tribunal verdicts (as presented in Pontus Meselesi (1922), pp. 381-390)", "Rumbold to Curzon, 15 Oct 1921 (Hamid Bey), DBFP, via Çetinoğlu, 'Pontos'taki Düzmece İstiklal Mahkemeleri ve Yargılamaları' (posted 7 Jan 2020; reprinted in Çilingir 2016, pp. 229ff.) [Turkish writer], pp. 9-10"], "sources_modern": ["Turkish historiography of the Pontus question (see H6-T04)", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), pp. 20-21", "Çetinoğlu, 'Pontos'taki Düzmece İstiklal Mahkemeleri ve Yargılamaları' (posted 7 Jan 2020; reprinted in Çilingir 2016, pp. 229ff.) [Turkish writer]"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Turkish historiography"], "main": false, "pending_category": false, "counter_evidence": "Partly corroborated: Pontic leaders abroad did campaign for a Pontic state, and the tribunal condemned three of them (Chrysanthos, Germanos, Laurentios) in absentia (H6-058); the US High Commissioner Bristol, no friend of the Pontic cause, judged that the condemned had played a part in propaganda for a Greek state fostered by Venizelos, as the Turkish historian Bilgiç reports him (quoted by Çetinoğlu). Against the rest: Bristol allowed that innocents may have been punished with them; the tribunal passed 622 death sentences (137 in absentia) in four months (H6-058); those hanged included teachers and students of Anatolia College, doctors, pharmacists and bankers; the tribunal's archives are still closed and its procedure is known mainly from memoirs and the Turkish government's own publication (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 18; Çetinoğlu); and the hangings drew Greek, British and Allied protests and a speech in the US Senate (H6-058, H7-060)."}, {"id": "B1-T02", "date": "1921-09-15", "date_end": "1922-05-18", "date_text": "15 September 1921 – 18 May 1922", "precision": "day", "title": "Ankara: the deportations were orderly and lawful", "summary": "Yusuf Kemal said that the deportation had gone without serious incident on the roads, with only 'one or two cases of theft' reaching the military courts (Biber). At a secret session on 18 May 1922 the interior minister Fethi told the Assembly that 'no Christians, Greek or Armenian, had been illegally punished' and that any deported 'provisionally from the sea coast' had been moved 'solely in the interests of safeguarding [Turkish] independence'; Mustafa Kemal denounced the 'lying Yowell' and his 'calumnious statement'.", "places": [{"name": "Sivas", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.015, "role": "the roads inland (generic)"}], "people": ["Yusuf Kemal (Tengirşenk)", "Fethi (Okyar)", "Mustafa Kemal"], "sources_primary": ["Statement of Yusuf Kemal (via Biber)", "Grand National Assembly secret session, 18 May 1922 (via Morris & Ze'evi nn. 299-300)"], "sources_modern": ["Biber, ch. 13 in the TÜBA history series, p. 442", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 426"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Ankara government statements"], "counter_evidence": "Contradicted by the Near East Relief officers (Ward and Yowell: 5,000 dead between Sivas and Harput, 3,000 between Harput and Diyarbakır; B1-002), by US naval reports (90 percent of the Bafra men killed, USS Brooks, 8 July 1921; B1-013), by the sighting of 4,000 Bafra women and children near Sivas without food or shoes (B1-017), by Nureddin's own admission that women and children were deported with the men (B1-022), and by the deputies in Ankara who attacked Nureddin.", "cross_ref": ["H7-T04"], "main": false, "overlaps_with": ["H7-T04"]}, {"id": "H7-T10", "date": "1921-12-29", "date_end": "1922-08-21", "date_text": "29 December 1921 - 21 August 1922", "precision": "day", "title": "Ankara's figures for Muslim victims of the Greek bands, and for the bands' arms", "summary": "Ankara's ministers and publications gave figures for Muslim victims. The interior minister Ali Fethi told the Assembly on 29 December 1921 that, in reply to the Central Army's operations against 50-60 bands of 180-200 men in the Nebiyan region and the raids on metropolises and schools, armed Greek groups had killed 1,303 Muslims, wounded 147 and damaged 3,303 dwellings; on 10 June 1922 he reported 69 killed, 51 wounded and 415 structures damaged between 15 October and December 1921, and 82 dead, 26 wounded, 5 kidnapped and 151 dwellings pillaged between January and May 1922 (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, pp. 14-15). The government's book Pontus Meselesi (1922) gave 1,641 Muslims killed and 323 wounded, 3,723 dwellings burned and 1,800 robberies, and said that over 10,000 weapons had been distributed to the Greeks by Tsarist Russia and later Britain, of which only about 2,000 were handed in (ibid., pp. 20-21). The General Staff put the Turks killed by Greek bands in 1916-23 at 1,817 (Ayşe Hür, in Çilingir p. 228).", "places": [{"name": "Nebyan Dağı", "lat": 41.35, "lon": 36.0, "role": "band area named by Fethi (approximate)"}, {"name": "Ankara (Angora)", "lat": 39.933, "lon": 32.86, "role": "Grand National Assembly"}], "people": ["Ali Fethi (Okyar)"], "sources_primary": ["TBMM Zabıt Ceridesi, Term I, vol. XV, 29 Dec 1921, pp. 240-241; TBMM Gizli Zabıt Ceridesi, Term I, vol. III, 10 June 1922, p. 373 (via Korucu & Dağlıoğlu)", "Pontus Meselesi (TBMM Hükümeti Matbuat Müdüriyet-i Umumisi, 1922; facsimile 1995), ch. 2 and 4 (via Korucu & Dağlıoğlu)", "General Staff figure (via Ayşe Hür, reprinted in Çilingir p. 228)"], "sources_modern": ["Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), pp. 14-15, 20-21", "Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], p. 228"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Ankara government statements and publications; General Staff", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Korucu & Dağlıoğlu; Ayşe Hür (transmitters)"], "main": false, "pending_category": true, "counter_evidence": "We found no non-Turkish count of Muslim victims. The figures conflict among themselves (1,303 killed to December 1921; 1,641 in the 1922 book; 1,817 for 1916-23). One band raid with deaths is documented by the US Navy (H7-063). The same ministers' statements admit far larger Greek losses: 5,051 'dead' in four months of 1922 (H7-065) and 11,198 killed by February 1923 (H7-067); by the Turkish figures themselves, Greek deaths in operations exceeded Muslim deaths several times over.", "overlaps_with": ["H6-013"]}, {"id": "H7-T14", "date": "1921-12-30", "date_end": "1922-01-17", "date_text": "30 December 1921 - 17 January 1922", "precision": "day", "title": "Nureddin Pasha's defence: the Greeks a 'snake' whose 'poison' is the women", "summary": "In the two defences he sent to Ankara after his dismissal (30 December 1921 and 3 January 1922), read in the Assembly on 17 January 1922, Nureddin Pasha justified the deportation of women and children: 'All Greeks have the ideal of a state... the Greeks in our country are a snake; the poison of these snakes is the women'; the women had helped their men 'in thought, body and property', so 'we did the same to them as to the men; we did not separate them from their children either'. He cited an old Greek hanged near Bafra for signalling a Greek warship (Balcıoğlu, via Çilingir pp. 126, 130; Korucu & Dağlıoğlu p. 23).", "places": [{"name": "Ankara (Angora)", "lat": 39.933, "lon": 32.86, "role": "defence read in the Assembly"}, {"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "Central Army zone (generic)"}], "people": ["Nureddin Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["Nureddin's defences of 30 Dec 1921 and 3 Jan 1922 (TBMM archives), in Balcıoğlu, İki İsyan (2000/2003), pp. 273-283, via Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], pp. 126, 130, and Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), p. 23", "Nureddin Pasha's statement of defence (Balcıoğlu 2000, pp. 273-275)"], "sources_modern": ["Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], pp. 126, 130", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), p. 23", "See also B1-T03", "Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Turkish-language work), p. 130"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Nureddin's justifications", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Çilingir; Korucu & Dağlıoğlu (transmitters)", "Turkish sources only: Nureddin's justification"], "main": false, "pending_category": true, "counter_evidence": "His admissions (women and children deported with the men; armed local Turks enrolled at Bafra and Kavak) are told in the chapter (B1-022, H7-026). No non-Turkish source describes the women of Bafra as combatants or informers; US officers called their treatment 'an unpardonable sin against civilization' (B1-018), and the Assembly in Ankara itself attacked Nureddin's conduct and removed him (H7-031).", "merged_from": ["H7-T14"], "cross_ref": ["H6-T06"]}, {"id": "H7-T11", "date": "1922", "date_end": "1923", "date_text": "1922 (French translation 1923; facsimile 1995)", "precision": "year", "title": "The Ankara government's case: the book Pontus Meselesi (1922)", "summary": "For the Lausanne talks the Ankara government's press directorate published Pontus Meselesi ('The Pontus Question', 1922; French 1923; modern Turkish facsimile by the Grand National Assembly, 1995). It argues that Anatolia was a Turkish homeland before 1071 and that the Turks had been tolerant; that churches, the Patriarchate, Greek societies and the Pontus organisation (founded at the American College of Merzifon) incited the Christians; it prints documents seized at the metropolises and the college, lists murders and robberies by Greek bands in Bafra, Nebyan, Samsun, Ladik, Gümüşhacıköy, Havza, Çarşamba, Terme, Merzifon, Amasya, Köprü and Tokat, with Muslim casualty figures (H7-T10); and it presents the disarmament call, the 1921 relocation as carried out 'fastidiously', the trial of the 'very small number' of officials who abused it, the tribunals' sentences, Nureddin's orders, and Ankara's replies to American and Allied protests (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, pp. 19-21). Çetinoğlu and Ayşe Hür note that the Turkish official thesis from Kemal's 1927 speech to the present largely repeats this book.", "places": [{"name": "Ankara (Angora)", "lat": 39.933, "lon": 32.86, "role": "published"}], "sources_primary": ["Pontus Meselesi (Ankara: TBMM Hükümeti Matbuat Müdüriyet-i Umumisi, 1922); ed. Yılmaz Kurt (TBMM, 1995)"], "sources_modern": ["Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), pp. 19-21", "Çetinoğlu, 'Pontos'taki Düzmece İstiklal Mahkemeleri ve Yargılamaları' (posted 7 Jan 2020; reprinted in Çilingir 2016, pp. 229ff.) [Turkish writer], p. 1", "Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], p. 121"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Ankara government publication", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Korucu & Dağlıoğlu; Çetinoğlu; Çilingir (describe it)"], "main": false, "pending_category": true, "counter_evidence": "The book's own appendices admit the relocation of women and children (Bristol's protest of 18 July 1921 is printed in it) and the tribunals' death sentences. US, British and relief reports describe the marches as lethal (H7-025, H7-038); Ankara's own deputies accused Nureddin of massacre and pillage (H7-031); its claim that the relocation was orderly is contradicted by the interior minister's figures of Greeks killed (H7-065) and by the Interior Ministry's order of 25 June 1921, which did not cover women and children (H7-026)."}, {"id": "B1-T08", "date": "1922-02", "date_text": "February 1922", "precision": "month", "title": "Yusuf Kemal: women and children deported because of spies and military operations", "summary": "Asked by a journalist in February 1922 what the women and children were guilty of, Yusuf Kemal replied that the military command had judged it necessary to deport them 'so that they do not interfere with military operations', and that 'there are many spies among the Christians, and this is the main cause of the deportations'.", "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "Pontus coast (generic)"}], "people": ["Yusuf Kemal (Tengirşenk)"], "sources_primary": ["Yusuf Kemal to a journalist, Feb 1922 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 243)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 415"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Ankara foreign minister's justification"], "counter_evidence": "He had told Bristol's aide that Ankara had not ordered the deportation of women (Morris & Ze'evi p. 415). The '15 to 50' decree of June 1921 named men only (H7-026). US and NER observers saw women, children and orphans deported in winter without food (B1-017, B1-002, B1-027).", "cross_ref": ["H7-T04"], "main": false, "overlaps_with": ["H7-T04"]}, {"id": "H7-T09", "date": "1994", "date_end": "2026-05-19", "date_text": "1994 - 19 May 2026", "precision": "year", "title": "The Turkish state's position on the term 'genocide'", "summary": "Turkey does not recognise a Pontic or Greek genocide. Its foreign ministry issues a statement each 19 May (e.g. No. 167 of 19 May 2022; No. 95 of 19 May 2026). These call the 'Pontus' allegations baseless; say that Greece promotes them through the 1994 law and school circulars, to cover its defeat in the 'Megali Idea' campaign and its own army's atrocities; and cite the Allied commission reports, Article 59 of the Treaty of Lausanne and Tripolitsa (1821). The ministry objects to Greece's choice of 19 May, a Turkish national day (Kemal's landing at Samsun), and condemned the 1998 law on Asia Minor as 'without any historical basis'. The Turkish Embassy in Australia called the South Australian motion 'the work of an ethnic lobby' (19 June 2009; Kritikakos 2026, p. 191); Morris & Ze'evi (p. 382) trace the official narrative to Kemal's Nutuk (1927).", "places": [{"name": "Ankara (Angora)", "lat": 39.933, "lon": 32.86, "role": "Grand National Assembly"}, {"name": "Athens", "lat": 37.976, "lon": 23.736, "role": "Hellenic Parliament"}], "sources_primary": ["Turkish MFA press release No. 167, 19 May 2022", "Turkish MFA press release No. 95, 19 May 2026", "Turkish MFA statement on Law 2645/1998 (as cited on Wikipedia)"], "sources_modern": ["Kritikakos 2026, p. 191 (Turkish Embassy, 19 June 2009: 'the work of an ethnic lobby')", "Morris & Ze'evi 2019, p. 382 (Kemal's Nutuk, 1927, as the root of the official narrative)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The statements are the official position; what they establish is the position itself, not the facts they assert.", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Turkish Ministry of Foreign Affairs statements"], "main": false, "pending_category": false, "counter_evidence": "Partly corroborated: the Greek army's atrocities in western Anatolia, which the statements cite, were documented by the Inter-Allied commission (Yalova, 1921), but they were not in the Pontus. Against the rest: the deportation orders of 1916-17 and 1921 are Ottoman and Ankara documents (H6-026, H7-011, H7-026, H7-055); the Ottoman Empire's own allies reported the burnings and deaths of 1916-17 (Kwiatkowski, Pallavicini, the German consulate at Samsun); US naval and Near East Relief officers reported the marches of 1921-22; Turkish deputies in Ankara attacked Nureddin in 1921 (H7-031) and the interior minister reported thousands of Greeks killed in 1922 (H7-065); the term genocide is used by the IAGS (2007), Greece (1994), Armenia (2015), Cyprus (2025), the Swedish Riksdag (2010), South Australia (2009), Tasmania (2023) and others. The Turkish Ministry of Foreign Affairs condemned the New South Wales Legislative Council's motion of May 2013 (press release No. 133, 7 May 2013; Kritikakos p. 273 n. 79)."}, {"id": "H7-D02", "date": "2007", "date_end": "2020", "date_text": "2007-2020", "precision": "year", "title": "Sait Çetinoğlu's theses", "summary": "Sait Çetinoğlu, a Turkish writer who argues for recognition, uses Ottoman, Turkish and Greek documents. His main theses in the texts read: the Pontic genocide continued from the Unionists to the Kemalists, with the same methods and some of the same men (Topal Osman; the Special Organization) ('TC'nin soykırımlar geleneği', pp. 14-16); the 1916 decision to clear a 50-km strip behind the front was part of a German-Unionist plan to homogenise Anatolia and seize Christian wealth (The Pontos Question, 2007); the Independence Tribunals of the Pontus were 'sham' execution bodies whose archives are still closed, and the official Turkish thesis repeats what he calls 'the 1922 propaganda book', Pontus Meselesi ('Düzmece İstiklal Mahkemeleri', pp. 1-10); the resistance in the western Pontus consisted of self-defence groups (ibid., p. 4); and official documents record 11,188 Greeks killed (ibid., p. 10). Entries that use him as a source include H7-012, H7-022, H7-026, H7-029, H7-057, H7-060 and H7-067.", "people": ["Sait Çetinoğlu"], "sources_modern": ["Çetinoğlu, 'Pontos'taki Düzmece İstiklal Mahkemeleri ve Yargılamaları' (posted 7 Jan 2020; reprinted in Çilingir 2016, pp. 229ff.) [Turkish writer]", "Çetinoğlu, 'T.C.'nin soykırımlar geleneği ve Pontos soykırımı' [Turkish writer]", "Çetinoğlu, 'Germanos'tan Konstantinidés'e Tarihi Mektup ve Pontos Soykırımı' [Turkish writer]", "Cibran & Çetinoğlu, The Pontos Question (English summary, January 2007) [Turkish writers]"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "turkish_dissident", "basis_detail": ["Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Çetinoğlu"], "main": false, "pending_category": true}, {"id": "H7-D01", "date": "2016", "date_text": "2016", "precision": "year", "title": "Tamer Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği (2016): a Turkish writer's theses", "summary": "Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 ('The Pontus Truth', Belge, 2016) argues for recognition of a Pontic Greek genocide. Its main claims: (1) the persecution of the Pontic Greeks by the Unionists (1908-18) and the Kemalists (1919-23) was one continuous, planned genocide, which official Turkish history splits in two in order to exonerate the founders of the Republic (pp. 6-7); (2) 353,000 Pontic Greeks were killed, the figure he repeats throughout (pp. 7, 19, 66); (3) the 1916 deportations were a Turkish-German plan disguised as a military measure (pp. 54-57); (4) the Central Army organised the deportations, used irregular bands and labour battalions, and plundered Greek property (pp. 112-126); (5) Ankara's own record, the secret sessions, the 1922 book Pontus Meselesi and Nureddin's defence, documents the genocide (pp. 117-130); (6) the descendants of Islamised Pontic Greeks today make up much of the Black Sea population, and Turkish nationalism on the coast is a reaction to that lost identity (pp. 6, 17-18, 174 ff.). He relies heavily on Turkish archival documents (ATASE, BOA, TBMM minutes), which is why this chapter cites him for those documents. The Turkish official claims he reproduces are given under their authors (H7-T05, H7-T08, H7-T10, H7-T14).", "people": ["Tamer Çilingir"], "sources_modern": ["Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], pp. 1-7, 50-57, 112-130, 226-229"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "turkish_dissident", "basis_detail": ["Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Çilingir 2016"], "main": false, "pending_category": true, "counter_evidence": "His 353,000 is Valavanis's figure (H7-043), itself built on the 1922 Black Book's recycled deportation counts (H7-034), and he treats it as established; his population arithmetic (e.g. 'at least 90,000 Greeks of Canik destroyed', p. 121) is his own reckoning, not a source."}, {"id": "B1-T06", "date": "2023", "date_text": "1921 (as treated in Turkish historiography)", "precision": "year", "title": "Turkish historiography: the Bafra and Kavak figures are Greek propaganda", "summary": "Biber treats the Black Book's convoy figures for Samsun and Bafra (2,000; 1,901; 2,000; 500; 620; 330 and 660 killed at Kavak) as exaggerated and unreliable propaganda, and notes that Turkish archival detail on the convoys has not been found.", "places": [{"name": "Kavak", "lat": 41.079, "lon": 36.044, "role": "disputed killings"}, {"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.903, "role": "disputed figures"}], "sources_modern": ["Biber, ch. 13 in the TÜBA history series, pp. 437, 440, 443"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Biber (TÜBA) assessment"], "counter_evidence": "Partly fair: the Black Book is an advocacy work and its Bafra narrative rests on one witness (B1-010). But its Kavak figures are matched by a deportee's letter (660 in the third convoy), by Fuller (700 of 900 missing), by Hopkins (550 on 26 June, 1,300 on 15 August) and by Rendel (1,300) (B1-013, B1-011); US naval records confirm the deportation of thousands of Bafra women and children (B1-015, B1-018); and the Central Army's own count of 63,844 moved (B1-023) is of the same order as the Greek figures for those deported.", "cross_ref": ["H6-T08"], "main": false, "overlaps_with": ["H6-T08"]}], "synthesis": "## The evidence\n\nThe destruction of the Pontic Greeks came in two phases, under two governments and in two wars: from 1914 to 1918 under the Committee of Union and Progress, at war with Russia, and from 1919 to 1923 under the nationalist government at Ankara, at war with Greece. For each phase this chapter sets the evidence in five groups. First come outside observers at the time: diplomats of the Ottoman Empire's allies, Austria-Hungary and Germany, and of neutral states; later the American relief workers and naval officers, the British Foreign Office and High Commission, the French commissioner at Trebizond, and the Catholic clergy and Vatican diplomats whose reports Kyriakidis has published. Second come the Ottoman and Turkish documents. What they admit the state did is told in the chapter: orders, numbers moved, numbers killed, sentences, and the condemnation of officials; their justifications and their claims about others are given separately at the end, unless a non-Turkish source confirms them. Third come the survivors' testimonies. Fourth come the Greek compilations of 1919–1925, by the Patriarchate, the Central Council of Pontus and Valavanis, which were made to argue a political case. Fifth come the Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Sait Çetinoğlu, Tamer Çilingir, Ayşe Hür, and the researchers Serdar Korucu and Emre Can Dağlıoğlu, who matter above all because they transcribe Ottoman and Turkish documents.\n\n## The first phase, 1914–1918\n\n**The labour battalions.** From August 1914 most Christian men were drafted into unarmed labour battalions (*amele taburları*) (Zürcher 2002). A postwar Greek report gives 23 survivors of some 3,000 labour conscripts from Ayvalık, another a death toll of 80 percent at İslahiye, and Colonel Rawlinson, a British officer held hostage in the east, judged that \"the life of a Greek in a labor gang is generally about two months\" (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 387). Many Pontic men deserted to the mountains, and some formed bands. Morris & Ze'evi write that during the war \"Greek armed bands—composed chiefly of youngsters avoiding the draft—occasionally attacked Turkish villagers and gendarmes\", and that \"the Turks charged that they were being armed and reinforced from Russia\" (p. 383). They cite no particular source, but it is the first non-Turkish statement we have found of band attacks on Muslims in 1914–18, and the Greek historian Agtzidis (1992, p. 167) also names \"Russian aid\" among the bands' sources of arms (H6-009, H7-063). The details of the raids rest on Turkish sources alone and are given at the end.\n\n**What the government said it intended.** On 31 January 1916, according to an Austrian report cited by Fotiadis, Talat told an Austrian agent that \"he sees the increased necessity for Turkey to get rid of Greeks, as she did before with Armenians\" (Kyriakidis 2026, p. 50). In October 1916 he told the German ambassador Radowitz that a wholesale deportation of the Greeks from the coasts had been planned earlier in the year but stopped by Enver so as not to provoke Greece; Radowitz reported that the \"cleansing\" had been partly accomplished (Hofmann 2011, p. 59).\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch09_deportations.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch09_deportations.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1120\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map of the Pontus west of the front showing the places from which Greeks were deported in 1915–17, with dates, the inland destinations named in the sources, and the bands of deserters in the mountains.\"></a><figcaption>The deportations of 1915–17 from the Ottoman-held coast: the places named in the sources with their dates, the destinations, and the bands of deserters in the mountains. The lines show direction only; most of the routes are not known. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\n**The deportations.** The Russians held Trebizond and the east from 18 April 1916 to 23 February 1918, so the Greeks east of the front were not deported in 1916–17. A Capuchin missionary recorded that conditions for Christians at Trebizond improved under Russian rule (Kyriakidis 2026, p. 57). West of the front the removals went as follows.\n\n| When | From | To | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| December 1915 | the Ünye district | not stated | Morris & Ze'evi, p. 389, after the US official Jackson (1918) |\n| January–April 1916 | Greek villages behind the receding front, in winter, on the orders of the Third Army commander Kâmil Pasha | inland | Morris & Ze'evi, p. 389, after a missionary letter from Erzurum |\n| June 1916 | İnebolu and its villages | the interior | Morris & Ze'evi, pp. 389–390, after the Patriarchate |\n| 3 July 1916 | Görele, Vakfıkebir and Şarlı (\"for espionage\") | Sivas, via Giresun and Samsun | Talat's cipher to the vali of Sivas (Çilingir, p. 51, from Çetinoğlu's collection) |\n| July 1916 | Sinope and the Kastamonu coast | the interior | German consular telegram |\n| November 1916 – February 1917 | Tirebolu, Espiye, Giresun, Samsun, Bafra, Çarşamba, Ordu, Ünye | Şebinkarahisar, Amasya, Tokat, Sivas, the Ankara district | Pallavicini; Greek reports; Morris & Ze'evi, pp. 390–391; Hofmann, p. 60 |\n| 10, 11 and 13 January 1917 | Samsun town, mostly women and children | inland | Morris & Ze'evi, p. 391 |\n| 11 and 21 January 1917 (orders) | \"the Greeks along the coast\"; the Samsun basin | 30–50 km inland; villages of Sivas, Kastamonu, Ankara and Bolu | Talat's cable and the Security Directorate's circular (Akçam 2012, p. 111) |\n\nAfter the Russians withdrew in February 1918, the same Capuchin missionary's diary records orders \"from Constantinople\" to deport all the Greeks of Trebizond, women and children included, and Turkish irregulars burning and killing in the villages around the city; many Greeks fled to Batum (pp. 62–63). Whether that order was carried out is not recorded.\n\n**The orders.** A cable to Trebizond of 15 May 1916 had limited removals to places judged militarily unsafe (Akçam 2012, pp. 106–107) (H6-014). In December 1916 Enver ordered the Greek population of the Black Sea coast removed from a strip 50 km deep, \"for allegedly strictly military reasons\", in Hofmann's words (2011, pp. 59–60); the Metropolitan of Kerasous was told of a 50-km evacuation within one month, through the German embassy and again by Vehip Pasha on 20 November 1916 (Emmanouilidis' memoir, via Çilingir, p. 54). On 11 January 1917 Talat cabled the Samsun district that, by Enver's word, the Third Army had made the arrangements to take \"the Greeks along the coast some thirty to fifty kilometers inland\", without harm to persons or property (BOA DH.ŞFR 71/234). On 21 January the Security Directorate told Sivas, Kastamonu, Ankara and Bolu that the Third Army judged it urgent to move the Greeks of the Samsun basin into villages in those provinces. Both are quoted by Akçam (2012, pp. 111–112), who adds that the order was not carried out as written (H7-011); in 2023 he gave the Russian advance as the stated reason for the deportations of late 1916 from the Samsun and Trebizond coasts. The instructions of 3 February 1917 to Canik left the choice to the army and extended the removals to the townspeople, while Muslim refugees were settled in the emptied villages and Greek property was to be kept, not liquidated (Akçam 2012, pp. 113, 118–119) (AK-003).\n\n**How many were moved, and how many died.** The Ottoman counts come first. Refet Bey reported 13,406 Greeks sent inland from Samsun (BOA DH.ŞFR 548/1, March 1917, via Biber), and Talat estimated in late 1917 that more than 93,000 Ottoman Greeks had been deported to the interior during the war and 164,000 to Greece (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 392). A table \"according to official sources\" printed by Çilingir (p. 53) gives 62,000 deported from Canik and 29 from Trabzon in 1914–17, but names no source.\n\nThe Greek reports are larger. For the Kerasous district the Greek Legation gives 88 villages emptied and burned and about 30,000 people sent to the Ankara district, \"one-fourth\" of whom died on the road (21 April 1917); 38 coastal villages and 23,000 people (7 February 1917); and 22,356 people to January 1917, village by village (Brown & Ion 1918, pp. 13, 47, 67). For the Samsun district it gives over 20,000 deported by a rough estimate, then 30,000 by February, a quarter of them said to be dead already (pp. 52, 55, 68). The Greek government's total for the whole empire by 1917 was about 240,000 deported from the districts its consuls could watch (p. 64). German diplomats confirmed that the Greeks of Giresun, promised they could stay at Kulakkaya, were driven towards Sivas in midwinter (Hofmann, p. 60). The Metropolitan of Trebizond wrote that over 90 percent of the deportees from Rize and Tripolis did not survive; Hofmann gives 550 survivors of 16,750 inhabitants of the Elevi and Tripolis area, without a source (p. 62). Germanos Karavangelis, Metropolitan of Amaseia, reported about 30,000 from his diocese sent towards Ankara, most of whom died, and wrote in 1918 that \"out of 160,000 people of Pontus deported, only a tenth and in some places a twentieth have survived\" (Hofmann, p. 102, after the *New York Times*). An American inquiry of 1919 by Ralph Chesbrough found that about 5,000 Greeks and as many Armenians had been \"eliminated\" from Samsun \"by massacre, expulsion, and flight to the hills\"; Morris & Ze'evi add that Samsun's Turks also suffered heavy wartime losses (p. 391).\n\n**The witnesses.** The Austrian consul at Samsun, Ernst von Kwiatkowski, reported at the end of November 1916 what the commander Rafet (Refet) Bey had told him. Its English versions differ. In one: \"We must at last do with the Greeks as we did with the Armenians…\" (26 November) and \"We must now finish with the Greeks. I sent today battalions to the outskirts to kill every Greek they pass on the road\" (28 November). Kyriakidis (p. 50), after Enepekidis (1996, p. 139), dates the report 29 November and renders it: \"We must at last do away with the Greeks. Today I sent patrols of gendarmes to the outskirts ordering them to kill any Greek they would come across.\" The German original has not been seen. The Austrian ambassador Pallavicini's report of 19 December 1916, continued on 2 January 1917, lists five villages plundered and burned and their people sent away on 11 December, villages around the city on fire on the 12th, whole villages burning with their schools and churches on the 14th, eleven villages burned in the Samsun province by the 17th, and by the 31st about eighteen villages burned to the ground and fifteen in part, with about sixty women raped (Shirinian 2017, p. 52). On 20 January 1917 he is quoted as writing that \"the situation of the deported is for despair. Death awaits them all\", and that he had warned the Grand Vizier the persecution could take \"the form and dimensions of the Armenian persecutions\"; this letter is known only in a website's translation, which Çetinoğlu repeats.\n\nIn mid-July 1916 the German consulate at Samsun telegraphed that \"the entire Greek population of Sinope and the coastal area of the district of Kastanome has been exiled\". In Hofmann's English: \"In Turkish the terms deportation and destruction have the same meaning, for in most cases those who are not killed fall victim to diseases or starvation\", and the vali of Kastamonu used the flight of conscripts and espionage \"as a pretext to annihilate the entire people\" (2011, p. 57); in German, *\"Ausweisung und Ausrottung sind türkisch gleiche Begriffe\"*. Who sent it, and when, is not settled: Hofmann's text says Vice-consul Kuckhoff on 16 July, her footnote \"telegram no. 129, 15 July 1916, by Heinrich Bergfeld, Consulate Samsun\" (after Fotiadis); Shirinian (p. 52) and Kyriakidis (p. 62) give 16 July. Bergfeld, consul at Trebizond until April 1916, may have moved to Samsun as Kwiatkowski did; only the German Foreign Office file can decide it.\n\nAmong the Americans, Morgenthau's often-quoted passage concerns Thrace and the Aegean in 1914–15, not the Pontus. The missionary Mary Graffam at Sivas received Germanos' report of December 1916 (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 391), and a US naval officer reported in 1919 that the Bafra deportees of 1916–17 had been taken from hot baths and marched \"in the dead of winter, with very little clothing and generally without food\" (p. 390).\n\n**What the Ottoman documents admit.** Enver's 50-km order of December 1916; Talat's cipher of 3 July 1916 on Görele, Vakfıkebir and Şarlı; his cable of 11 January 1917; the instructions of January–March 1917 on the emptied villages and Greek property; Refet's 13,406; and Talat's instruction of 22 May 1917 to split suspect deportees into small groups (BOA, via Çilingir, p. 51).\n\n**The Ottoman parliament, 4 November 1918.** The motion of the Greek deputies Emmanouilidis, Tokinidis and Vangel, as Çilingir transcribes it from the minutes (p. 109), says that after the war began \"550,000 more Greeks were killed and annihilated (*katl ve imha edilmiş*)\" on the coasts of the Black Sea, the Dardanelles, the Marmara and the islands; it also cites 250,000 Greeks expelled before the war and 250,000 dead in the labour battalions. Hofmann (p. 102, after Akçam 1996) adds that Turkish deputies called the figures exaggerated while admitting losses of 500,000–600,000. It is a deputies' claim for all the coastal Greeks, not a count for the Pontus.\n\n## The second phase, 1919–1923\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch09_ankara.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch09_ankara.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1120\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map of the Pontus from Zonguldak to Trebizond showing the places of killings, deportations, trials and attacks named for 1919–1923.\"></a><figcaption>Under the Ankara government, 1919–1923: the places of killings, deportations, trials and attacks, with the dates the sources give. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\n**The setting.** The Greek army landed at Smyrna on 15 May 1919; Mustafa Kemal landed at Samsun on 19 May. Pontic leaders sought a Pontic state at Paris, and in March 1920 they put the Greeks of the Pontus at 850,000, including a quarter of a million exiles in Russia and the Caucasus, while \"Western diplomats thought the real number was more like 450,000\" (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 382). Morris & Ze'evi judge that the separatist agitation produced \"almost no acts of overt rebellion\" and \"very little anti-Turk terrorism\", that most Ottoman Greeks \"remained unmoved by ethnic-nationalist appeals\", and that Metropolitan Chrysanthos opposed separatism (p. 383).\n\nPaul Lépissier, the French High Commission's delegate at Trebizond from November 1918, had only a few gendarmerie officers with him. His reports, studied by Georganopoulos (2023, pp. 75–84), record armed Muslims obstructing the return of Greek refugees from Russia and killing returnees, 600 Pontians prevented from landing in May 1919, only 5,000–6,000 Greeks and 300 Armenians back in the Trebizond area by June 1919, and \"organised persecutions\" around Kerasous, Merzifon and Bulancak from 4 June 1919. The Orthodox, Protestant and Catholic leaders of Trebizond protested jointly to him about raids on Christian villages (Kyriakidis 2026, pp. 63–64). He was forced out on Ankara's orders in 1920; a French envoy thought he \"greatly dramatized the events\" (Georganopoulos, p. 84). In October 1919 the Apostolic Delegate, Mgr Dolci, still judged Christian fears \"excessive\" (Kyriakidis, p. 66): not every observer expected what followed.\n\n**Topal Osman.** On 13 August 1920 Topal Osman imprisoned the Christian men of Giresun and had \"five or six\" shot each evening until a ransom of 300,000 lira was paid (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 401, after British records); a Capuchin report blames his \"approximately six hundred Laz\" for many assassinations (Kyriakidis, pp. 66–67). In October 1920 Kemal made him governor general of the coast from İnebolu to Hopa (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 404). His men led the killing at Merzifon in July 1921, and on 15 February 1922 they herded about 170 women and children and a few men into two houses above Ordu and burned them, and treated nine villages alike, according to a survivor's account forwarded by an American missionary (p. 416). In May 1922 the Capuchin superior at Trebizond wrote that at Giresun Osman killed and looted unpunished, \"supported by the Turkish government and honoured by the Muslim population\" (Kyriakidis, p. 82). Rıza Nur, health minister at Ankara, wrote in his memoirs that he told Osman to \"clean the Pontus well\" and to leave no stone on stone in the Greek villages, churches included (as quoted by Çetinoğlu, p. 15, and by Hür, in Çilingir, p. 227).\n\n**The Central Army and its orders.** Ankara decided on 9 December 1920 to create the Central Army at Amasya, about 15,000 men under Nureddin Pasha, for the \"Pontus question\" and the Koçgiri rising. It arrested Samsun notables from January 1921, raided the metropolis of Samsun and the American college at Merzifon on 16 February 1921, and sent suspects to the Sivas Independence Tribunal, which punished 454 Greeks between October 1920 and March 1921 (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019, pp. 12, 16, 19, 24, after Aybars and Balcıoğlu). Nureddin's circular No. 2082 of 12 January 1921 is read three ways: as the deportation of Christian men aged 16–50 from Samsun, Ordu, Amasya, Tokat and Şarkikarahisar with the conscription of the class of 1901 (Çilingir, p. 117); as the gathering of recruits at Şarkikarahisar and Amasya (Çetinoğlu); or as deportation to Amasya, Tokat and Şebinkarahisar (Güzel). To conscript younger boys, orphans' birth certificates were forged (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 404). In March 1921 labour battalions of local Christians were formed at Samsun, Havza, Merzifon, Sivas, Tokat and Çorum (army documents, via Çilingir, pp. 112–114). For the spring operation in the Nebyan mountains the orders were \"to chasten the Greeks so they never again attempt banditry and to destroy villages which serve as bandit shelters\"; the deputy governor of Samsun proposed expelling the people of all the Greek villages, so that the Greeks would nowhere be a majority, and settling Turks in their place (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, pp. 24–25). The villages around Bafra and Alaçam were burned in April and May 1921 (B1-001).\n\n**The orders of June 1921.** The defence minister Fevzi told the Central Army on 3 June that the government had decided to move armed Greeks inland. The Council of Ministers' order of 12 June moved the Greeks aged 15 to 50 and took effect on 16 June (decree No. 941), and the coast was declared a war zone from 12 June (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, pp. 12–13; Çilingir, pp. 117–118; Biber). Nureddin's order No. 2245 of 19 June sent the men to Ergani Madeni, Malatya and Maraş, and to Gürün and Darende. On 16 June the police began to round up the men of Samsun, and on the 19th about 2,000 were marched inland (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 410). An Interior Ministry order of 25 June did not recommend sending women and children inland; on Nureddin's orders they were sent anyway (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 25). Seventy Turkish notables of Samsun telegraphed Kemal that it was \"against their religion to massacre\" women and children, and the mufti issued a fatwa against it; Kemal answered that exiling Samsun's Greeks was \"a merciful act\" (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 410), and Nureddin put the 56 petitioners under town arrest. The Central Army's records count 63,844 Greeks moved inland by November 1921; a breakdown from the same records gives 58,974, of whom 27,995 came from Samsun, 14,000 from Amasya, 8,500 from Giresun and 4,910 from Ordu, with smaller numbers from Sivas, Tokat, Çorum and Sinope (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 25).\n\n**The road to Kavak.** The escorts fired on the men's convoys on the road to Kavak. Rendel's memorandum of 20 March 1922 recorded 1,300 men shot in the gorge on 15 or 16 August, and the Greek delegate to the League put the dead of at least two convoys at \"at least 900\" (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 408); the Black Book gives 330 and 660 for two convoys, and Stanley Hopkins of the Near East Relief reported 550 shot on 26 June and 1,300 on 15 August (Kyriakidis 2023, p. 51). An Ottoman report of 2 June 1921 already admits that a convoy of 766 from Samsun had been attacked in the Canik mountains with many dead, and blames \"brigands\" (Çilingir, p. 117); in the Assembly a Turkish deputy said that the first convoy was attacked six hours out of Samsun (H7-031); and Turkish documents summarised by Korucu & Dağlıoğlu say the deportees were killed by the militias of Topal Osman and Şaki Ali (p. 25).\n\n**Merzifon.** From 23–24 July 1921 Osman's men spent four days killing and pillaging, followed by days of violence by local men with gendarmes and troops. Men and boys sheltering in the French school were shot, about 400 Armenian houses were burned, and the Greek quarter was spared (Morris & Ze'evi, pp. 411–413). Of 2,000–2,500 Christians \"almost all the men were killed\". The toll is put at \"more than a thousand\" (p. 411), at 950 by the British and about 1,200 by Bristol and the missionary Gertrude Anthony (p. 413), and at \"maybe 1,000 or 1,500\" by Anthony elsewhere (Shenk, p. 203).\n\n**Trebizond.** Early in April 1921 the notables, Kapetanidis and Kofidis among them, were arrested and the men deported, while frightened Greeks sheltered in the Catholic monastery (Kyriakidis, p. 75, after the Capuchin Father Cirillo). In August 1921, according to two refugees questioned by the British, 500 or more men summoned for \"fortification work\" were deported in groups of fifty and many were shot (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 423). In May 1922 the British High Commissioner Rumbold reported \"the whole Greek male population from the age of 15 upwards\" being deported (p. 419); the journalist Gibbons found Trebizond with \"half a dozen priests and ten other men and boys\", the boys of 11 to 14 being seized, and named the mayor and the vali as opponents of the persecution (p. 420); and the Capuchin superior wrote that convoys left every week for the interior by way of Erzurum and that most of their people died (Kyriakidis, p. 82).\n\n**Samsun and the coast, 1922.** About 1,324 Greeks, mostly women and children, were deported from Samsun on 10 or 11 April 1922 and 1,462 on 15 April (Morris & Ze'evi, pp. 417–418). The Capuchin superior reported on 8 and 14 May 1922 that at Samsun, İnebolu and Giresun only the old, the women and the children were left, and that no Greek villages remained inland (Kyriakidis, pp. 82–83). At Zonguldak, west of the Pontus, a Catholic missionary's note records about 750 Greek men and boys deported from November 1921 (pp. 159–160).\n\n**The Amasya trials.** The Samsun Independence Tribunal began work on 17 August 1921 and sat mostly at Amasya. According to Aybars, who saw its dockets, it passed 1,163 sentences, 622 of them death sentences (137 in absentia), before its records stop on 27 December 1921 (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 18). The counts of those hanged at Amasya differ: 155 names in the list of the Samsun newspaper *Ehali* of 25 September 1921 (Vergeti), or 132 (Çilingir, pp. 123–126); about 250 or 168 hanged in September 1921 (two reports, Morris & Ze'evi, pp. 416–417); \"more than sixty\" executed and 44 sentenced to long prison terms (Bristol, 19 October 1921, via Çetinoğlu); 177 executed and 74 sentenced to death in absentia by 10 October 1921 by the tribunal's own records (Aybars, via Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, pp. 17–18), or 177 hanged and 44 sentenced in absentia (Tsirkinidis); 72 hanged in the square of Amasya on 8/21 September 1921, one day in the two calendars (Kyriakidis, p. 79); and 485 executed by the Samsun court (Shaw, via Morris & Ze'evi, and Tepeyran's 485 death sentences). Among the dead were Matthaios Kofidis, Nikos Kapetanidis, teachers and students of Anatolia College, many doctors, pharmacists, bankers and merchants of Samsun, and the archimandrite Platon Aivazidis (whom Morris & Ze'evi wrongly call \"Metropolitan of Samsun\", p. 417); the assistant bishop Euthymios of Zela, already dead in prison, was hanged after his death. Admiral Bristol, no friend of the Pontic cause, judged that the condemned had played a part in propaganda for a Greek state on the Black Sea fostered by Venizelos, while allowing that innocent men may have been punished with them (via Bilgiç and Çetinoğlu).\n\n**Protests.** Bristol telegraphed Kemal on 18 July 1921 asking him to stop the expulsion of the women, children and old people of Samsun. The French, British and Italian representatives, Fourcade, Rumbold and Garroni, telegraphed Ankara their concern about the deportations on 22 September 1921; both telegrams are printed in Ankara's own book, *Pontus Meselesi* (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 21). In October the Allied High Commissioners protested the deportations and executions (Rumbold to Curzon, 15 October 1921, via Çetinoğlu). Pope Benedict XV, through the Apostolic Delegate, telegraphed Kemal on 18 August 1921 asking him to cancel the exile of 5,000 women and children near Samsun (Kyriakidis, p. 79).\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch10_marches.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch10_marches.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1360\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map of Anatolia from Samsun and Trebizond to Diyarbakır and Aleppo showing the main road of the deportations of 1921–22, a Bafra convoy of women and children, the other destinations of the order of 19 June 1921 and the road of the survivors to Aleppo.\"></a><figcaption>The roads of the deportations, 1921–22: from Samsun by Kavak, Havza, Amasya, Tokat and Sivas to Malatya, Harput and Diyarbakır, a convoy of women and children from Bafra, the men of Trebizond sent by Erzurum, and the survivors who reached Aleppo, with the numbers the sources give. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\n**The marches.** The route ran from Samsun by Kavak, Havza, Amasya, Tokat, Sivas, Malatya and Harput to Diyarbakır and Bitlis. An estimated 70,000 Pontic deportees passed through Sivas (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 421). The Near East Relief officers Yowell and Ward reported the losses along the way (p. 425):\n\n| Stage | Deportees | Dead | Escaped or left behind |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Passed Sivas, early summer 1921 | 30,000 | | |\n| Sivas to Harput | | 5,000 | 5,000 escaped |\n| Reached Harput | 20,000 | 2,000 died there | 3,000 scattered in the province |\n| Sent on to Diyarbakır | 15,000 | 3,000 on the way, 1,000 in the town | 2,000 remained |\n| Sent on towards Bitlis | 9,000 | | |\n\nWard recorded 20,526 deportees through Mezre-Harput from May 1921 to February 1922, about 18,000 of them Greeks (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 426; his pamphlet gives 20,378). Yowell and Ward reported that Turkish officials \"were frank in their statements that it was the intention to have all the Greeks die\" (p. 425), and Rumbold, citing Ward, wrote that \"the Turks appear to be working on a deliberate plan to get rid of minorities\" (p. 419). Ethel Thompson wrote that \"the Turks had given them no food on the 500 mile trip from Samsun\" (p. 422). There were exceptions: the vali of Diyarbakır let the relief workers shelter and feed Greek deportees, and in October–December 1921 allowed some to settle and work (p. 424). Bristol called the Yowell–Ward reports a British \"intrigue\" and worked to discredit them (p. 427). Rendel's Foreign Office memorandum of 28 December 1921 estimated \"well over 35,000\" Greeks deported from the Pontus that year, the rest \"massacred\" (p. 415). His often-quoted \"over 500,000 Greeks were deported, of whom comparatively few survived\" (his memoranda of 28 December 1921 and 20 March 1922) refers to the First World War and to all the Ottoman Greeks, not to the Pontus (Hofmann, p. 103; Morris & Ze'evi, p. 385).\n\n**What the Turkish documents admit, 1921–23.** Besides the orders and the Central Army's counts given above, there are the Assembly debates of October 1921. On 5 and 29 October the Lazistan deputies Ziya Hurşit and Osman Bey accused Nureddin of deporting women and children beyond his orders, of killings in Samsun before foreign witnesses, and of driving Greeks into the mountains with irregular bands; the Trabzon deputy Hafız Mehmet said he had seen a battalion of the Giresun regiment loot and burn villages. Nureddin was removed on 3 November 1921 for, among other things, using irregulars, taking a bribe from a Pontic Greek, ordering open plunder during the deportations and \"harming the Muslims by setting the stage for the Pontians to take to the mountains\"; Kemal blocked his trial (Çilingir, pp. 118–120; Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, pp. 13–14). The Pontus, not only Koçgiri, was at the centre of the debate. On 10 June 1922 the interior minister Fethi told a secret session that his measures \"to cleanse these Greeks\" had disposed of 13,175 people up to 15 May 1922, 3,388 surrendered, 5,051 dead, 1,592 captured and 3,144 wounded; that destroying their food stores and \"starving them is the most effective measure\"; and that the Samsun authorities reported that the remaining Greeks would not reach even five percent of the Muslims (Çilingir, p. 121; Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 15, with the same totals for a period starting on 29 December 1921). By the end of the operations in February 1923, 11,198 Greeks had been killed and 10,886 had surrendered (Turkish military records, via Balcıoğlu, in Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 25); the General Staff history gives 11,188 (via Çetinoğlu). In September 1923 Refet Pasha told the Apostolic Delegate that \"Latin Catholics have nothing to fear... As for Greeks and Armenians, they will have to pay the price for their sins\" (Kyriakidis, pp. 100–101). A British compilation of October 1921, from Greek and Armenian information, counted 420 of 720 Greek villages \"burnt or destroyed\" (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 416).\n\n**Trebizond's last Greeks.** After the vali Kâzım Bey, married to a Catholic and friendly to Christians, was removed on 11 December 1922, his successor İhsan ordered the Greeks and Armenians out within fifteen days; the Orthodox clergy left on 17 January 1923, and Turkish ships came twice a week (Kyriakidis, p. 85, after Father Cirillo). Father Cirillo wrote on 22 December 1922 of \"systematic deportations organized by Kemal's government\", about 5,000 people gone and three times as many still waiting; he was arrested and expelled in March 1923 for helping the Greeks (pp. 90–94).\n\n**The other side.** Greek bands were active in the mountains. Non-Turkish sources confirm that they existed and that at least one attacked a Muslim village: the destroyer *McFarland* recorded a band of nearly a hundred attacking a Turkish village eight miles from Samsun in early September 1921, killing villagers and burning houses, with a similar raid in October (Shenk, p. 201). Colonel Rawlinson put the armed Greeks in the mountains at 20,000–30,000 in May 1922 (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 415), and Agtzidis at more than 18,000 at the peak, about 1920 (1992, p. 167); Agtzidis also writes of clashes between Muslims and Christians after the armistice with \"hundreds of victims\" (2005, p. 184) (H7-063). No non-Turkish figure for Muslim victims has been found. Greek sources honour the bands as defenders; the last of them left for Poti in December 1923. The Greek navy shelled İnebolu on 9 June 1921, with \"no casualties\", in what an Italian witness called \"an excuse for the massacres\" (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 407), and Samsun on 7 June 1922. Ankara's case also cites the Greek army's atrocities in western Anatolia; these were real, as the Inter-Allied commission on the Yalova peninsula found in 1921, but they were not in the Pontus.\n\n**The survivors' testimonies** are kept in the Centre for Asia Minor Studies' *Η Έξοδος*, by the Committee for Pontic Studies, and in the archive of the Pontic National Council (Chatzikyriakidis 2023), and in memoirs such as Kulehoris' account of the Amasya trials (summarised by Çetinoğlu) and Gavriilidis' of Bafra (B1-028). George E. White's memoir (1940) has not been consulted; Morris & Ze'evi use his letters.\n\n## Bafra and its region, 1921–1924\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch10_bafra.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch10_bafra.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1120\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map of the country between Bafra, Samsun and Havza showing the area where about 100 villages were destroyed from 17 May 1921, the roads of the men and of the women and children, and the places of the killings.\"></a><figcaption>Bafra and its region in 1921: the area of the villages destroyed from 17 May, the roads taken by the men from June and by the women and children from August, and the places of the killings named in this chapter. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\n**Spring 1921.** The Central Army began operating around Bafra in April 1921. The destroyer *Overton* relayed a missionary's report that about 100 Greek villages south of Bafra had been destroyed from 17 May, a campaign \"directed by high authority—probably Angora\" (Morris & Ze'evi, pp. 405, 407). The Christian men of Alaçam over twelve years old were imprisoned in May and killed (a British record, via Hofmann 2011, p. 74).\n\n**The men, June–August.** Bafra was surrounded on the morning of Saturday 5/18 June 1921 (Morris & Ze'evi's \"June 5\" is the Julian date), and after the decree of 16 June three convoys of 500–600 men each left Bafra on 17, 21 and 24 June (Yerasimos). The fullest account of what followed is that of the Bafra notable Ioannis Dilmitzoglou, written in Turkish in Greek letters and signed in Athens on 13 October 1921, after his escape by sea to Midye in Thrace; the Black Book printed it in translation. He writes that about 535 men were tied back to back and killed in the church at Suludere near Elezli, the seven priests with an axe at the door; that the second convoy, about 500 men, was burned in the church of Selamelik; that the third, 400 recruits and 280 exiles, was taken to the church of \"Kodje-sou\", and \"we know neither details nor the manner of their destruction\"; and that a fourth convoy of 35 reached Elbistan near Maraş. Morris & Ze'evi give the same figures, citing the same text from the Toynbee Papers (p. 408), so the church burnings rest on one Greek witness. Villages were burned with their people: 250 at Muğamlı, by the same witness, and 300 at Sürmeli (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 409).\n\nOn the road to Samsun the escort shot 330 men of the first convoy and 660 of the third near Kavak (Black Book; a deportee's letter confirms the 660). Wilford Fuller of the Near East Relief saw only 200 of the first 900 reach Sivas, and Stanley Hopkins of the Near East Relief reported 550 killed at Kavak on 26 June and 1,300 on 15 August. On 8 July the destroyer *Brooks* reported that in the Bafra area \"as many as 90 percent of deported Greeks have been killed\" (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 409); written before any women were deported, it concerns the men.\n\n**The women and children, August–December.** On 3 August the mutasarrıf of Bafra told the *Overton* that the deportation \"of all remaining Greeks, including women and children, had been ordered by Angora\". On 8 August the women and girls were collected, raped, and many forced to convert (Morris & Ze'evi, pp. 407–408). US destroyers then reported about 6,000 women and children deported from Bafra around 31 August and 2,500 more on 19 September (p. 408; Shenk 2017, pp. 200–201). Fotiadis describes two convoys in detail. The first left on 16 September with 4,120 people, among them 150 orphans of the American orphanage, and walked for 70 days by Hacıhamza, Osmancık, Çorum, Mecitözü, Turhal, Tokat, Sivas, Tecer Han, Deliktaş, Kangal and Hekimhan to Malatya; 2,700 adults and 130 orphans died on the way, the Americans took in 20 orphans at Sivas, and 1,270 reached Malatya, where most died of typhus. The second, \"the eighth convoy\", left on 24 September with 1,900 people; between Deliktaş and Kangal 310 froze and 790 died, and 800 reached Hekimhan, where about 400 were left, the rest going on to Malatya and Harput, where nearly all died. Fotiadis does not name his source for these convoys, and they may be the same people as those counted by the Americans, but American sources confirm a convoy of this size on this road. Americans met \"a column of 4,000 Bafran women and children, near Sivas\", shoeless and without food (*McFarland*, October 1921; Morris & Ze'evi, p. 409); a traveller reported a convoy of about 4,000 from Samsun to Tokat (British minutes, 9 November 1921; Hofmann, p. 77); and at Sivas the relief worker Theda Phelps met a Bafra woman who had made her children walk the snowy paths because \"the children who were being carried were all freezing to death\" (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 421). The women of Alaçam went another way: 1,600 were driven by Boyabat towards Osmancık, and most died on the road (Black Book notes, 25 September).\n\n**Malatya, Harput, Diyarbakır.** Ward put 4,000 deportees at Malatya that winter, half of whom died. This is where the Bafra women's convoys ended. In January 1922 women coming down from the mountains under an amnesty were deported, and in February the Bafra hinterland was swept; a refugees' telegram speaks of \"five sacks full of heads\", and of Bafra Circassians hiding about 140 Greek neighbours (Morris & Ze'evi, pp. 409–410, 416).\n\n**Where the survivors went.** The roads of the interior pointed south, and Kévorkian suspects a planned final expulsion into the French and British mandates. Expelled from Harput in March 1922, Yowell and Ward reported to the US consul at Aleppo, Jackson, and missionaries arriving at Aleppo from Harput told him of \"at least 75,000 Greeks\" deported, of whom only \"20,000\" had survived (Morris & Ze'evi, pp. 415, 425). The Bafra tobacco merchant Antonios Gavriilidis escaped from Malatya in late August 1922 with two Armenians and two brothers named Lazaridis (not related to the author of this atlas), reached Aleppo with Kurdish muleteers, and went by train to Beirut, by French ship to Constantinople, and on to Athens, where he arrived on 1 November 1922. The Near East Relief moved the surviving children. On 10 October 1922 Lester Wright, its director at Malatya, wrote from Aleppo that he had taken 214 children over the mountains in seven days to the railhead at Jarabulus, in French territory: \"We have permission to move out Greek children ... About 700 of them at Malatia and 100 at Harpoot. We had many more at Malatia last fall but thousands died during the winter ... they were all refugees that were driven from their homes a year ago\" (Krikorian & Taylor 2011, pp. 403–404). They were very probably children of the coastal convoys of 1921. Wright was killed on 27 October 1922 escorting 200 children, half of them Greek, from Harput to Aleppo. Greece took over 16,000 orphans from the Near East Relief in 1923, many of them housed on Syros.\n\nIn January 1923 the destroyer *Barry* reported 50,000 Armenian refugees in Aleppo \"along with 12,000 Greeks\" (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 465); this is the figure on the Library of Congress photograph \"Where 12,000 Greek refugees were fed by Americans\". By August 1923 most of the Greeks had been \"shipped off to Greece\", leaving \"the poorest and weakest\" begging (Near East Relief, Beirut, 28 August 1923). Other figures are 17,000 Greeks in all the Syrian cities (Yıldırım, via a journalist) and 8,000 at Aleppo (Near East Relief Museum). None of these sources says how many were Pontic; most were probably from Cilicia and central Anatolia, and some came overland from the interior after the expulsion order of October 1922, robbed on the way (pp. 458, 461). The routes to Greece we know of were by rail from Aleppo to Beirut and then by ship, as Gavriilidis went in 1922 and as Kopasis, a deportee from Konya held at Diyarbakır, did in 1924–25; from Malatya or Harput by Jarabulus to Aleppo, for the orphans; and by the Black Sea ports for everyone else. In March 1923 there were 9,000 refugees being fed at Samsun and 10,000 waiting for ships, with 8,000 at Alexandretta and 3,500 at Mersin (pp. 462–463). Topalidis, after Fotiadis, gives 10,460 Christians of the Samsun area shipped out in November 1922, and about 52,000 left the Samsun region under the exchange. No lists of ships from Beirut to Greece have been found.\n\n**The Turkish-speaking Orthodox of Bafra.** The Orthodox of Bafra town spoke Turkish: the consular census of 1911 marks them so, the list of the Centre for Asia Minor Studies says that \"the native Greeks ... only spoke Turkish\", and Dilmitzoglou wrote his account in Karamanlidika, Turkish in Greek letters. No source treats them differently from the Greek-speakers. Their men were killed, their women and children deported, and some forced to convert. Speaking Turkish did not protect them.\n\n| Bafra, 1921 | Figure | Who gives it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Men's convoys, June | three of 500–600 | Yerasimos |\n| Killed at Elezli, Selamelik and Kocasu | 535, 500, 680 | Dilmitzoglou (one witness), repeated by Morris & Ze'evi |\n| Killed near Kavak | 330 and 660; 550 (26 June) and 1,300 (15 Aug); at least 900 | Black Book; Hopkins; the Greek delegate to the League |\n| Men deported who died | \"as many as 90 percent\" | USS *Brooks*, 8 July 1921 |\n| Women and children deported | about 6,000 (31 Aug); 2,500 (19 Sept) | US Navy |\n| Two convoys described by Fotiadis | 4,120, of whom 1,270 arrived; 1,900, of whom 800 arrived | Fotiadis (no source named) |\n| Totals for Bafra | 14,010 deported, at least 9,140 dead | Gavriilidis 1924, via Topalidis, who gives 83%; 9,140 of 14,010 is 65% |\n\n## The figures\n\n**Before the war.** The Greek count of 1910–12, made by the Patriarchate and the consulates, found 400,586 Greeks in the six metropolises and 298,183 in the vilayet of Trebizond with Canik; the Ottoman census of 1914, which undercounts Christians, gives 260,313 for the same area (Karpat). For that area the range is therefore 260,313 to 298,183. Larger figures were claims: 353,533 for Trebizond and Canik (Soteriades 1918), 700,000 for the Pontus in a Greek memorandum of February 1919, and 850,000 in March 1920, including 250,000 exiles in Russia and the Caucasus, while Western diplomats put the Greeks of the Pontus at about 450,000 (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 382).\n\n**The losses: who says what.**\n\n| Source | Figure | What it counts |\n|---|---|---|\n| Refet's report, 1917 | 13,406 | Greeks moved inland from Samsun, 1916–17 |\n| Ottoman table, 1914–17 (Çilingir, p. 53, no source) | 62,000 Canik; 29 Trabzon | Greeks deported |\n| Talat, late 1917 (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 392) | over 93,000 inland and 164,000 to Greece | all Ottoman Greeks deported in the war |\n| Germanos, 1918 (Hofmann, p. 102) | of 160,000 deported, a tenth survived | the Pontus, 1916–18 (a Greek claim) |\n| The Emmanouilidis motion, 1918 | 550,000 \"killed and annihilated\" | all the coastal Greeks, 1914–18 (a claim) |\n| The Patriarchate's Black Book, 1919 | 257,019 | deported in 1914–18, six metropolises |\n| Central Army records | 63,844 (58,974 in the breakdown) | Greeks moved inland by November 1921 |\n| Rendel, 28 December 1921 | \"well over 35,000\" deported, \"the balance ... massacred\" | the Pontus, 1921 |\n| US missionaries, via the consul at Aleppo, March 1922 | at least 75,000 deported, 20,000 survived | Pontic deportees, 1921–22 |\n| Yowell and Ward (Shenk, p. 201) | at least 14,000 | Greek women, children and old men dead, to their knowledge, on the Harput route |\n| Samsun Independence Tribunal (Aybars) | 622 death sentences, 137 in absentia | August–December 1921 |\n| Fethi, 10 June 1922 | 5,051 dead (13,175 \"neutralised\") | the Pontus operations to 15 May 1922 |\n| Turkish military records (Balcıoğlu) | 11,198 killed, 10,886 surrendered | the Pontus operations to February 1923 |\n| Central Council of Pontus, Black Book, 1922 | 303,238 | \"exterminated population\", defined as those dispossessed, exiled, violated, dead of cold and hunger, murdered and massacred |\n| Valavanis 1925, p. 24 (via Shirinian 2017) | 353,000 | the Black Book's 303,000 plus 50,000 added by Pontic refugee intellectuals for 1922–24 (Agtzidis 2005, p. 201) |\n| Yerasimos 1988 (via Ayşe Hür, in Çilingir, p. 228) | 65,000–70,000 | Greek losses, 1916–1923 |\n| Greek census of 1928 | 182,169 (and 47,091 from the Caucasus, 11,435 from Russia) | survivors settled in Greece |\n| Jones 2010; Peterson 2004; Tsirkinidis; Shirinian 2017 | 300,000–360,000; 360,000; 353,000; about 350,000 | the Pontus, 1914–23 (from the Greek figures) |\n| Rummel 1998 | 347,000; 264,000 for 1919–22 | all Anatolian Greeks |\n| McCarthy (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 487) | about 300,000 | Anatolian Greeks, 1912–22 |\n| League of Nations, 1926 | about half of 2,000,000 | the Greeks of Asia Minor, 1914–24 |\n| Hofmann 2011, p. 105 | about 1.5 million | all Ottoman Greeks, 1912–22 |\n| Morris & Ze'evi, pp. 467, 487 | \"several hundred thousand\" | all Ottoman Greeks |\n| Sjöberg 2016 | 300,000–700,000 | all Ottoman Greeks |\n\n**Why the figures differ.** They count different things. The Ottoman counts and the Patriarchate's book of 1919 count people moved; the Turkish counts of 1922–23 count only those killed in operations, not those who died on the marches. The Black Book of 1922 repeats the deportation figures of 1919 for four of the six metropolises and defines \"exterminated\" to include the dispossessed and the exiled. Valavanis adds a round 50,000. The census of 1928 counts only the survivors who reached Greece. And the totals cover different areas and periods. The one estimate for the Pontus by a scholar that is not built on the compilations of 1919–25, Yerasimos' 65,000–70,000 for 1916–23, reaches us at third hand.\n\nWhat can be said is this. Pontic Greek deaths in 1914–1923 are put at 300,000–360,000 in totals derived from the Greek compilations, which scholars repeat, though those compilations count the exiled and the dispossessed among the \"exterminated\". One reconstruction, by Yerasimos, gives 65,000–70,000 for 1916–23. The Ankara records themselves admit 11,198 killed in operations and some 60,000 moved inland in 1921, and the relief workers' figures for a single route exceed 14,000 dead. The figures are best read side by side, not averaged.\n\n**By metropolis.** The Black Book of 1922 gives these figures, set beside the Patriarchate's of 1919:\n\n| Metropolis | Communities | Churches | Schools | Deported 1914–18 (1919) | \"Exterminated population\" (1922) | Difference |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Amaseia | 400 | 603 | 518 | 89,370 | 134,078 | +44,708 |\n| Chaldia | 145 | 182 | 152 | 64,582 | 64,582 | 0 |\n| Trebizond | 70 | 127 | 84 | 38,434 | 38,434 | 0 |\n| Neocaesarea | 95 | 135 | 106 | 27,216 | 27,216 | 0 |\n| Kolonia | 64 | 74 | 55 | 19,938 | 21,448 | +1,510 |\n| Rhodopolis | 41 | 53 | 45 | 17,479 | 17,479 | 0 |\n| **Total** | **815** | **1,174 (printed 1,134)** | **960** | **257,019** | **303,237 (printed 303,238)** | **+46,218** |\n\n## The word genocide\n\nThe International Association of Genocide Scholars (2007) calls the destruction of the Pontic Greeks a genocide, as do Hofmann, Bjørnlund, Meichanetsidis, Jones, Gaunt, Schaller and Zimmerer, Totten and Bartrop, and Hovannisian. Morris & Ze'evi describe a \"thirty-year genocide\" of the Christians of Anatolia, from 1894 to 1924. Akçam's view has moved. In 2012 he found the treatment of the Greeks different from that of the Armenians \"in scope, intent, and motivation\" (p. 123), and wrote that during the war \"the policy toward the Ottoman Greeks was limited to relocation from the coastal areas into the interior out of military necessity\", while \"the genocidal massacres of the Pontic Greeks\" came later, in the republican era (p. xvii). In 2023 he wrote that \"the Pontus genocide of 1921/1922 and the population exchange in 1924 were the last chapters in a process of ethnic cleansing—genocide, in fact—against the Ottoman Christians\" (p. 15), and that the deportations of 1916–17 were followed by \"a similar, more thoroughgoing series of deportations and massacres of the Pontic Greeks\" in 1921–22 (p. 10). The Turkish researchers Korucu and Dağlıoğlu and the writers Çetinoğlu and Çilingir also use the word.\n\nOthers differ or qualify it. Barth, Schwartz and Varnava prefer \"ethnic cleansing\", and Kritikakos lists Bruce Clark, Çağlar Keyder, Mark Mazower and Sean McMeekin as holding that view (p. 60, after Travis). In Sweden the historian Ingmar Karlsson called the parliament's inclusion of the Pontic Greeks \"inaccurate historiography\" (Kritikakos, p. 44). In Greece the SYRIZA politician Nikos Filis said that the persecution of the Pontic Greeks was not a genocide \"in the scientific sense\", and met a strong backlash (Kritikakos, p. 128, dates this to 2015); in 2001 three researchers of the Centre for Asia Minor Studies, St. Anestidis, M. Kouroupou and I. Petropoulou, wrote that the wartime persecutions and massacres, \"culminating in the case of the Pontians, do not legitimise the use of the term genocide at the scientific level\" (*Αυγή*, 18 February 2001, quoted in Agtzidis 2010, p. 276). Midlarsky revised his view (2005, 2014). Sjöberg (2016) studies the claim as a matter of memory and notes that activists inflate the totals, and McCarthy concedes about 300,000 Anatolian Greek deaths within the general mortality of the war. Several scholars separate 1916–17 from 1921–22, when Ankara's orders, its ministers' statements in secret session and its officials' reported words bear more directly on intent.\n\n## The sources\n\nThe Austrian and German consular and embassy reports are the strongest independent evidence for 1916–17, coming from the Ottomans' allies; they are known through the editions of Fotiadis and Enepekidis and their translations, and the sender and date of the German telegram of July 1916 are still unsettled. The American naval, Near East Relief and missionary reports, known through Morris & Ze'evi and Shenk, are eyewitness accounts of 1921–22, often at odds with Admiral Bristol's line. The British records (Rendel, the High Commission's Armenian-Greek Section, Rumbold) show the range of reports; the Armenian-Greek Section drew much on Greek and Armenian representatives. The French reports of Lépissier, studied by Georganopoulos, are detailed for Trebizond in 1919–20, though one French colleague thought them over-dramatic. The Catholic clergy and Vatican diplomats published by Kyriakidis (2026) were independent of the Greek and Turkish states and wrote in fear of interception; they are strong for Trebizond in 1918–23 and for the Pope's interventions, and the texts appended to his book are safer than his narrative, which sometimes blends them with Fotiadis. Among the Greek compilations, Brown & Ion (1918) print the Greek Legation's and consulates' reports of 1915–17 in translation, with their archive numbers, and the Legation's village lists for the Kerasous and Samsun districts, which are entered on the village pages of this atlas; the compilations are valuable for detail, but their figures are claims, and the Black Book's \"exterminated\" includes the exiled. The Ottoman and Turkish state documents (orders, Assembly minutes, Central Army records, tribunal dockets, the 1922 *Pontus Meselesi*) are essential as admissions; many reach us through Turkish historians (Biber, Balcıoğlu, Aybars) or through the writers who argue for recognition, whose value lies in the documents they transcribe. Turkish official historiography (Çapa, *Pontus Meselesi*, Aydın and Duman, Biber) is the only detailed source on the violence of the bands. Morris & Ze'evi (2019), Akçam (2012, 2023), Hofmann (2011), Shirinian (2017, 2019), Sjöberg, Rummel and Kritikakos (2026) are indispensable for the debate, the figures and the recognitions. For Bafra, Dilmitzoglou is first-hand but a single, partisan witness who slips on dates, good for places and sequence and uncertain on numbers; Fotiadis' convoy figures have no named source, though one convoy is confirmed by the Americans; Gavriilidis' memoir of 1924 reaches us in excerpts; Kévorkian repeats the Black Book; and Krikorian & Taylor (2011) are a scholarly guide to the Near East Relief documents. The Exodus volumes of the Centre for Asia Minor Studies and Barton (1930) have not been consulted, and Ward's pamphlet is known to us only from a summary on greek-genocide.net.\n\n## What the Turkish sources say\n\nThese claims rest on Turkish sources alone; each is given with what other sources say. They show what the Turkish state and Turkish historiography say. The Ottoman and Ankara documents are used in the chapter above wherever they record what the state did.\n\n**The Turkish state's position.** Turkey does not recognise a Pontic or Greek genocide. Its foreign ministry's statements each 19 May (for example No. 167 of 2022 and No. 95 of 2026) call the \"Pontus\" allegations baseless, say that Greece promotes them to cover its defeat and its own army's atrocities, and cite the Allied commission reports, Article 59 of the Treaty of Lausanne and the massacre at Tripolitsa in 1821. Turkey objects to 19 May as a day of remembrance, the day of Kemal's landing at Samsun, and condemned the Greek law of 1998 and the New South Wales motion of 2013. Turkish historiography speaks of the *Pontus meselesi*, the Pontus question, and of a wartime relocation (*sevk*, *tehcir*). *Against:* the orders are Ottoman and Ankara documents; the Ottomans' own allies reported the burnings and deaths of 1916–17; US naval and relief officers reported the marches; Turkish deputies attacked Nureddin; and the interior minister reported thousands of Greeks killed in 1922.\n\n**The claims about 1916–1923.**\n1. **The security case for 1916–17.** Deportation as a measure against deserters and collaboration with Russia. *Against:* the German consulate called desertion and espionage \"a pretext to annihilate the entire people\"; German diplomats learned that the \"ban\" on Samsun's Greeks under the pretext of pursuing bands was \"in reality\" a persecution on a large scale (Hofmann, p. 60); Talat spoke in January 1916 of getting rid of the Greeks as of the Armenians; and the Russian-held zone, where collaboration was likeliest, was not deported.\n2. **The bands as a separatist movement.** *Partly confirmed:* Pontic leaders sought a state in 1919–20, and Lépissier named the movement's leaders. *Against:* Morris & Ze'evi's \"almost no acts of overt rebellion\" and Greeks \"unmoved by ethnic-nationalist appeals\" (p. 383); Western observers \"uniformly\" denied a rebellion in 1921 (p. 406).\n3. **Band raids around Çarşamba, 1916–18.** The general fact of occasional attacks is accepted by Morris & Ze'evi (p. 383), and Russian arms in general terms are mentioned by a Greek historian (Agtzidis 1992, p. 167) (H7-063). The details (road blocks, raids on convoys and grain stores, Russian support from Trebizond, and the raids of Vasil Usta as Ayşe Hür tells them) rest on Turkish sources alone.\n4. **Kemal's \"forty guerrilla bands\".** Morris & Ze'evi report the claim, after Shaw (p. 384), and judge that \"the reality was different\" (p. 383). Kemal's own report of 22 May 1919 counted thirteen Turkish bands as well.\n5. **Ankara's case for 1921–22.** A feared Greek landing, the bands, the shellings, Pontic Greeks in the Greek army, disarmament, and an Interior Ministry letter of 25 June 1921 calling the measure a matter of security, not *tehcir*. *Against:* the shelling of İnebolu caused no casualties and came weeks after the campaign began (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 407); a convoy was on the Kavak road by 2 June; no landing was attempted; women and children were deported against the Interior Ministry's own order; and the deputy governor of Samsun proposed emptying all the Greek villages, while the interior minister reported the aim of keeping the Greeks below five percent of the Muslims.\n6. **The dead of Kavak and Bafra.** Yusuf Kemal said on 15 September 1921 that the Kavak dead fell in battle after Greek bands attacked; an Ottoman report of 2 June 1921 blamed \"brigands\" for an attack on a convoy of 766; and for Bafra Ankara cited fighting with the Nebyan bands. *Against:* the Kavak dead were deportees under escort (Fuller, Hopkins, Rendel, a deportee's letter); a Turkish deputy and Turkish documents themselves blame Nureddin's irregulars and the militias of Topal Osman and Şaki Ali; and at Bafra, though the church burnings rest on one Greek witness, the *Brooks* reported on 8 July 1921 that \"as many as 90 percent\" of the deported men had been killed, US officers reported deportations \"regularly accompanied by mass murder\", and thousands of women and children were deported.\n7. **Merzifon a \"legend\".** Yusuf Kemal's denial (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 413), later replaced by \"the troops simply got from under control\" and blame on brigands. *Against:* the American missionaries' accounts, and 950–1,500 dead.\n8. **The Amasya trials.** The lawful suppression of a separatist conspiracy; Hamid Bey is said to have told Rumbold that the condemned had collaborated with Greek forces, though the statement is not at the place usually cited (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, pp. 20–21). *Partly confirmed:* Bristol judged that the condemned had taken part in separatist propaganda. *Against:* 622 death sentences in four months; teachers, doctors, pharmacists and a dead bishop among the hanged; the tribunal's archives still closed; and the Allied protests.\n9. **Band attacks on Muslims, 1919–23, and Ankara's figures.** Deputies' claims of sixty Muslim villages burned in three months; Fethi's 1,303 Muslims killed to December 1921, and later figures; *Pontus Meselesi*'s 1,641 killed and 3,723 dwellings burned; the General Staff's 1,817 Turks killed in 1916–23; bands of 6,000–7,000, later 25,000 men; and over 10,000 rifles from Russia and Britain. *Partly confirmed:* one band raid (*McFarland*, September 1921), Rawlinson's estimate of the armed Greeks, and, in general terms, Russian arms and post-armistice clashes with \"hundreds of victims\" (Agtzidis 1992, p. 167; 2005, p. 184). *Against:* no non-Turkish casualty figure; the same Assembly debate blamed a Turkish battalion for burning villages; and by Ankara's own figures Greek deaths in the operations (5,051 in four months of 1922; 11,198 in all) far exceeded the Muslim deaths it claimed.\n10. **The official case in print: *Pontus Meselesi* (1922).** Its theses, an ancient Turkish homeland, Greek societies and the Merzifon college behind a revolt, and an orderly relocation, underlie the official view to this day, according to Çetinoğlu and Hür. The book nevertheless prints Bristol's protest against the deportation of women and children.\n11. **Yusuf Kemal's circular of 16 July 1921**, sent to the foreign representatives and the Apostolic Delegate: a Greek plot, armed risings provoked by the \"Pontos\" society, massacres by the bands, a Greek call-up of Ottoman Greeks, and responsibility for any reprisals laid on the Greeks.\n12. **Kemal's reply to the Pope, March 1921:** the Christians under Ankara were calm; Greeks and Armenians committed crimes.\n13. **Nureddin's defence:** \"the Greeks in our country are a snake; the poison of these snakes is the women\", and hence the women and children were deported with the men (Balcıoğlu 2000, via Çilingir, p. 130). He cited an old man hanged near Bafra for signalling to a Greek warship. His admissions are in the chapter's cards (B1-022): women and children deported with the men \"without separating them from their children\", and 1,000 armed local Turks enrolled at Bafra and 600 at Kavak.\n14. **Bafra in Ankara's words.** Yusuf Kemal said that Greek bands had burned Koçhisar and the Sakızcı quarter of Bafra (Biber, p. 441), that the deportation had passed with \"one or two cases of theft\" (p. 442), and that women and children were deported because of \"spies\" and military operations (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 415); Fethi told the Assembly in May 1922 that no Christian had been \"illegally punished\". Biber treats Mount Nebyan as a rebel base and the destroyed villages as suppliers of the bands, and calls the Black Book's figures propaganda. *Against:* the Black Book does rest on one witness for Bafra, but US naval and Near East Relief reports independently confirm the Kavak killings, the deportation of thousands of Bafra women and children and mass deaths on the road; and Ankara's own deputies removed Nureddin.\n\n**Turkish writers who argue for recognition.** Tamer Çilingir, in *Pontos Gerçeği 1914–1923* (2016), holds that the Unionist and Kemalist persecutions were one planned genocide that official history splits in two (pp. 6–7), adopts the figure of 353,000 dead, treats the deportations of 1916 as a Turkish-German plan (pp. 54–57), and documents the Central Army's measures from Turkish archives (pp. 112–130). Sait Çetinoğlu, in three articles and in *The Pontos Question*, with Dara Cibran (2007), argues for a continuity of method from the Unionists to the Kemalists, calls the Independence Tribunals a sham, describes the bands as self-defence groups rather than a rebellion, and gives 11,188 dead by the official count. Korucu & Dağlıoğlu (2019) map the Turkish archives (Assembly minutes, tribunal dockets, military records) and supply most of the figures from Turkish documents used above; Ayşe Hür, reprinted by Çilingir, supplies Yerasimos' 65,000–70,000 and the General Staff's 1,817.\n"};
